WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.444
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.286 --> 00:00:14.160
BROWN: The reason we've
got people here today

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.350
is that this is part of
a series of, I guess,

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.919
group oral histories,
where we're

00:00:17.919 --> 00:00:20.460
trying to get people together
who were involved in the Apollo

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.140
guidance project and
just get you to tell us

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.070
a few of your stories.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.500
Just tell us a little bit
about your experiences,

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.000
what you really
like about working

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.915
on this particular project.

00:00:29.915 --> 00:00:33.300
This is part of a larger, a
much, much larger project,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.660
which breaks down into five
units of which we are one.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.642
We're studying the
Apollo guidance computer.

00:00:38.642 --> 00:00:40.350
There are also projects
that are studying

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.400
the history of materials science
on the rise of bioinformatics,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.660
on the dispute of the
physics about the process

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.770
of renormalization, and
there is one other one--

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.620
MINDELL: Material
science, renormalization.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:56.700
BROWN: Bioinformatics.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.128
MINDELL: Bioinformatics,
molecular evolution.

00:00:58.128 --> 00:00:59.365
BROWN: Molecular evolution.

00:00:59.365 --> 00:01:01.740
MINDELL: Pretty broad coverage
in the history of science,

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.502
but we're really the
only engineering one.

00:01:04.502 --> 00:01:06.210
BROWN: And this is
part of a project that

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.500
has a couple of items.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.570
The first one is
really to figure out

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.760
how to do research into
very large projects

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.310
in recent history of
science and technology.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.419
Historians tend to find that
the further back they go,

00:01:17.419 --> 00:01:19.085
the easier it is to
manage your sources.

00:01:19.085 --> 00:01:21.272
There are a lot less
things to deal with.

00:01:21.272 --> 00:01:23.230
Once you start to deal
with projects in science

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.261
and engineering post
the Second World War,

00:01:25.261 --> 00:01:26.760
it starts to become
a problem of how

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.230
to manage the huge
amount of information

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:32.180
that you have access to and
huge amount of information that

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:33.910
might bear on your
particular project.

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:36.180
So one of the things that we're
doing as part of this exercise

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.304
is really an investigation.

00:01:37.304 --> 00:01:39.420
How do we tell the history
of such a large scale

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.960
project, as something like
the Apollo guidance computer?

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.340
And another thrust
of the project

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.590
is really to figure out what
use the world wide web is

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.600
going to be in helping us
with these kinds of things.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.920
And as you can see, and
I know that some of you

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.680
have seen this, we
have put together

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.010
a website, which is an
ongoing project, which

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.410
is gathering together the
information that we gather,

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.420
and the results of these
kinds of conferences,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.711
to make this available both
to us through our research,

00:02:02.711 --> 00:02:04.950
and to other people in
the historical community

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:08.820
to do their research, and to
people in the wider community.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.830
You'd be surprised how
much interest there

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.330
is in the wider community.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.120
Since we put this
web site up, we

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.080
would get emails,
some random emails,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.870
from members of the
public on a regular basis,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.020
either asking us
about the website,

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.040
telling us about information
that's out there,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.030
asking questions about the
information that's on there.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.446
There was a guy
who was attempting

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:27.960
to rebuild one of
these machines,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.650
and he's had some
communication with us.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.280
There's a lot of interest there.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.071
HALL: Lots of luck.

00:02:33.071 --> 00:02:34.810
BATES: Was he the
guy in Venezuela?

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:35.640
BROWN: No, this is
a different one.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.014
SPEAKER: This is
a different guy.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.710
There's one trying to
program the simulator--

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:42.430
BROWN: Yeah.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:43.950
SPEAKER: --on the PC too.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.257
Did he contact here yet?

00:02:46.257 --> 00:02:48.090
BROWN: He sent me a
very brief email saying,

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:49.339
this is what I'm trying to do.

00:02:49.339 --> 00:02:51.820
But he hasn't send
any results yet.

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:52.440
We shall see.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.480
HANLEY: [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:54.980
BROWN: Yeah.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.640
There's a guy who
actually, apparently,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.890
judging from the email that
he sent me, actually wants

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.234
to rebuild with the hardware.

00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:01.400
He wants to go from scratch.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.660
HANLEY: You can never do that.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:05.810
SPEAKER: Don't tell him.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.560
SPEAKER: We don't even
know how we did it.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.990
HANLEY: A good example is when
Eldon was the Trident program,

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.390
and he had to get diodes.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:20.500
And he went to Fairchild
and he wanted diodes

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.980
with given specs for
the Trident program.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:26.000
And they couldn't do it.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.480
They said they couldn't do it.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.480
And then he pulled
out of his pocket

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.010
a whole bunch of
Fairchild diodes

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:36.490
from the Apollo program that
met all of the specifications.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:41.560
These people just don't
know how to do it anymore.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.247
I'll keep quiet.

00:03:44.247 --> 00:03:45.830
SPEAKER: We're
recording some of this.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:47.394
SPEAKER: We are.

00:03:47.394 --> 00:03:50.610
BROWN: I thought I'd jsut very
briefly run through the website

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:54.640
and explain one or two things
that we are doing here.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.490
We've already run a
couple of conferences,

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.220
and I know there are
people here today

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.800
who should have been at
the first conference.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.710
It's surprisingly difficult
sometimes to track them down.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.640
So as we hold these conferences,
we put these up on the web.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:14.290
So there are lots of
familiar faces there.

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:31.685
Then we make the transcriptions
of those conferences available.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:45.130
We also are gathering
documents and scanning these

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:46.540
and putting these on the web.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:49.510
This is, I guess, perhaps
a sort of complement

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:51.010
to the oral histories,
the accounts,

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.040
where you can actually
get your hands on--

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:53.373
SPEAKER: What kind of documents?

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:00.106
HALL: There's a whole
bunch of them on the web.

00:05:00.106 --> 00:05:01.480
BROWN: I can give
you an example.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:08.525
HALL: I assume NASA still
has a library of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:05:08.525 --> 00:05:10.075
SPEAKER: No, they founded that.

00:05:10.075 --> 00:05:12.270
They did have a library.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.520
MINDELL: NASA is--
yeah, actually.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.650
I was down at NASA last
week, and actually, they're

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.560
very happy and very
supportive of this project.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:20.280
SPEAKER: Down in Houston?

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.446
MINDELL: No, not in Houston.

00:05:21.446 --> 00:05:23.610
In Washington at the
[? first ?] place.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.660
All the good stuff's
in Houston and all

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:29.590
of the bureaucratic
stuff's in Washington.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.980
It turns out we have to get
NASA to let Draper have us,

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:35.770
to tell Draper they let us
have access to a lot of paper

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:37.135
archives as well.

00:05:37.135 --> 00:05:39.260
Even though NASA
doesn't think so.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:40.420
But that's another story.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:43.960
POUNDSTONE: Does Draper still
got all the drawings archived?

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.750
HALL: We're trying to
find the drawings at home.

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:48.981
I don't know.

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:52.380
It seems like they should
be there someplace.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:56.230
Nobody there knows, even
they run the Apollo program.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:58.230
So they don't know
where the drawings are.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.188
BATES: Well, it's the
same problem in Raytheon.

00:06:00.188 --> 00:06:02.740
I tried to go in and
find archives in Raytheon

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:07.200
and they're worried about
tomorrow, not yesterday.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.690
POUNDSTONE: But I do know the
aperture cards were saved.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:12.350
HALL: Well, they were, but
where are those aperture cards?

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:14.400
POUNDSTONE: They
were shipped to NASA.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.229
HALL: You think so?

00:06:15.229 --> 00:06:15.895
POUNDSTONE: Yes.

00:06:15.895 --> 00:06:17.572
It was a contract requirement.

00:06:17.572 --> 00:06:18.780
HALL: Well, I would think so.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:22.180
Then NASA must have
them someplace.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:25.690
But I had a copy that I sent
to the American Computer Museum

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:27.160
up in Bozeman, Montana.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:30.820
And I can get those
back, if necessary.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:32.885
It's better to send
those aperture cards.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.050
POUNDSTONE: For
some strange reason,

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:40.420
back when I was
working, I got picked

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:43.000
to go to a conference
in Washington that

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:48.930
held by the Smithsonian,
a similar organization.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:50.800
I forget what it
was, but it had to do

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.920
with archiving and maintaining
the history of all kinds

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:58.600
of various special projects.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.430
And they had
representatives from Boeing

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:03.520
and Grumman and Lockheed,
and everybody in the country

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:04.960
was there.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:11.210
And I learned about some of
the details of how they do it

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:14.030
and all the problems of storing
documents, and all that.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:17.570
But the one you spoke
of is a common problem

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:21.290
in the whole aerospace industry.

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:24.230
Although there are historians
and archives who would just

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.060
love to get a hold
of this stuff,

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:29.342
the companies could care less.

00:07:29.342 --> 00:07:30.800
There's a few
companies that do it.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:33.290
Boeing had done some on theirs.

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:36.020
But most companies,
they're, you know.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:37.999
And when I went
back to my company

00:07:37.999 --> 00:07:39.415
and tried to report
that there was

00:07:39.415 --> 00:07:43.992
a lot of interest in the air,
I got the same old, who cares?

00:07:43.992 --> 00:07:45.450
MINDELL: Most
history of technology

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.810
ends up getting written about
government and universities.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:48.980
POUNDSTONE: Well, you
can't make any money

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:51.271
doing it is the problem, and
we're money making people.

00:07:51.271 --> 00:07:53.320
MINDELL: The companies
are classically--

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:54.180
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.280
POUNDSTONE: Is that Cline?

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.470
SPEAKER: That's Cline.

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:57.230
POUNDSTONE: Jack Poundstone.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:57.680
FRASIER: Hi, Jack.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:58.440
Long time, no see.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.100
POUNDSTONE: Good to see you.

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:00.850
BATES: Dave Bates.

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:01.467
How are you?

00:08:01.467 --> 00:08:02.300
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:03.820
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:08:09.395 --> 00:08:10.520
SPEAKER: He's not here yet.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.979
SPEAKER: [INAUDIBLE]
is coming, I know.

00:08:15.979 --> 00:08:16.770
SPEAKER: Oh, he is?

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:20.300
SPEAKER: Yeah, because
I generally [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.507
SPEAKER: Bart said
he was coming?

00:08:22.507 --> 00:08:23.090
SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:24.289
SPEAKER: OK, good.

00:08:24.289 --> 00:08:26.330
BATES: I ran into the same
thing as he just said.

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:27.954
I mean, they're
worried about tomorrow.

00:08:27.954 --> 00:08:30.370
They're not worrying
about yesterday, OK.

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:32.120
And I couldn't
find any pictures,

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.370
or anything else like that.

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:37.370
POUNDSTONE: Well, I know in
our company, Raytheon, is now--

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:43.220
there is a retiree who's working
up in Lexington who is actively

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.470
trying to develop a--

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:47.270
I guess it's more of a museum.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:48.491
BATES: A museum, yeah.

00:08:48.491 --> 00:08:50.490
POUNDSTONE: Of hardware
and pictures and things.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:52.370
He's been on the phone
with me several times,

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:56.310
wanting me to produce stuff like
this, which I never got around

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:59.222
to until I got ready to
come to this meeting.

00:08:59.222 --> 00:09:01.430
But I don't know if he ever
got a hold of you or not.

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:03.060
I gave him your name.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:04.940
BATES: He's only
open on Thursdays.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:05.648
POUNDSTONE: Yeah.

00:09:05.648 --> 00:09:07.430
Well, it's a retiree
who's not being paid.

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:09.100
All the company ever
did is giving him

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:11.475
a little space in the building
they didn't know what else

00:09:11.475 --> 00:09:12.320
to do.

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:13.640
SPEAKER: Who is that?

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.201
POUNDSTONE: I forget his name.

00:09:15.201 --> 00:09:15.700
Smith?

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.120
BATES: Yeah, Smith, I think.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:20.137
POUNDSTONE: Some guy
from [INAUDIBLE]..

00:09:20.137 --> 00:09:21.470
BATES: [INAUDIBLE] in Lexington.

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:23.803
POUNDSTONE: But anyway, I
think that's typical of what--

00:09:23.803 --> 00:09:26.540
the only reason it's happening
is because the retiree is

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:27.140
interested.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:30.290
The company could care less.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:32.679
MINDELL: More often,
companies actively--

00:09:32.679 --> 00:09:34.470
I mean, they worry a
lot about liabilities.

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.049
And they destroy as they can.

00:09:36.049 --> 00:09:38.340
POUNDSTONE: Well then, of
course, the government policy

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.430
say you're supposed
to do that anyway.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.950
You almost have to cheat to
keep an old piece of hardware.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:46.000
Of course, that never
bothered Draper.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.391
I notice you guys used
to do it all the time.

00:09:49.391 --> 00:09:51.200
FRASIER: Actually,
the guys at Delco

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.200
managed to find all
the pieces, I think,

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.530
for a complete system
for one of the reunions

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.240
they had about three years ago.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.240
So at their reunion
in Milwaukee they

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.570
had laid out on the table
all the major pieces--

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:07.598
I think they had
a computer, too.

00:10:07.598 --> 00:10:10.070
POUNDSTONE: I remember
one time, somebody

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:14.050
went to one of these
junkyards and found a PIPA.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:16.870
And they picked it
up for $1 or two

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:19.230
and gave it to the
gentleman and me.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:22.500
BATES: It was right over
here in the back area.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:24.625
He used to buy it by the pound.

00:10:24.625 --> 00:10:26.750
SPEAKER: Do you remember
what the name of that was.

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:30.610
BLAIR-SMITH: If you talk
about old-time packaging,

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:34.104
do you remember Dimitri Grabee?

00:10:34.104 --> 00:10:34.920
SPEAKER: Who?

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.950
BLAIR-SMITH: Dimitri Grabee.

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:38.210
SPEAKER: Grabee, yeah.

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:40.690
BLAIR-SMITH: He was
the one that holds

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:42.990
the patent on the
multi-layer board technology.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:44.180
SPEAKER: Yeah, we
made the [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:44.790
out of their boards.

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:46.335
POUNDSTONE: Who did he work for?

00:10:46.335 --> 00:10:47.150
BLAIR-SMITH: What?

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:48.830
POUNDSTONE: Who did he work for?

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:52.390
BLAIR-SMITH: Photocircuits,
I think, at the time.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:55.400
Anyway, the only reason
why I mention that is he

00:10:55.400 --> 00:11:01.100
has a fantastic museum, of all
types of [? patented ?] stuff.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:04.869
Anything that he ever met with.

00:11:04.869 --> 00:11:06.410
HALL: And one of
these, he walked out

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:08.545
of the lab with the
logic tray at block one.

00:11:08.545 --> 00:11:10.420
Now, how he got out of
the lab, I don't know.

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:11.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.732
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:13.732 --> 00:11:15.440
POUNDSTONE: Like I
said, Draper was never

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:17.260
too particular about the rules.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:19.804
HALL: I would have gladly walked
out with it if I could have.

00:11:21.174 --> 00:11:21.840
BROWN: So as I--

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:22.390
HALL: He did.

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:22.520
OK.

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:23.464
BROWN: Oh, I'm sorry.

00:11:23.464 --> 00:11:25.940
So as I said, we're
collecting documents.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:27.170
We're collecting photos.

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:29.240
So I guess I'll put out
a plea at this point.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:31.330
If you guys have documents
or photos or anything

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:34.392
in your basement
or any memorabilia

00:11:34.392 --> 00:11:36.350
that you might have, we
would love to see this.

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.090
We'd like to scan it,
put it on the web,

00:11:38.090 --> 00:11:40.282
make it available for posterity.

00:11:40.282 --> 00:11:42.620
POUNDSTONE: Did you
ever get a computer?

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:46.040
BROWN: No, we did not manage
to get a computer yet.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:47.850
POUNDSTONE: [INAUDIBLE]
the Smithsonian

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:49.095
has a bunch of them.

00:11:49.502 --> 00:11:51.710
SPEAKER: We shipped five
down out of the Smithsonian,

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:52.750
as I remember.

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:55.362
They've got one on display.

00:11:55.362 --> 00:11:56.570
They used to have it working.

00:11:56.570 --> 00:11:59.390
And we had a contract
to service it.

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:00.674
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:12:00.674 --> 00:12:03.090
SPEAKER: I think they actually
have a computer on display,

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:04.167
don't they?

00:12:04.167 --> 00:12:05.000
BATES: That's right.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:06.645
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:08.395
SPEAKER: If you went down
and asked, you might get one.

00:12:08.395 --> 00:12:09.936
SPEAKER: [INAUDIBLE]
a long time ago.

00:12:09.936 --> 00:12:12.650
They had a block two, you
know, the gold anodized one.

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:13.320
[INAUDIBLE]

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:15.278
SPEAKER: It's not there
anymore, I don't think.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:16.324
SPEAKER: Oh, really?

00:12:16.324 --> 00:12:18.740
BATES: Did you ask him about
looking into their warehouse?

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:20.531
Because that's where
they shipped them all.

00:12:20.531 --> 00:12:24.350
SPEAKER: Well, they also sent
one of them back up here.

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:25.719
MIT Museum has one.

00:12:25.719 --> 00:12:27.010
SPEAKER: Well, is complete now?

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:29.160
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:30.570
SPEAKER: Just the memory tray?

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:31.653
SPEAKER: Just memory tray.

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:37.430
HALL: Well, did you check that?

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:40.346
INTERVIEWER: What?

00:12:40.346 --> 00:12:42.245
HALL: The [INAUDIBLE]
Bell, out there

00:12:42.245 --> 00:12:44.090
in Moffett Field, that museum.

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:47.090
BROWN: No, [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:50.655
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:12:50.655 --> 00:12:51.530
HALL: That's amazing.

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:52.655
Where's that one then?

00:12:54.035 --> 00:12:56.294
SPEAKER: Well, I had one once.

00:12:56.294 --> 00:12:58.630
I don't where it-- but
[INAUDIBLE] probably

00:12:58.630 --> 00:12:59.450
[INAUDIBLE].

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:03.550
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:05.210
BATES: We had a display case.

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:09.050
Jack wanted the display case
with all the stuff that we had.

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:10.500
We have brought people in.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.120
You could show him what we had.

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.110
And he had all the stuff
from the standpoint

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:17.000
of the integrated
circuits and the diodes

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.250
and everything else,
both raw and plastic

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:21.445
that we got from the
lines that we had there.

00:13:21.445 --> 00:13:24.100
But we also had an
Apollo computer.

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:27.260
And what happened
with that Zagrodnik

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:30.830
should know because he was the
last program manager, I think.

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:37.776
POUNDSTONE: No, it was
right in front of my office.

00:13:37.776 --> 00:13:38.275
BATES: Yeah.

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:41.540
POUNDSTONE: Perhaps
it's still there.

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:42.640
I don't know.

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:43.240
BATES: No.

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:44.780
I've been in there lately, yeah.

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:47.810
It's not there.

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:48.680
Let me run it down.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.160
Just give me the assigment,
and I'll go ahead and do it.

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:52.370
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:53.880
SPEAKER: Let's just finish
up with our [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:54.890
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:56.220
POUNDSTONE: Excuse us, Alex.

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:57.890
We talk a lot.

00:13:57.890 --> 00:13:59.900
BROWN: So as I said, we're
collecting documents.

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:02.690
There are other features of
the website, which include

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:04.550
comment forums, in particular.

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:06.890
So once we've had
this conference,

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:10.310
we would like to invite you
guys to go to the website,

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:13.040
log on, and just pass comment,
if you have any comments

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:14.880
to make, about the transcripts.

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:16.670
And this is part
of the exercise is

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.967
to encourage this
online interaction so we

00:14:18.967 --> 00:14:21.050
can get a little bit of
debate and a little bit if

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:21.200
[INAUDIBLE].

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:22.949
HALL: On that point,
it's not clear to me,

00:14:22.949 --> 00:14:25.550
even, how do you make comments?

00:14:25.550 --> 00:14:28.700
Even though I've been
supposedly on the inside.

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:30.630
BROWN: Yeah, this is
kind of embarrassing.

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:31.700
This is not an
aspect of the project

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:33.450
that we have a great
deal of control over.

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:35.840
I'm assured by the people
who manage the larger

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:38.570
website that they are putting
time and effort into making

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:41.325
this more user friendly.

00:14:41.325 --> 00:14:43.325
HALL: Because I can put
in quite a few comments.

00:14:43.825 --> 00:14:46.310
POUNDSTONE: I assume at the
moment, you could email him.

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:48.742
BROWN: You could
certainly email me.

00:14:48.742 --> 00:14:50.700
As you can see, the
transcripts are already up.

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:52.910
And so if you would like to read
those and email me comments,

00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:54.530
I would be more than
happy to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:56.821
POUNDSTONE: Well, I notice
there's quite a few question

00:14:56.821 --> 00:14:58.460
marks in places
where you couldn't

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:01.010
understand what they said.

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:02.604
Somebody can fill those in.

00:15:02.604 --> 00:15:04.520
MINDELL: The website
requires that you log in.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:06.290
So if we have all
your email addresses,

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:09.600
you just log in with your email
address, make up a password.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:11.930
And then when you
log in, it allows

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:14.190
you to comment in their
discussion forums.

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:18.440
And a lot of the idea is
that of the projects that

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:22.160
are on the site, the other
scientists people are studying,

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:24.320
we're fortunate that we
have the only one that's

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:28.110
really inherently localized
to Cambridge, Massachusetts.

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:31.210
And so we figured we'll
start by getting everybody,

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:33.530
as many people as we
can, who are around here,

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:35.930
in a room talking and
then hopefully make

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:40.040
a clean segue to either you
guys or other people who are not

00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:42.470
able to come to Cambridge,
who can then participate

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:43.950
in the conversations as well.

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:46.556
So in a way, everything
we do here today

00:15:46.556 --> 00:15:47.930
and what we've
already been doing

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:53.150
is intended as a starter for
a national or global comment

00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:55.010
network.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:58.980
And this is part of the
research part of this program.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:01.220
So we don't know how well
it's going to work yet.

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:03.920
And we have the
fortunate, also, of having

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:06.680
all engineers pretty much
were involved in our project.

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:09.710
So we figure if these guys
can't make the thing work,

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:11.712
then we've got to go
back to the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:11.712 --> 00:16:13.380
BLONDIN: You're kind of on the
tail end of the distribution

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:13.879
group.

00:16:13.879 --> 00:16:15.245
It's dropping rather rapidly.

00:16:15.245 --> 00:16:16.769
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:16.769 --> 00:16:18.185
BLONDIN: If you
don't do it quick,

00:16:18.185 --> 00:16:19.760
there'll be nothing left.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.010
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:25.610
MINDELL: So Sandy will show
you some of the documents.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:28.320
and hopefully by later today
we'll have the web working.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:32.360
We can do a demo of the
actual comment site.

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:36.380
And as usual with
programmers, it's

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:38.930
a little more sophisticated
than it needs to be.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:41.990
And we're spending a lot
of time beating on them,

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:44.570
trying to tell them.

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:46.470
If the engineer with
40 years of experience

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:48.350
can't make the thing
work, then what

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:50.480
are the chances that a
material scientist is going

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:53.322
make it work or a biologist?

00:16:53.322 --> 00:16:55.280
HALL: Or even an expert
on the Apollo computer.

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:56.156
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:56.156 --> 00:16:57.470
MINDELL: Well, exactly.

00:16:57.970 --> 00:16:59.810
BLONDIN: Is this a
scientific endeavor

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:01.880
or a historical endeavor?

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:04.380
Are you people
historians or scientists?

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:05.630
MINDELL: Well, that's a good--

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:08.800
I'm actually both a control
engineer and an historian.

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:12.109
And so some of the
background for this project

00:17:12.109 --> 00:17:16.339
comes from I'm just publishing
a book on the history of control

00:17:16.339 --> 00:17:20.286
engineering from
1915 to 1948, which

00:17:20.286 --> 00:17:23.599
is all sort of before
classical control theory really

00:17:23.599 --> 00:17:26.569
kind of jelled and has a
lot of the early Draper

00:17:26.569 --> 00:17:30.460
stuff during the Second World
War, with the Mark 14 gun site

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:33.620
and a lot of computing in a
whole bunch of different ways.

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:37.220
And basically it's turned out
to be mostly antiaircraft fire

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:39.789
control and early radar
during the Second World War.

00:17:39.789 --> 00:17:41.705
And that's been a seven-
or eight-year project

00:17:41.705 --> 00:17:43.130
that I've been working on.

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:46.180
And coming to the close of that
project a couple of years ago,

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:47.810
we sort of decided what's next.

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:49.550
And I'd always thought
that the right way

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:51.950
to end that book would have
been with Apollo because it

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:55.689
was a very logical, consistent
set of progressions there.

00:17:55.689 --> 00:17:56.980
Cline's actually read the book.

00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:58.910
FRASIER: I was going to say,
I highly recommend the book

00:17:58.910 --> 00:17:59.660
when it comes out.

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:01.830
I've thoroughly enjoyed it.

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:03.650
They gave it to me
to read because I

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:06.650
worked on antiaircraft
fire control systems

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:09.270
that he's had in the book.

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:12.110
MINDELL: And if I had gone
all the way through Apollo,

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:14.340
it would have been 1,000
pages instead of 500 pages,

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:16.170
and nobody at all
would have read it.

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:18.310
And so what we're
doing here is sort of--

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:20.900
we're going to skip a little bit
in the middle, unfortunately.

00:18:20.952 --> 00:18:22.561
POUNDSTONE: Your
book is all analog.

00:18:22.561 --> 00:18:23.810
MINDELL: It's actually about--

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:26.000
it's about the role
that control engineering

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:28.640
played in the emergence
of digital computing

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:30.680
and about how people
began to think

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:32.910
about the relationship
between analog and digital,

00:18:32.910 --> 00:18:35.300
in the context of design
and control systems.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:38.610
FRASIER: A lot of history
about Doc and Webb,

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:40.680
and the interaction
is Sperry, too,

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:42.360
that I found very interesting.

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:47.170
I read Webb's book, the
biography of Webb just before.

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:50.250
And how it all fit
together was quite--

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:52.212
MINDELL: And a big part
of it is manufacturing.

00:18:52.212 --> 00:18:53.670
And one of my
arguments in the book

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:55.890
is you can't really
understand this history

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:58.710
until you look at the
manufacturing issues that

00:18:58.710 --> 00:18:59.670
came up.

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:01.330
Because during the
Second World War,

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:02.970
a big reason that
people started moving

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:05.550
toward electronic
analog computing

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:07.840
had less to do with anything
like accuracy or speed.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:10.740
It had everything to do with
the problems of manufacturing

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:14.400
mechanical computers and at
that time, the relative ease

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:18.330
of having Western Electric build
the computers instead of people

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:19.560
like Chrysler.

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:24.280
So we've extended that basic
approach into this project.

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:28.350
And we're saying,
the Apollo computer

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:33.450
is generally under appreciated
by historians of computing

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:36.100
because all they tend to
care about is architecture.

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:39.820
And they sort of work out of
this very abstracted framework.

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:42.259
And we're looking
at it saying, we

00:19:42.259 --> 00:19:43.800
think the Apollo
computer's important

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.920
for several different reasons.

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:49.470
Obviously the integrated
circuits aspect of it

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:53.070
is important, the general
manufacturing aspects,

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:56.340
the human-machine interface
control systems, et cetera, et

00:19:56.340 --> 00:19:56.920
cetera.

00:19:56.920 --> 00:20:03.420
And so in some way, this
is the climactic chapter

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:07.470
of what's sort of a century-long
development of feedback

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:09.660
control computing
human-machine interface

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:12.840
and advanced manufacturing
techniques for controlling

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:14.500
vehicles of various types.

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:16.755
HALL: And have you seen the
latest Scientific American

00:20:16.755 --> 00:20:18.550
and their article
about the development,

00:20:18.550 --> 00:20:21.330
or the history of the
development of PCs?

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:22.880
MINDELL: Uh-uh.

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:27.340
HALL: It's interesting,
except they've completely

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:31.664
ignored just what you said
about the Apollo computer.

00:20:31.664 --> 00:20:33.205
I would think it
was one of the first

00:20:33.205 --> 00:20:35.790
that really had
human interaction

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:39.004
with a visual computer, and
they don't even mention it.

00:20:39.004 --> 00:20:40.920
But there's other
interesting things in there.

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:42.780
It goes way back to Whirlwind.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:45.790
MINDELL: I will check that out.

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:48.100
It is-- I mean, we're trying
to change the categories

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:50.100
so that people think
about history [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:50.599 --> 00:20:52.670
POUNDSTONE: From a
manufacturing point of view,

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:57.370
to talk about the
evolution of Apollo,

00:20:57.370 --> 00:20:59.150
you really have to
start with Polaris.

00:20:59.150 --> 00:21:00.029
SPEAKER: Exactly.

00:21:00.029 --> 00:21:01.070
Yeah, that's why I wish--

00:21:02.735 --> 00:21:04.945
POUNDSTONE: It was
the genesis of--

00:21:04.945 --> 00:21:08.140
not only from a
manufacturing technology,

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:09.500
but from the people.

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:10.990
Those guys all
worked on Polaris.

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:12.948
MINDELL: And one thing
we're very interested in

00:21:12.948 --> 00:21:18.310
is the people side of it,
actually, because there, again,

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:19.600
people are far too easy--

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:20.892
POUNDSTONE: Stay out of that.

00:21:20.892 --> 00:21:22.600
MINDELL: Well, we went
into politics too.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:26.470
People tend to be far
too simple about looking

00:21:26.470 --> 00:21:27.780
at influences of technology.

00:21:27.780 --> 00:21:29.602
They say here's a computer.

00:21:29.602 --> 00:21:30.560
What was the influence?

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:32.230
And they go, what
other computers

00:21:32.230 --> 00:21:34.360
did people design
with that computer?

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:36.670
As opposed to saying,
here's a project.

00:21:36.670 --> 00:21:38.005
People built this project.

00:21:38.005 --> 00:21:39.880
What did those people
learn from the project?

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:42.280
And how did that carry on
in the rest of their careers

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:45.820
or in starting a company
or later projects they did?

00:21:45.820 --> 00:21:47.530
This is something
that actually-- when

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:49.084
I was at NASA last
week, they're very

00:21:49.084 --> 00:21:51.250
interested about this project
because they're always

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:54.670
being pinged on to
document their influence.

00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:58.130
And they have this really
kind of dumb idea of spin-off.

00:21:58.130 --> 00:22:00.400
Like, the astronauts
drink Tang, and then

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:03.277
everybody on the
ground drinks Tang.

00:22:03.277 --> 00:22:05.110
And there are some of
those, but they're not

00:22:05.110 --> 00:22:07.810
nearly as significant
as the companies who

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:09.430
got involved in Apollo.

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:10.810
They did things in a new way.

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:11.740
People learned things.

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:12.446
They move on.

00:22:12.446 --> 00:22:13.945
And that's a lot
harder to document.

00:22:13.945 --> 00:22:15.950
But it's a lot more
meaningful because you got

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:17.116
to follow the people around.

00:22:17.116 --> 00:22:19.240
And sometimes it's
within the same company.

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:21.230
Sometimes they move
on to other companies.

00:22:21.230 --> 00:22:25.600
And so they were very interested
in that part of this project.

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:29.650
And as we go around, we'll ask
you to introduce yourselves

00:22:29.650 --> 00:22:33.310
and say something about both how
you got to the Apollo project,

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:36.340
but also where your
career went afterward.

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:38.920
And we may, then,
interview individually

00:22:38.920 --> 00:22:41.260
in more detail about
this because we're

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:45.460
very interested in exactly
those kinds of developments.

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:48.402
BATES: Well, they can't fire
us or put us in jail now.

00:22:48.402 --> 00:22:50.680
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:51.545
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:22:51.545 --> 00:22:53.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:55.780 --> 00:22:57.750
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:01.780
MINDELL: So the site
is up on the web.

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:04.021
How many of you have
seen the site already?

00:23:04.021 --> 00:23:05.020
So a good number of you.

00:23:05.020 --> 00:23:07.120
And we'll send you the address.

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:09.300
And it's about to
be updated again.

00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:12.040
And what we'll do from
today-- as you can see,

00:23:12.040 --> 00:23:13.240
we're recording it--

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:15.040
we're going to
make a transcript.

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:18.370
And we edit the transcript to
make it a little more readable

00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:22.150
just because conversation
tends to be very verbose.

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:23.500
And we compress it a bit.

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:26.290
And then we'll send it to
you by email or however

00:23:26.290 --> 00:23:28.672
where you like so that before
anything goes on the web

00:23:28.672 --> 00:23:30.130
that you've said,
you have a chance

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:34.040
to review it, correct to it,
add to it, if you choose.

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:35.710
And then we recompile
it and put it

00:23:35.710 --> 00:23:37.660
on the web in this
conversational format.

00:23:37.660 --> 00:23:41.254
So sometimes people
remember things

00:23:41.254 --> 00:23:43.170
that they didn't remember
when they were here.

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:44.750
And they go look something up.

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:46.774
And so what you do
see on the web--

00:23:46.774 --> 00:23:48.190
everything that
you see on the web

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:49.564
from our previous
conferences has

00:23:49.564 --> 00:23:52.956
been signed off on basically
by the participants.

00:23:52.956 --> 00:23:54.580
HALL: I didn't get
a chance to do mine.

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:55.020
And I need--

00:23:55.020 --> 00:23:56.520
MINDELL: Oh, you
haven't done yours?

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:57.275
Okay.

00:23:57.275 --> 00:23:58.620
HALL: I wasn't [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:58.620 --> 00:23:59.350
I wasn't here--

00:23:59.350 --> 00:24:00.141
MINDELL: Oh, right.

00:24:00.141 --> 00:24:01.780
HALL: --when it was available.

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:03.287
So I need to be able to do that.

00:24:03.287 --> 00:24:03.870
MINDELL: Okay.

00:24:03.870 --> 00:24:07.060
We should talk to [INAUDIBLE]
about that, who I thought

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:08.710
I was going to be here today.

00:24:08.710 --> 00:24:10.900
So what I think we'd
like to do to start,

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:12.760
and it may take up a
lot of the morning,

00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:16.870
is just have people go around
and tell your own version

00:24:16.870 --> 00:24:19.370
of the story, or at
least say who you are,

00:24:19.370 --> 00:24:21.170
what your background
was, how you got here.

00:24:21.170 --> 00:24:24.019
We'll get into some of the
more detailed stuff later--

00:24:24.019 --> 00:24:25.310
where you worked, for how long.

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:28.940
We have Raytheon people here
and [? IL ?] people here.

00:24:28.940 --> 00:24:33.700
And so at least everybody
here gets a sense of, if not

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:35.320
a refresher for
who else is there.

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:41.530
And then when we
complete that, we'll

00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:43.000
get into some
specific questions.

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:45.400
But usually that process
itself generates a lot

00:24:45.400 --> 00:24:48.160
of interesting conversation.

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:54.600
And I'll be taking pictures
just for conversation,

00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:56.225
the web-based part
of the conversations

00:24:56.225 --> 00:24:59.860
so get a sense of
what we're doing.

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:03.850
BATES: You got a time
limit for each interview?

00:25:03.850 --> 00:25:05.860
Because we could stand
here and sit forever.

00:25:05.860 --> 00:25:06.630
MINDELL: Right.

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:10.840
For the first go around,
five minutes would be great.

00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:12.530
And then we can--

00:25:12.530 --> 00:25:14.900
that'll certainly
generate questions for us.

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:18.250
And I think we'll start to hear
patterns of [INAUDIBLE] things.

00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:22.390
And that'll enable us
to get around the room

00:25:22.390 --> 00:25:24.974
and get a sense for who's
here and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:29.420 --> 00:25:35.510
We can start here or wherever.

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:37.340
Maybe Clyde, do
you want to start?

00:25:37.340 --> 00:25:38.360
FRASIER: Sure.

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:40.450
I'm Cline Frasier.

00:25:40.450 --> 00:25:46.220
I got to the Apollo program by
the Army and Sandia Corporation

00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:50.050
and then to NASA in Houston.

00:25:50.050 --> 00:25:56.330
Got involved with the guidance
system work in about 1963.

00:25:56.330 --> 00:26:01.010
And my role into that
was I was the instigator

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:04.900
of the change of the spacecraft
guidance and control system

00:26:04.900 --> 00:26:08.230
configuration from
basically an analog system

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:11.180
on both the command
service module to using

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:18.520
digital autopilot, and incurring
Eldon's wrath because we moved

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:20.930
from two guidance
computers to one guidance

00:26:20.930 --> 00:26:22.910
computer in the process.

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:26.430
And I talked for quite a
while in the last interview.

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:29.860
So why don't I just
let it go with that.

00:26:29.860 --> 00:26:31.940
Well, to add that,
then from there

00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:35.042
I went on and ended up
as the program manager

00:26:35.042 --> 00:26:37.250
for the guidance and control
system, primary guidance

00:26:37.250 --> 00:26:38.960
and control system.

00:26:38.960 --> 00:26:42.383
As a result, I had a lot to
do with MIT and with Raytheon

00:26:42.383 --> 00:26:45.810
and all the other people.

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:49.310
TURNER: I'm Bard Turner.

00:26:49.310 --> 00:26:53.090
And I kind of go way
back to the Polaris days.

00:26:53.090 --> 00:26:56.646
I worked for Raytheon
Chapel Street in Newton,

00:26:56.646 --> 00:27:00.590
who were, at that time,
making tubes, welding.

00:27:00.590 --> 00:27:04.970
And this was back
when the lab had

00:27:04.970 --> 00:27:09.890
started repackaging the Polaris
guidance computer, which

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:15.530
was all discrete, into what
was called cord wood modules

00:27:15.530 --> 00:27:19.880
and welding rather
than soldering them.

00:27:19.880 --> 00:27:23.660
And so that was
sort of my entry.

00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:30.250
I was sent by
Raytheon as a resident

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:34.970
and working for Ed Duggan
doing design work on this thing

00:27:34.970 --> 00:27:36.767
because I had applied for it.

00:27:36.767 --> 00:27:37.600
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:39.380
TURNER: Polaris, yeah.

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:43.940
In fact, I have-- this really,
as Jack said, goes back.

00:27:43.940 --> 00:27:48.830
This is really the start of
the basic physical organization

00:27:48.830 --> 00:27:49.925
of the Apollo.

00:27:49.925 --> 00:27:53.390
In other words, the concepts
that we learned on Polaris

00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:57.590
really was the one that
tracked the key points up

00:27:57.590 --> 00:27:59.150
through Apollo.

00:27:59.150 --> 00:28:06.740
So when that Polaris program
was done, I did leave Raytheon

00:28:06.740 --> 00:28:10.940
and went to work for
the lab directly, just

00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:12.920
at the start of
the Apollo program.

00:28:12.920 --> 00:28:17.150
And was pretty close to
the design of the logic

00:28:17.150 --> 00:28:20.674
section of the Apollo computer.

00:28:20.674 --> 00:28:24.960
From the first version,
I have two modules here,

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:29.900
which was the Block one
Which still retained

00:28:29.900 --> 00:28:32.060
a welded interconnect,
but it had

00:28:32.060 --> 00:28:34.160
the micrologic
integrated circuit

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:40.490
NOR gates back in the cans,
to the final version, which

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:43.900
was interconnected with the
multi-layer boards, which

00:28:43.900 --> 00:28:49.850
were hard to make, and made by
one place, mainly, in Virginia.

00:28:49.850 --> 00:28:54.650
And significant also,
backline interconnected

00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:57.770
with an automatic
wiring machine made

00:28:57.770 --> 00:29:03.680
by Gardner Denver in
Michigan, and clearly was

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:06.460
involved with the transition
over the Raytheon,

00:29:06.460 --> 00:29:07.800
to the manufacturer.

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:14.150
So I have been
around this program

00:29:14.150 --> 00:29:19.970
all the way back to before
Apollo, which was Polaris.

00:29:19.970 --> 00:29:25.380
So that's where I am.

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:27.060
BATES: Yes, my
name is Dave Bates.

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:29.570
I go before Polaris.

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:32.290
When I got drafted in the
Army and sent to Huntsville,

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:34.375
Alabama, they took
it any guy that

00:29:34.375 --> 00:29:36.750
was a mechanical engineer that
came from Sperry Gyroscope

00:29:36.750 --> 00:29:39.030
must know all about
gyroscopes, which

00:29:39.030 --> 00:29:40.590
show was kind of
difficult, because I

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:42.480
was a structural engineer.

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:45.370
But anyway, I was assigned
to the guidance and control

00:29:45.370 --> 00:29:49.710
laboratory under Dr.
Heusermann, and I

00:29:49.710 --> 00:29:52.110
was in charge of the guidance
and control laboratory

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:53.875
from a military standpoint.

00:29:53.875 --> 00:29:55.750
MINDELL: Could you just
back up a little bit?

00:29:55.750 --> 00:29:56.140
BATES: Sure.

00:29:56.140 --> 00:29:57.973
MINDELL: When were you
at Sperry, and where,

00:29:57.973 --> 00:29:59.100
and what'd you work on?

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:05.208
BATES: I was at Sperry
from about '53 to '55.

00:30:07.710 --> 00:30:10.200
Got drafted in the Army
and sent to Huntsville,

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:13.950
and I was lieutenant
the Army in charge

00:30:13.950 --> 00:30:16.320
of the guidance and control
laboratory, as I said.

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:18.580
We were working on the
Redstone, Jupiter, Pershing.

00:30:18.580 --> 00:30:20.370
And as you probably
well know, Von Braun

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:23.680
was very much interested
in space exploration,

00:30:23.680 --> 00:30:27.850
so we were building the explorer
satellite in the back room.

00:30:27.850 --> 00:30:29.460
So I worked on
that and a program

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:32.970
for Hardtack, which was an
atomic firing of a Redstone

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:34.400
missile.

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:37.660
They would always revert back
to the V-2 type of hardware,

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:41.880
so it was kind of difficult to
work as to what was going on.

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:47.100
So the old Redstone
computers were analog,

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:49.530
and they used ball
and disk integrators,

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:51.990
which held me from the
standpoint of being

00:30:51.990 --> 00:30:53.070
on the mechanical side.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:54.500
MINDELL: Ford Instrument
Company make those?

00:30:54.500 --> 00:30:54.990
BATES: Pardon?

00:30:54.990 --> 00:30:56.365
MINDELL: Was it
Ford who made it?

00:30:56.365 --> 00:30:59.970
BATES: Ford Instrument Company
made the first ones, yes.

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:03.780
In Long Island City.

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:09.720
So then what happened, was
the Navy got interested

00:31:09.720 --> 00:31:12.240
in the Polaris program,
and they decided

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:15.300
that they were going
to use the Jupiter A--

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:17.400
we had the Jupiter A, B, and C.

00:31:17.400 --> 00:31:20.340
The Jupiter B was
the first forerunner

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:23.040
of Polaris, the Mariner
ships, and they were first

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:25.860
talking about taking
a Jupiter missile

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:27.510
and putting it aboard a ship.

00:31:27.510 --> 00:31:30.030
Aboard a submarine, which
is kind of ridiculous,

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:32.520
because they used liquid
oxygen, liquid hydrogen,

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:36.300
and air-bearing gyros, which
made it rather difficult.

00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:38.310
My job through this
whole thing was not

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:41.160
only from the mechanical
engineering standpoint,

00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:45.180
but when the head of the project
office had a heart attack,

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:49.720
I went over and took over the
project office for Von Braun,

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:51.330
and for the people there.

00:31:51.330 --> 00:31:53.730
So I was assigned the job
of working with the Navy

00:31:53.730 --> 00:31:56.250
to try to determine
how the Jupiter

00:31:56.250 --> 00:32:00.829
B would be utilized in the
next generation of Polaris.

00:32:00.829 --> 00:32:02.370
And I worked with
all the people that

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:04.770
came down from the
Instrumentation Laboratory,

00:32:04.770 --> 00:32:08.910
like Davey Hoag and Ralph
Ragan, and the rest of them.

00:32:08.910 --> 00:32:13.200
And the people in the
Navy, like Sam Porter.

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:21.070
So what happened was that
the Navy decided that,

00:32:21.070 --> 00:32:23.070
since the Army was having
a problem with the Air

00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:26.460
Force on a Jupiter versus the
Thor, that they would pull out.

00:32:26.460 --> 00:32:30.720
And so they pulled out of
the Army in Huntsville,

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:32.460
but we kept an awful
lot of the people

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:35.040
down there in Huntsville
to work on the program.

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.440
One of them, Jim Matthews,
who finally came with me

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:41.400
to Raytheon, worked on
the early guidance scheme

00:32:41.400 --> 00:32:43.650
from the standpoint
of the trajectory,

00:32:43.650 --> 00:32:45.010
and what was going to be done.

00:32:45.010 --> 00:32:47.510
And actually, he was the guy
that worked with Dahlgren

00:32:47.510 --> 00:32:50.340
and determined those equations.

00:32:50.340 --> 00:32:54.960
When I got to my end
of time at the Army,

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:58.500
they wanted to stay there,
but my home was in Boston.

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:02.670
So I took a job at GE in
Pittsfield, who had the Polaris

00:33:02.670 --> 00:33:04.980
program, and I was program
manager for the Polaris

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:07.260
guidance system.

00:33:07.260 --> 00:33:11.310
At that time we had the Mark 1
system, working with the Draper

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:15.120
Laboratory, and that was an
instrumentation laboratory

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:17.220
during that time.

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:19.350
My main responsibility
was the coordination

00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:23.470
of all the components from
the standpoint of the first EA

00:33:23.470 --> 00:33:25.980
there was worked
on with the Mark 1,

00:33:25.980 --> 00:33:33.260
and then all of the IMU, which
we built at GE in Pittsfield.

00:33:33.260 --> 00:33:38.940
The next thing was, when Polaris
Mark 2 came, we kept on going.

00:33:38.940 --> 00:33:41.100
The situation became,
it didn't look

00:33:41.100 --> 00:33:44.880
like Polaris was going to
go much further than that.

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:47.040
The guy that hired me
for GE in Pittsfield

00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:48.750
knew I came from Huntsville.

00:33:48.750 --> 00:33:52.170
He came down and
worked for Raytheon.

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:55.920
The problem that Raytheon
had at that time, and also

00:33:55.920 --> 00:33:59.010
the Instrumentation Laboratory,
was a working relationship

00:33:59.010 --> 00:34:01.690
between Nasa Huntsville
and NASA Houston.

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:04.872
I, having been a NASA
Huntsville for a long time,

00:34:04.872 --> 00:34:06.830
and being in charge of
the guidance and control

00:34:06.830 --> 00:34:09.630
laboratory, knew the
people down in Huntsville.

00:34:09.630 --> 00:34:11.969
So I was hired in my
first stint in marketing

00:34:11.969 --> 00:34:15.600
and advanced program
development to work

00:34:15.600 --> 00:34:19.139
at Raytheon, to
look at how Raytheon

00:34:19.139 --> 00:34:22.139
could work with both
Houston and with Huntsville.

00:34:22.139 --> 00:34:25.820
So it got to be more a working
relationship and an interface

00:34:25.820 --> 00:34:27.090
to try and do that.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:29.920
So I work well with customers,
and with the people who

00:34:29.920 --> 00:34:32.810
are building the hardware and
designing the hardware, which

00:34:32.810 --> 00:34:35.000
included the
Instrumentation Laboratory

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:38.150
and the people in Waltham,
and the rest of it.

00:34:38.150 --> 00:34:42.409
I went through a number
of jobs in that area,

00:34:42.409 --> 00:34:45.560
but finally ended up
working for Jack Poundstone

00:34:45.560 --> 00:34:49.429
in the area of advanced
program development.

00:34:49.429 --> 00:34:52.070
I retired about 10
years ago, and now I'm

00:34:52.070 --> 00:34:55.735
a consultant to a
number of companies.

00:34:55.735 --> 00:34:57.370
That's about it.

00:34:57.370 --> 00:34:57.880
That's all.

00:34:57.880 --> 00:34:59.640
And I did it in less
than five minutes.

00:35:05.312 --> 00:35:06.270
POUNDSTONE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:07.519
MINDELL: Yeah, those comments.

00:35:07.519 --> 00:35:09.900
The Sperry gyroscope is
of great interest to me,

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:12.050
because a lot of
the first book is

00:35:12.050 --> 00:35:16.990
about the Sperry gyroscope and
Ford, and the relationship--

00:35:16.990 --> 00:35:19.560
BATES: I'll bring in one
of his first auto-pilots.

00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:22.350
I bought it as
junk at $5 a pound.

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:24.490
MINDELL: I bought
a Mark 14 gunsight.

00:35:24.490 --> 00:35:27.736
A brand new for $100 on eBay.

00:35:27.736 --> 00:35:30.134
It's in my office
downstairs, actually.

00:35:30.134 --> 00:35:32.550
BATES: That's how I learned
about gyros, because you could

00:35:32.550 --> 00:35:34.590
blow into the end of
this thing, and you

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:37.410
could see the way that
the gyro was precessing

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:39.192
when you moved the thing.

00:35:39.192 --> 00:35:40.733
POUNDSTONE: Has
anyone looked on eBay

00:35:40.733 --> 00:35:42.524
to see if there's an
Apollo computer there?

00:35:42.524 --> 00:35:45.050
MINDELL: I have
actually looked there.

00:35:45.050 --> 00:35:49.447
There's a lot of little junk.

00:35:49.447 --> 00:35:51.030
Some of the really
big auction houses,

00:35:51.030 --> 00:35:56.700
like Christie's, have
auctioned hardware,

00:35:56.700 --> 00:36:00.950
but it's all a glove here,
and a mission card there.

00:36:04.530 --> 00:36:07.060
BATES: As a side thing,
they had come down

00:36:07.060 --> 00:36:10.990
to Huntsville at one time, to
the museum that's down there,

00:36:10.990 --> 00:36:14.320
and go up into the third floor
and look at all the hardware.

00:36:14.320 --> 00:36:17.040
And if you've never seen a lot
of junk, there's a lot of junk

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:17.651
up there.

00:36:17.651 --> 00:36:19.650
And you'd go around and
you'd point at each one,

00:36:19.650 --> 00:36:20.970
and what it was.

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:24.490
The AB-5s, the old gyros,
the all accelerometers,

00:36:24.490 --> 00:36:26.780
the old lateral and
range computers.

00:36:26.780 --> 00:36:30.930
What we used-- LEV-3 was the
old platform for the Germans

00:36:30.930 --> 00:36:32.790
on the V-2.

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:35.460
MINDELL: This is on display,
or in their back room?

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:39.240
BATES: In their back room,
but there are some on display,

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:41.500
now that they've figured
out what they are.

00:36:41.500 --> 00:36:44.000
MINDELL: I've been there,
but not for a long time.

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:45.590
BATES: There is a
blockhouse that you

00:36:45.590 --> 00:36:48.810
can go to with the Cape,
which you can get into,

00:36:48.810 --> 00:36:51.690
which has got an old
LED-3 system in it,

00:36:51.690 --> 00:36:54.150
plus a whole bunch of hardware.

00:36:54.150 --> 00:36:56.750
And they use it for VIPs.

00:36:56.750 --> 00:37:00.120
I brought, who was it,
Dennis in there, one time.

00:37:06.940 --> 00:37:08.500
So unless my memory
goes bad, you

00:37:08.500 --> 00:37:10.655
can have me for
historical stuff.

00:37:10.655 --> 00:37:11.530
MINDELL: I'd love to.

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:19.700
POUNDSTONE: All right, my
name is Jack Poundstone.

00:37:19.700 --> 00:37:24.800
I got on the Apollo program
by another devious route.

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:31.260
I was a graduate mining engineer
from West Virginia University.

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:35.510
And after spending about
a year, year and a half

00:37:35.510 --> 00:37:38.630
as a sales engineer
crawling around

00:37:38.630 --> 00:37:41.040
in the coal mines of
Kentucky and West Virginia,

00:37:41.040 --> 00:37:43.610
I decided that
wasn't the thing do.

00:37:43.610 --> 00:37:48.650
I went in the Army, got drafted,
or volunteered for the draft.

00:37:48.650 --> 00:37:52.280
And through a bunch of
strange circumstances,

00:37:52.280 --> 00:37:55.960
I ended up in Huntsville at
the missile guidance school

00:37:55.960 --> 00:37:57.950
down there.

00:37:57.950 --> 00:38:01.520
I ended up being an
instructor teaching

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:07.955
guidance on the Corporal
missile, and also on the Nike--

00:38:07.955 --> 00:38:11.120
I guess it was
Nike Ajax, I think.

00:38:11.120 --> 00:38:14.660
Corporal had an analog
computers doing everything,

00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:17.600
so I sort of cut my teeth
on analog computers,

00:38:17.600 --> 00:38:20.120
in those days.

00:38:20.120 --> 00:38:21.560
When I got out of
the Army I went

00:38:21.560 --> 00:38:24.640
to work for the
Westinghouse in Baltimore,

00:38:24.640 --> 00:38:29.270
and I worked on
the Bomarc project,

00:38:29.270 --> 00:38:36.810
which was a very early missile
program for this country.

00:38:36.810 --> 00:38:43.460
And the Bomarc was one of the
first digital computers being

00:38:43.460 --> 00:38:45.890
used.

00:38:45.890 --> 00:38:50.930
You may recall that IBM had
a big computer in Kingston,

00:38:50.930 --> 00:38:56.270
New York, I guess it was,
SAGE, and they were somehow

00:38:56.270 --> 00:38:59.120
processing all the
radar data that came out

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:01.840
of Eglund Air Force Base.

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:06.600
But my job was
developing simulators,

00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:10.140
because the IBM computer was
so far behind in schedule,

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:11.890
there was no way you'd
eve be able to test

00:39:11.890 --> 00:39:14.960
this missile without something
to simulate those computers.

00:39:14.960 --> 00:39:21.920
So I got a touch of working
in physical computers there.

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:24.260
And subsequently,
after four years

00:39:24.260 --> 00:39:27.620
there, and getting
a high school degree

00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:29.120
in electrical
engineering, I went

00:39:29.120 --> 00:39:31.895
on to a small
company in Huntsville

00:39:31.895 --> 00:39:34.020
for about six months.

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:38.680
And when NASA was formed
and Huntsville almost died,

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:41.120
it seemed like it
was time to leave.

00:39:41.120 --> 00:39:43.860
And I ended up working with
Raytheon up in New England.

00:39:43.860 --> 00:39:47.780
And I got there probably
about a month or two

00:39:47.780 --> 00:39:52.040
after they had gotten their
first Polaris contract.

00:39:52.040 --> 00:39:57.050
So I was probably the only
guy in the Raytheon company,

00:39:57.050 --> 00:40:01.300
at that time, who'd ever heard
of the word digital computer.

00:40:01.300 --> 00:40:04.860
It was a very analog
kind of company.

00:40:04.860 --> 00:40:10.970
So I was sort of in the right
place at the right time.

00:40:10.970 --> 00:40:13.910
Since Raytheon got the contract
to be an industrial support

00:40:13.910 --> 00:40:18.110
contractor to MIT
instrumentation lab,

00:40:18.110 --> 00:40:21.770
I ended up working with
Eldon Hall starting almost

00:40:21.770 --> 00:40:23.750
from the day I got there.

00:40:23.750 --> 00:40:28.430
And I was a project
engineer Raytheon,

00:40:28.430 --> 00:40:31.580
providing support to
the instrumentation lab

00:40:31.580 --> 00:40:33.440
and the development of Polaris.

00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:37.600
And ultimately, we produced the
machine in our manufacturing

00:40:37.600 --> 00:40:40.430
plant, and, as several
people have said,

00:40:40.430 --> 00:40:43.580
the evolution of Apollo
really started from Polaris.

00:40:43.580 --> 00:40:49.280
So when Apollo came along,
after Doc Draper had

00:40:49.280 --> 00:40:52.310
done such a great
job of getting Draper

00:40:52.310 --> 00:40:53.900
the responsibility
for the guidance

00:40:53.900 --> 00:41:00.530
system on a sole source
basis, without a competition,

00:41:00.530 --> 00:41:04.900
they decided they needed some
industrial support contractors.

00:41:04.900 --> 00:41:10.620
There were several aspects
of the program, the IMU,

00:41:10.620 --> 00:41:14.620
the computer, some of
the analog electronics,

00:41:14.620 --> 00:41:17.340
quite a few things.

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:22.610
So we ended up bidding on
it, and thanks to a gentleman

00:41:22.610 --> 00:41:25.690
across the table,
Mr. Hall, Raytheon

00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:29.690
was able to win that
contract, and I then

00:41:29.690 --> 00:41:32.870
became the Raytheon technical
director for our efforts

00:41:32.870 --> 00:41:36.580
to support Draper and Apollo.

00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:41.360
And I spent four years on
that program doing everything

00:41:41.360 --> 00:41:45.110
that had to be done
to make sure that all

00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:48.600
of the resources at Raytheon
were applied to the program

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:52.370
as effectively as they could be.

00:41:52.370 --> 00:41:56.240
I spent four years that--

00:41:56.240 --> 00:41:58.880
I don't even remember my kids.

00:41:58.880 --> 00:42:01.610
We worked so hard.

00:42:01.610 --> 00:42:06.230
And after that, I became
so close to Draper Lab,

00:42:06.230 --> 00:42:08.560
that I became--

00:42:08.560 --> 00:42:11.780
well, they said I didn't
have the right attitude,

00:42:11.780 --> 00:42:13.400
or something like that.

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:20.560
Because NASA had decided
to organize the program.

00:42:20.560 --> 00:42:22.190
After we'd been
gone for four years,

00:42:22.190 --> 00:42:24.260
it was time to get organized.

00:42:24.260 --> 00:42:27.560
So they took Raytheon
and Kollsman,

00:42:27.560 --> 00:42:30.830
who were contractors
for the optics,

00:42:30.830 --> 00:42:34.160
and said they had to be
subcontractors to AC Spark

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:35.480
Plug.

00:42:35.480 --> 00:42:39.770
And that was done by decree.

00:42:39.770 --> 00:42:42.725
So we did that.

00:42:42.725 --> 00:42:45.204
The contractual
arrangements changed,

00:42:45.204 --> 00:42:47.120
but Eldon and I kept
doing the same thing we'd

00:42:47.120 --> 00:42:50.720
been doing for years, which
was to try to get the job done,

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:53.210
and do it in an
efficient manner.

00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:55.760
And after a few
months, AC Spark Plug

00:42:55.760 --> 00:42:58.710
decided that I was much
too close to Draper Labs,

00:42:58.710 --> 00:43:03.470
so they fired me, or they told
me to get off the program.

00:43:03.470 --> 00:43:05.210
Now that was probably
the luckiest thing

00:43:05.210 --> 00:43:08.000
that ever happened to
me, because I got off

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:11.900
just as all the fun
was over, and was

00:43:11.900 --> 00:43:15.345
able to get onto
the Poseiden program

00:43:15.345 --> 00:43:18.410
and the rest of the Navy
programs, when a lot of people

00:43:18.410 --> 00:43:22.410
here got stuck in a program
that was sort of going downhill.

00:43:22.410 --> 00:43:24.380
But anyway, at the
time, I thought

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:27.110
it was earth-shattering.

00:43:27.110 --> 00:43:32.260
But certainly, Apollo was
a wonderful experience.

00:43:32.260 --> 00:43:35.840
I have never seen such a group
of dedicated, hardworking

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:38.660
people that work
between the contractors

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:42.500
and the instrumentation
lab, at that time.

00:43:42.500 --> 00:43:45.170
I don't know if NASA ever
appreciated how hard everybody

00:43:45.170 --> 00:43:47.540
worked, but they certainly did.

00:43:47.540 --> 00:43:51.477
And successes come from it.

00:43:51.477 --> 00:43:53.560
I've got a lot of pictures
here, we can show maybe

00:43:53.560 --> 00:43:55.434
at a later time, but
maybe that's good enough

00:43:55.434 --> 00:43:56.840
for the biographical sketch.

00:44:01.510 --> 00:44:04.770
Mr. AC Spark Plug is now up.

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:06.770
SPEAKER: We'd even forgotten
he worked for them.

00:44:09.499 --> 00:44:10.790
BLONDIN: My name is Ed Blondin.

00:44:13.570 --> 00:44:18.149
I got involved with
Apollo in the early '60s.

00:44:18.149 --> 00:44:19.690
I was superintendent
of manufacturing

00:44:19.690 --> 00:44:23.740
at AC Spark Plug
in Milwaukee, which

00:44:23.740 --> 00:44:29.170
was the defense electronics
division of General Motors.

00:44:29.170 --> 00:44:33.349
It was odd, because we'd
go down to Cape Canaveral,

00:44:33.349 --> 00:44:34.640
they'd say, where are you from?

00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:36.220
We'd say, AC Spark
Plug, and they'd

00:44:36.220 --> 00:44:38.590
say, what are you contributing?

00:44:38.590 --> 00:44:39.844
Spark plugs.

00:44:39.844 --> 00:44:40.832
Big ones.

00:44:46.270 --> 00:44:49.840
I was working on all
the manufacturing

00:44:49.840 --> 00:44:54.580
tests for the Titan program,
and the Apollo program

00:44:54.580 --> 00:44:58.770
at AC Electronics,
where we built the IMU

00:44:58.770 --> 00:45:01.120
and we integrated
the IMU with the new

00:45:01.120 --> 00:45:08.060
with the optics and the
computer, was behind schedule.

00:45:08.060 --> 00:45:11.920
And behind schedule
mostly because we were not

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:15.925
producing welded, encapsulated
modules fast enough.

00:45:15.925 --> 00:45:22.480
So I got transferred over
to that, and about a year

00:45:22.480 --> 00:45:26.420
after that we were
back on schedule,

00:45:26.420 --> 00:45:31.820
and we had tested our
first guidance system.

00:45:31.820 --> 00:45:37.160
We also made the ground
control equipment.

00:45:37.160 --> 00:45:41.440
Tested that, and tested our
first production system,

00:45:41.440 --> 00:45:46.780
and we were shut down because
we didn't have computers.

00:45:46.780 --> 00:45:51.150
So Hugh Brady was our program
manager at AC Electronics,

00:45:51.150 --> 00:45:53.410
we had changed the name by then.

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:56.380
Later on we changed it
again, to Delco Electronics.

00:45:56.380 --> 00:45:59.170
I wasn't there at that time.

00:45:59.170 --> 00:46:02.020
Hugh would ask me
to come here to be

00:46:02.020 --> 00:46:03.955
in residence at
the manufacturing

00:46:03.955 --> 00:46:08.410
arm of Raytheon in Waltham.

00:46:08.410 --> 00:46:10.810
I was delighted to do that,
because I had grown up

00:46:10.810 --> 00:46:13.660
in the area, I went
to Boston College.

00:46:13.660 --> 00:46:20.470
And I came out here and it was
a real culture shock for me.

00:46:20.470 --> 00:46:25.720
I was used to the
General Motors production

00:46:25.720 --> 00:46:34.070
ethic, which was things
moved from manufacturing.

00:46:34.070 --> 00:46:41.220
And the worst curse that
could happen anywhere

00:46:41.220 --> 00:46:45.060
was shut a line down.

00:46:45.060 --> 00:46:47.260
And I got out here,
and I found out

00:46:47.260 --> 00:46:52.790
that this was like getting
something through Congress.

00:46:52.790 --> 00:46:58.205
We had NASA, and we had
MIT, and we had Raytheon,

00:46:58.205 --> 00:47:02.480
and we had my bosses
back in Milwaukee.

00:47:02.480 --> 00:47:07.980
But I got to know some
people in a hurry,

00:47:07.980 --> 00:47:09.770
and I found that was the key.

00:47:09.770 --> 00:47:13.550
Eldon Hall was one of
them, and he goes down

00:47:13.550 --> 00:47:16.960
in my book as one of the
finest gentlemen I've met.

00:47:16.960 --> 00:47:19.760
Cline Frasier was outstanding.

00:47:19.760 --> 00:47:23.600
If you had to buck
something to Cline Frasier,

00:47:23.600 --> 00:47:25.990
he would make a decision.

00:47:25.990 --> 00:47:28.250
That was not something
that was easily done.

00:47:31.010 --> 00:47:32.510
There were an
awful lot of people

00:47:32.510 --> 00:47:35.150
who seemed to feel
that it was their job

00:47:35.150 --> 00:47:39.530
to say what all the
reasons were why

00:47:39.530 --> 00:47:41.090
you shouldn't do so something.

00:47:41.090 --> 00:47:43.580
Lots of them.

00:47:43.580 --> 00:47:48.160
But finally, you had to dig
out what your options were

00:47:48.160 --> 00:47:50.090
and you had to move.

00:47:50.090 --> 00:47:54.500
And Cline Frasier was excellent,
and my boss, Hugh Brady,

00:47:54.500 --> 00:47:57.020
was good.

00:47:57.020 --> 00:48:03.650
And at the time I got involved
with Apollo and Raytheon,

00:48:03.650 --> 00:48:06.590
Jack Poundstone had just
got transferred off,

00:48:06.590 --> 00:48:09.710
and so I didn't get a
chance to meet him much.

00:48:09.710 --> 00:48:12.705
At that time our program manager
was a guy named Bill Kurtz,

00:48:12.705 --> 00:48:15.200
I don't know whatever
happened to him.

00:48:15.200 --> 00:48:16.730
BATES: He's down in Arizona.

00:48:16.730 --> 00:48:19.230
I saw him, he moved down
there from Colorado.

00:48:19.230 --> 00:48:24.440
BLONDIN: He'd come out of
the Navy, was a Navy captain,

00:48:24.440 --> 00:48:27.040
and he worked for another
guy named Gus Guidi.

00:48:32.590 --> 00:48:37.840
Gus was the second person on
whom AC Electronics pulled

00:48:37.840 --> 00:48:38.350
the trigger.

00:48:40.724 --> 00:48:41.890
POUNDSTONE: I was the first.

00:48:41.890 --> 00:48:46.450
Gus told me to leave,
then they told him.

00:48:46.450 --> 00:48:49.390
BLONDIN: Apparently we had
a clause in our contract

00:48:49.390 --> 00:48:56.450
that said that we had approval
of Raytheon program managers.

00:48:56.450 --> 00:49:00.670
And Gus was one
of those guys that

00:49:00.670 --> 00:49:02.140
just loved stirring up trouble.

00:49:02.140 --> 00:49:04.900
He was a brilliant
guy, and everything

00:49:04.900 --> 00:49:13.190
was in a constant
state of argument.

00:49:13.190 --> 00:49:19.570
So it wasn't long before AC
asked Raytheon to remove him,

00:49:19.570 --> 00:49:21.700
and he got out of there.

00:49:21.700 --> 00:49:25.550
And they put another guy
in as head program manager,

00:49:25.550 --> 00:49:29.540
Ron Greenslade,
another real gentleman.

00:49:29.540 --> 00:49:34.550
From this point on, I'm
dealing with Ron Greenslade,

00:49:34.550 --> 00:49:40.510
Hugh Brady, Cline
Frasier, Eldon Hall.

00:49:40.510 --> 00:49:42.950
It didn't matter how
many different hats they

00:49:42.950 --> 00:49:46.250
were wearing, things moved.

00:49:46.250 --> 00:49:53.050
I also found out
how critical it was

00:49:53.050 --> 00:50:01.410
that the hands-on manufacturing
people were properly used.

00:50:01.410 --> 00:50:03.250
It wasn't long
after I got here--

00:50:03.250 --> 00:50:08.710
I got here '66, early '66--

00:50:08.710 --> 00:50:10.950
when Raytheon went on strike.

00:50:10.950 --> 00:50:13.850
And they had that
strike for years,

00:50:13.850 --> 00:50:16.490
and it was a total shock.

00:50:16.490 --> 00:50:22.360
And I remember driving into
the plant with picket signs,

00:50:22.360 --> 00:50:27.228
but we had a sign in our
window that said Apollo.

00:50:27.228 --> 00:50:33.880
And the lines would part,
people said, oh, the Feds,

00:50:33.880 --> 00:50:36.080
and let us in.

00:50:36.080 --> 00:50:41.420
They tried building the
Apollo components at Waltham

00:50:41.420 --> 00:50:48.320
with supervisors, industrial
engineers, foremen,

00:50:48.320 --> 00:50:52.250
people that allegedly
had experience on it,

00:50:52.250 --> 00:50:57.530
especially on these
memories, these ropes.

00:50:57.530 --> 00:51:02.780
Scrap, just everything
they made was scrap.

00:51:02.780 --> 00:51:06.560
I also found out that
welding these modules,

00:51:06.560 --> 00:51:10.420
which, on the
surface, seemed easy--

00:51:10.420 --> 00:51:13.940
you took the jig, put it
between two electrodes,

00:51:13.940 --> 00:51:16.130
came down with a foot
pedal, and the machine

00:51:16.130 --> 00:51:18.595
was programmed to apply the
right amount of pressure

00:51:18.595 --> 00:51:23.180
and the right amount of
current, and you got a weld.

00:51:23.180 --> 00:51:23.770
More scrap.

00:51:26.440 --> 00:51:31.757
There was a technique,
how you positioned it,

00:51:31.757 --> 00:51:40.480
and when your hand shook,
and these female operators

00:51:40.480 --> 00:51:44.980
were good at, and those that
stood around telling them

00:51:44.980 --> 00:51:48.400
what to do were terrible at it.

00:51:48.400 --> 00:51:55.475
I remember, we had to suddenly
rebuild all these memories.

00:51:57.980 --> 00:52:00.969
There was apparently
a vibration problem.

00:52:00.969 --> 00:52:02.510
I didn't know what
it was, but I knew

00:52:02.510 --> 00:52:07.300
that MIT was writing
a new program,

00:52:07.300 --> 00:52:10.790
and we had to get them
out within three weeks,

00:52:10.790 --> 00:52:11.600
I think it was.

00:52:11.600 --> 00:52:13.940
We had to get them to
NASA in three weeks,

00:52:13.940 --> 00:52:16.570
or we were going
to scrub a flight.

00:52:21.065 --> 00:52:25.060
And then, you'd think
this is automated.

00:52:25.060 --> 00:52:30.870
We had x-y tables that
positioned these cores exactly

00:52:30.870 --> 00:52:34.770
between a couple of cores where
you passed a needle through.

00:52:34.770 --> 00:52:37.320
It was like a spinning
wheel with a wire on it.

00:52:37.320 --> 00:52:39.940
You'd think you could just--

00:52:39.940 --> 00:52:45.280
but you couldn't, you
needed those girls--

00:52:47.978 --> 00:52:51.210
little old ladies--
who wound those ropes.

00:52:51.210 --> 00:52:55.500
Anyway, you get
everybody out of the way.

00:52:55.500 --> 00:53:01.040
No cheerleaders, nothing,
put a rope around the area,

00:53:01.040 --> 00:53:05.780
told them what we needed to
do, and we shipped the ropes

00:53:05.780 --> 00:53:09.420
on time, and the
schedule went forward.

00:53:12.540 --> 00:53:14.380
That's the first time
I felt like I was back

00:53:14.380 --> 00:53:16.950
at General Motors
with a production--

00:53:16.950 --> 00:53:19.490
we're going to get this
done, get out of the way.

00:53:22.909 --> 00:53:24.450
BATES: Those little
old ladies mostly

00:53:24.450 --> 00:53:26.160
came from the Waltham
Watch company.

00:53:26.160 --> 00:53:27.660
MINDELL: They did.

00:53:27.660 --> 00:53:30.020
I think Waltham
had closed by then.

00:53:30.020 --> 00:53:30.940
BATES: That's right.

00:53:30.940 --> 00:53:33.535
MINDELL: When did they
close, do you remember?

00:53:33.535 --> 00:53:34.410
We can find that out.

00:53:34.410 --> 00:53:36.701
BATES: I don't know, but they
were the only ones I ever

00:53:36.701 --> 00:53:38.090
saw with that much patience.

00:53:38.090 --> 00:53:40.969
BRISS: And they came out
of the tool division,

00:53:40.969 --> 00:53:42.510
mostly, the ones
that worked with me.

00:53:42.510 --> 00:53:46.560
BATES: Right, they came from
Waltham to the tool division,

00:53:46.560 --> 00:53:48.294
and then from the
tool division--

00:53:48.294 --> 00:53:49.585
BLONDIN: Apollo soon became a--

00:53:49.585 --> 00:53:52.220
HALL: There was a little bit
of tender loving care in that,

00:53:52.220 --> 00:53:52.530
too--

00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:53.238
BATES: There was.

00:53:53.238 --> 00:53:55.420
HALL: --those little
old ladies had.

00:53:55.420 --> 00:53:56.614
They were essential.

00:53:56.614 --> 00:53:57.530
BLONDIN: Yeah, it was.

00:54:00.420 --> 00:54:03.270
Apollo then became a
relatively small part

00:54:03.270 --> 00:54:05.640
of what was going on in Waltham.

00:54:05.640 --> 00:54:11.310
Waltham grew, ultimately,
to over half a million

00:54:11.310 --> 00:54:12.330
square feet.

00:54:12.330 --> 00:54:15.150
We ended up with
3,700 people, and I

00:54:15.150 --> 00:54:25.605
ended up in a vice-president of
Raytheon, and retired in 1989.

00:54:25.605 --> 00:54:29.490
Maybe it would've been better
if I had retired in 1950.

00:54:29.490 --> 00:54:38.610
I wouldn't have had a bypass
in 1990, [? February. ?]

00:54:38.610 --> 00:54:41.620
BLAIR-SMITH: So my name
is Hugh Blair-Smith.

00:54:41.620 --> 00:54:45.190
I am the most software-flavored
person here, I believe.

00:54:45.190 --> 00:54:48.180
So my path to Apollo
begins at Harvard,

00:54:48.180 --> 00:54:51.100
where, in my senior
year, suddenly there

00:54:51.100 --> 00:54:53.550
was a computer course
available to undergraduates,

00:54:53.550 --> 00:54:56.260
and that was because the
Univac 1 had become just

00:54:56.260 --> 00:54:59.940
obsolete enough that Univac was
happy to give one to Harvard.

00:54:59.940 --> 00:55:03.520
So suddenly, undergraduates
could have programming courses.

00:55:03.520 --> 00:55:06.730
So I fell under the influence
of Al Hopkins, and Ray Alonso,

00:55:06.730 --> 00:55:10.409
and Jim Lincoln, and Scotty,
and a whole lot of other people.

00:55:10.409 --> 00:55:11.950
But just to pick
the ones up, that's,

00:55:11.950 --> 00:55:13.324
I think, all the
migrants we had.

00:55:13.324 --> 00:55:14.350
Wasn't it, Eldon?

00:55:14.350 --> 00:55:15.130
Harvard to MIT?

00:55:15.130 --> 00:55:16.463
HALL: That's all I can remember.

00:55:16.463 --> 00:55:19.030
Well, all the important
ones, I'll say.

00:55:19.030 --> 00:55:19.989
You were in that group.

00:55:19.989 --> 00:55:21.946
BLAIR-SMITH: Only I didn't
come with the group,

00:55:21.946 --> 00:55:22.960
nor did they recruit me.

00:55:25.510 --> 00:55:28.270
My path to MIT
was that, as I was

00:55:28.270 --> 00:55:33.710
doing one full-time software
job at Harvard after graduation,

00:55:33.710 --> 00:55:35.680
I got involved
moonlighting, and the fellow

00:55:35.680 --> 00:55:38.540
I was moonlighting with
was named Dan Goldenberg.

00:55:38.540 --> 00:55:42.130
And he ran the computing group
at the instrumentation lab.

00:55:42.130 --> 00:55:45.460
So he recruited me,
because he needed somebody

00:55:45.460 --> 00:55:49.650
who would be able to do
an assembler program,

00:55:49.650 --> 00:55:51.730
or what we now call a
cross-assembler program,

00:55:51.730 --> 00:55:53.290
for an unknown
number of machines

00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:55.480
with unknown characteristics.

00:55:55.480 --> 00:55:59.180
Sounds simple
enough, so why not?

00:55:59.180 --> 00:56:05.260
But I was, without realizing,
prepared quite well for that,

00:56:05.260 --> 00:56:07.150
because what I did
in my spare time

00:56:07.150 --> 00:56:10.420
when I was at college
and my postgraduate year,

00:56:10.420 --> 00:56:12.340
I would go around
to all the showrooms

00:56:12.340 --> 00:56:14.340
that the various
computer companies had,

00:56:14.340 --> 00:56:15.940
and I would beg
programming manuals,

00:56:15.940 --> 00:56:17.500
and they'd all give me one.

00:56:17.500 --> 00:56:25.180
So I believed, at one time,
back maybe up to 1958 sometime,

00:56:25.180 --> 00:56:28.540
that I had some knowledge of how
to program every computer that

00:56:28.540 --> 00:56:29.710
was abroad in the world.

00:56:31.877 --> 00:56:33.835
MINDELL: Last year that
was possible, probably.

00:56:33.835 --> 00:56:37.660
BLAIR-SMITH:
Probably it was, yes.

00:56:37.660 --> 00:56:43.050
So I came into the
computing group,

00:56:43.050 --> 00:56:45.340
and I was told, when I
arrived in September,

00:56:45.340 --> 00:56:48.010
that it was necessary
for this cross-assembler

00:56:48.010 --> 00:56:49.990
to be ready by Christmas,
because the Christmas

00:56:49.990 --> 00:56:53.830
computer would be already
up and running at that time.

00:56:53.830 --> 00:56:56.530
So I said, well, all right,
if it's a Christmas computer,

00:56:56.530 --> 00:56:58.750
we need a short, snappy
name for the system.

00:56:58.750 --> 00:57:00.890
I'll call it the Yul system.

00:57:00.890 --> 00:57:04.150
Y-U-L, nothing to do with
the Montreal airport code.

00:57:04.150 --> 00:57:05.890
But that was what
it was about, it

00:57:05.890 --> 00:57:09.769
was the assembler for
the Christmas computer.

00:57:09.769 --> 00:57:12.310
Well, needless to say, Christmas
was about three months late,

00:57:12.310 --> 00:57:12.980
as I recall.

00:57:12.980 --> 00:57:17.950
We actually got all this
stuff going in March,

00:57:17.950 --> 00:57:21.430
but by that time, the Yul
system, running on the 650,

00:57:21.430 --> 00:57:25.930
cranking out cards, was
indeed able to assemble

00:57:25.930 --> 00:57:29.930
programs for what was not
yet an Apollo machine.

00:57:29.930 --> 00:57:34.450
This was still 1960, this
was what we called the Mod 1.

00:57:34.450 --> 00:57:37.540
And the Mod 1 had a
two-bit op-code field,

00:57:37.540 --> 00:57:39.490
which meant it had first
three, and then later

00:57:39.490 --> 00:57:42.050
four, instructions.

00:57:42.050 --> 00:57:43.790
So I got involved,
among other things,

00:57:43.790 --> 00:57:46.140
with writing software for that.

00:57:46.140 --> 00:57:48.130
I remember a very
happy afternoon

00:57:48.130 --> 00:57:50.380
writing a sub-routine
with a great loop

00:57:50.380 --> 00:57:53.860
in it to perform addition.

00:57:53.860 --> 00:57:57.910
You didn't just write down add
instruction on that machine.

00:57:57.910 --> 00:58:02.020
So that was good fun.

00:58:02.020 --> 00:58:06.822
Anyhow, once I saw the way the
instructions were mechanized,

00:58:06.822 --> 00:58:09.280
which, of course, were very
reminiscent of the Harvard Mark

00:58:09.280 --> 00:58:13.330
4, because of the people
who were mechanizing them,

00:58:13.330 --> 00:58:17.740
I got involved in the
instruction set design.

00:58:17.740 --> 00:58:23.710
And so there was going to
be a machine called Mod 2,

00:58:23.710 --> 00:58:26.380
but it kind of
collapsed along the way.

00:58:26.380 --> 00:58:29.020
So there was going to be
a machine called Mod 3,

00:58:29.020 --> 00:58:31.720
and it has three bits in the
instruction code field, all

00:58:31.720 --> 00:58:33.950
of eight instructions.

00:58:33.950 --> 00:58:35.680
What a great field to work in.

00:58:35.680 --> 00:58:38.920
And so I got involved
with Al Hopkins

00:58:38.920 --> 00:58:42.970
and with Ray Alonso in designing
the instruction set for that,

00:58:42.970 --> 00:58:48.380
and we actually published
that, first in IEEE, and then

00:58:48.380 --> 00:58:50.350
in Gordon Bell's book
on computer structures,

00:58:50.350 --> 00:58:52.520
I'm sure that's
well known to you.

00:58:52.520 --> 00:58:54.430
And we put in there
a case study of how

00:58:54.430 --> 00:58:56.430
to design an instruction
set under these very

00:58:56.430 --> 00:58:57.826
tight constraints.

00:59:01.720 --> 00:59:03.820
Anyhow, Al Hopkins
looked at that and said,

00:59:03.820 --> 00:59:05.582
boy, that's
complicated, it's got

00:59:05.582 --> 00:59:07.290
multiply instructions
and all that stuff,

00:59:07.290 --> 00:59:09.520
we'll call that Mod 3C.

00:59:09.520 --> 00:59:14.860
And he had a rival design
called Mod 3S, for simple.

00:59:14.860 --> 00:59:20.830
And anyhow, those were just, I
don't know what you call them,

00:59:20.830 --> 00:59:22.490
pilot models of one
sort or another.

00:59:22.490 --> 00:59:27.040
But the Mod 3C was, in fact,
chosen as the original Apollo

00:59:27.040 --> 00:59:28.360
guidance computer.

00:59:28.360 --> 00:59:31.090
All 4,096 words of it.

00:59:31.090 --> 00:59:35.092
Each word 15 bits, plus parity,
all the stuff you now know.

00:59:35.092 --> 00:59:36.445
HALL: Pardon me a minute.

00:59:36.445 --> 00:59:38.910
Wasn't that called Mod 1B?

00:59:38.910 --> 00:59:43.840
BLAIR-SMITH: There was a 1B, but
that was those 2-bit op-code.

00:59:43.840 --> 00:59:46.140
There was Mod 1A and Mod 1B.

00:59:46.140 --> 00:59:48.600
HALL: Well, there's a
report that's about Mod 1B,

00:59:48.600 --> 00:59:53.010
and that sounds like it's what
you're describing as Mod 3.

00:59:53.010 --> 00:59:56.550
BLAIR-SMITH: Well, there may
have been a publication on 1B

00:59:56.550 --> 01:00:00.570
that I don't remember, but
the one I have the author

01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:01.920
credit for was about 3C.

01:00:06.670 --> 01:00:09.600
Anyway, so I guess the
closest I got to hardware

01:00:09.600 --> 01:00:15.180
is that I was encouraged
to keep the logic simple,

01:00:15.180 --> 01:00:18.300
to learn how to draw three
input NOR gate diagrams.

01:00:18.300 --> 01:00:21.750
So I drew the diagrams,
whether anything good ever

01:00:21.750 --> 01:00:25.150
came of that, I'm not at
all certain, but it was fun.

01:00:25.150 --> 01:00:27.420
And so I got kind of
a feel for the way

01:00:27.420 --> 01:00:30.080
these things were put together.

01:00:30.080 --> 01:00:34.830
Anyway, so, of
course, the Yul system

01:00:34.830 --> 01:00:37.320
had to, as it was
originally designed to do,

01:00:37.320 --> 01:00:40.920
flex so as to accommodate
somewhat different designs

01:00:40.920 --> 01:00:45.640
of machines, and it
did that all right.

01:00:45.640 --> 01:00:47.940
At some level, I think
it wasn't until we got

01:00:47.940 --> 01:00:52.680
into what was originally called
AGC-4 that is, as if it was Mod

01:00:52.680 --> 01:00:57.690
4, that we began to talk about
an interpretive language.

01:00:57.690 --> 01:01:01.920
So the assembler had to handle
both the interpretive code

01:01:01.920 --> 01:01:04.170
and the regular
code, and to make

01:01:04.170 --> 01:01:06.467
sure there weren't any improper
transitions between one

01:01:06.467 --> 01:01:07.050
and the other.

01:01:09.660 --> 01:01:12.960
But anyhow, the NGC-4
had 11 instructions

01:01:12.960 --> 01:01:15.630
in its 3-bit instruction
code, and that's

01:01:15.630 --> 01:01:20.310
because I lifted a great
stunt from the Bendix G-20,

01:01:20.310 --> 01:01:22.110
I believe it was called.

01:01:22.110 --> 01:01:24.870
And anybody who can remember
Bendix, even as a company,

01:01:24.870 --> 01:01:26.880
never mind a computer
maker, is doing well.

01:01:30.130 --> 01:01:32.250
But the key
instruction there that

01:01:32.250 --> 01:01:35.040
made a lot of additional
instructions possible

01:01:35.040 --> 01:01:38.330
was called index, which would
pick up a full-sized data

01:01:38.330 --> 01:01:40.680
word, ad it to the
next instruction line,

01:01:40.680 --> 01:01:45.460
and simply execute the
result as an instruction.

01:01:45.460 --> 01:01:48.040
And because it was possible
for this addition to overflow,

01:01:48.040 --> 01:01:49.740
we got more op-codes.

01:01:49.740 --> 01:01:53.820
So that that's how we
kind of cheated, on that.

01:01:53.820 --> 01:01:57.600
But ultimately, the instruction
sets of these machines

01:01:57.600 --> 01:02:02.040
were decided basically
around a table of four of us.

01:02:02.040 --> 01:02:05.340
Al Hopkins kind of
being the system man,

01:02:05.340 --> 01:02:09.120
Ray Alonso being
the, as I recall,

01:02:09.120 --> 01:02:13.150
the input-output specialist,
Herb Thaler of Raytheon--

01:02:13.150 --> 01:02:16.080
and you remember him, I'm sure--

01:02:18.900 --> 01:02:22.370
for circuitry, and I was
there representing software.

01:02:22.370 --> 01:02:26.040
So the outlines,
and to some extent,

01:02:26.040 --> 01:02:27.670
the details of the
instruction sets,

01:02:27.670 --> 01:02:30.480
got cut decided in that way.

01:02:30.480 --> 01:02:35.100
And I do remember in
AGC-4 just barely managing

01:02:35.100 --> 01:02:37.020
to sneak in a
divide instruction.

01:02:37.020 --> 01:02:39.730
Everybody said, oh no, that's
too hard, how can you do that?

01:02:39.730 --> 01:02:41.410
I said, well, here's how.

01:02:41.410 --> 01:02:44.400
And so we snuck it in there.

01:02:44.400 --> 01:02:47.790
And then, of course,
later on with Block 2,

01:02:47.790 --> 01:02:49.980
we had to go through a
lot more instructions.

01:02:49.980 --> 01:02:53.745
So we managed to buy
a number of stunts,

01:02:53.745 --> 01:02:55.620
that maybe I don't have
time to go into here,

01:02:55.620 --> 01:02:59.310
to make the total
number 34, plus about

01:02:59.310 --> 01:03:05.220
50 or 60 op-codes in the
interpretive set, which

01:03:05.220 --> 01:03:08.160
was a curious Polish
prefix notation.

01:03:08.160 --> 01:03:15.400
We owed a lot to
Lukasiewicz for that.

01:03:15.400 --> 01:03:19.440
So that was most of the
developmental things

01:03:19.440 --> 01:03:21.597
that I got involved in.

01:03:21.597 --> 01:03:23.680
I did get into a little
bit of system programming.

01:03:23.680 --> 01:03:26.242
I wrote something
called routine 29,

01:03:26.242 --> 01:03:28.200
which was supposed to
wave the rendezvous radar

01:03:28.200 --> 01:03:32.200
dish around so that it could
find the command module--

01:03:32.200 --> 01:03:34.830
that is, the dish on the LEM.

01:03:34.830 --> 01:03:37.960
And I got it so it
worked fast enough,

01:03:37.960 --> 01:03:40.874
although only with
perhaps 99% reliability,

01:03:40.874 --> 01:03:42.540
and I have a feeling
somebody trashed it

01:03:42.540 --> 01:03:45.720
as being a little weird.

01:03:45.720 --> 01:03:49.641
But in the time budget
allotted to this thing,

01:03:49.641 --> 01:03:51.390
there was no way to
do any more than that.

01:03:54.250 --> 01:03:59.400
So finally, in the
Apollo 13 business,

01:03:59.400 --> 01:04:03.510
I was among those
who valiantly tried

01:04:03.510 --> 01:04:05.100
to come up with software fixes.

01:04:05.100 --> 01:04:07.370
The particular
problem was, okay,

01:04:07.370 --> 01:04:09.400
the mated spacecraft are
approaching the Earth,

01:04:09.400 --> 01:04:11.070
they're got to be separated.

01:04:11.070 --> 01:04:14.100
Can we can we throw
a little program up

01:04:14.100 --> 01:04:19.780
into the erasable memory to fire
the maneuvering jets, the RCS

01:04:19.780 --> 01:04:22.200
jets of the LEM, to
make it blast away?

01:04:22.200 --> 01:04:24.669
So I hastily threw
something together.

01:04:24.669 --> 01:04:26.460
But then it occurred
to somebody that we've

01:04:26.460 --> 01:04:29.080
got air pressure in the tunnel
between the two spacecraft,

01:04:29.080 --> 01:04:31.121
and that will do very
nicely to blast them apart,

01:04:31.121 --> 01:04:32.670
and that's the way it was done.

01:04:32.670 --> 01:04:36.790
So that didn't come to anything.

01:04:36.790 --> 01:04:40.870
After that, I worked on
various other projects.

01:04:40.870 --> 01:04:44.110
There was a machine we called
the cubic inch computer,

01:04:44.110 --> 01:04:48.510
and Dave, you'll remember that,
I'm sure, which, of course, was

01:04:48.510 --> 01:04:49.510
really an imperial inch.

01:04:49.510 --> 01:04:53.170
I believe it was an inch
and a quarter on each side

01:04:53.170 --> 01:04:54.790
by the time it was done.

01:04:54.790 --> 01:04:55.970
But that was fun.

01:04:55.970 --> 01:04:58.300
It had a square root
instruction, as well as

01:04:58.300 --> 01:05:03.550
the divide instruction,
among other virtues.

01:05:03.550 --> 01:05:07.990
But it came to
nothing, in the end.

01:05:07.990 --> 01:05:10.570
Then I got involved in the
space shuttle business, and then

01:05:10.570 --> 01:05:13.780
just a little bit of
the control system

01:05:13.780 --> 01:05:16.180
mathematics for
the space station.

01:05:16.180 --> 01:05:18.430
Which, considering
how many years

01:05:18.430 --> 01:05:20.180
that was before there
was a space station,

01:05:20.180 --> 01:05:22.870
was kind of curious to remember.

01:05:22.870 --> 01:05:26.950
And then, at the end
of 1981, I decided

01:05:26.950 --> 01:05:30.850
that the government in general,
and NASA in particular,

01:05:30.850 --> 01:05:33.470
had lost their interest in
advancing the art of computing.

01:05:33.470 --> 01:05:36.010
So I bugged out
and I went and got

01:05:36.010 --> 01:05:39.730
involved in some crazy
start-ups in the PC field.

01:05:39.730 --> 01:05:42.260
And I've been involved
in that ever since,

01:05:42.260 --> 01:05:44.524
although I'm now in a
good company, rather

01:05:44.524 --> 01:05:45.190
than a start-up.

01:05:48.430 --> 01:05:50.770
HALL: I think, going
back to your Mod

01:05:50.770 --> 01:05:53.755
3, in my terminology,
that's AGC-3, I think.

01:05:53.755 --> 01:05:55.630
BLAIR-SMITH: Yes, we
did call it that, right.

01:05:55.630 --> 01:05:57.860
As soon as it became an
AGC we changed its name.

01:05:57.860 --> 01:06:01.162
HALL: That 1B was a
very early version,

01:06:01.162 --> 01:06:05.300
so that was my misunderstanding.

01:06:05.300 --> 01:06:08.020
BLAIR-SMITH: 1B, as I recall,
controlled a little stepper

01:06:08.020 --> 01:06:12.565
motor, to make a little wheel
adopt various angular states.

01:06:16.950 --> 01:06:20.030
BRISS: Very impressive
group, here.

01:06:20.030 --> 01:06:22.530
I'm the manufacturing grunt.

01:06:22.530 --> 01:06:27.170
In the history of flight, I
spent time my time in the Navy,

01:06:27.170 --> 01:06:30.800
during World War 2, in the
Air Force as an air crewman

01:06:30.800 --> 01:06:32.120
and as a mechanic.

01:06:32.120 --> 01:06:36.010
Coming out of there, I decided
to go to school off and on,

01:06:36.010 --> 01:06:38.850
like many of who knew
what we wanted to do.

01:06:38.850 --> 01:06:40.970
But bottom line, I got a
job with a company called

01:06:40.970 --> 01:06:42.670
American Machine and Foundry.

01:06:42.670 --> 01:06:45.710
And we got involved in--

01:06:45.710 --> 01:06:47.840
I did most of it,
the design work,

01:06:47.840 --> 01:06:50.390
a lot of the building work,
on the flight simulator

01:06:50.390 --> 01:06:53.400
that turned out to be the
flight simulator for the B52.

01:06:53.400 --> 01:06:54.650
MINDELL: What was the company?

01:06:54.650 --> 01:06:56.600
BRISS: American
Machine and Foundry.

01:06:56.600 --> 01:06:58.680
I think it's now
a Boeing company.

01:06:58.680 --> 01:06:59.420
BATES: Yes.

01:06:59.420 --> 01:07:01.236
HALL: Were you here in Boston?

01:07:01.236 --> 01:07:03.860
BRISS: Yeah, right on Comm Ave.
Right above the Clark and White

01:07:03.860 --> 01:07:04.360
building.

01:07:04.360 --> 01:07:11.140
HALL: I worked for
them until 1952.

01:07:11.140 --> 01:07:15.350
I think worked on
their radar simulator.

01:07:15.350 --> 01:07:16.600
BRISS: Okay, I wasn't on that.

01:07:16.600 --> 01:07:19.685
I was on the flight simulator.

01:07:19.685 --> 01:07:22.610
HALL: From '48 to '52.

01:07:22.610 --> 01:07:29.592
BRISS: I was there
probably around '50 to '54,

01:07:29.592 --> 01:07:32.219
and bummed around a
little bit, again,

01:07:32.219 --> 01:07:33.260
looking for things to do.

01:07:33.260 --> 01:07:35.390
Continued schooling
a little bit.

01:07:35.390 --> 01:07:41.669
And from America Machine and
Foundry the next job, real job,

01:07:41.669 --> 01:07:42.210
was Raytheon.

01:07:45.910 --> 01:07:48.330
A couple of people left
American Machine and Foundry,

01:07:48.330 --> 01:07:52.720
and suggested I
interview in Sudbury.

01:07:52.720 --> 01:07:55.490
I had a little experience
in magnesium machining

01:07:55.490 --> 01:07:59.630
and welding, and also in
new metal and new beryllium,

01:07:59.630 --> 01:08:01.845
and stuff like that, which
I never saw at Raytheon,

01:08:01.845 --> 01:08:04.580
by the way, either one of them.

01:08:04.580 --> 01:08:07.220
And I got hired by using some
buzz words that evidently

01:08:07.220 --> 01:08:09.950
impressed by the
interviewer, as most of you

01:08:09.950 --> 01:08:13.160
know, certain things
happen during an interview.

01:08:13.160 --> 01:08:17.220
And one of them was
potting those two metals.

01:08:17.220 --> 01:08:21.460
Potting, it turns out,
they meant encapsulation.

01:08:21.460 --> 01:08:24.649
Well, the encapsulation
they did on this program

01:08:24.649 --> 01:08:28.510
was electric frying
pan and a little mold.

01:08:28.510 --> 01:08:31.939
And put the stuff in the frying
pan, heated it up, and sealed

01:08:31.939 --> 01:08:33.140
it.

01:08:33.140 --> 01:08:36.970
Bottom line, after a few
months of building breadboards

01:08:36.970 --> 01:08:40.890
there, that was in
1963, I believe.

01:08:40.890 --> 01:08:47.420
Multi-layer boards were not in
the cards at that time for us.

01:08:47.420 --> 01:08:54.319
We built prototypes, still
using diodes and resistors,

01:08:54.319 --> 01:09:00.410
phased into what we called a TL
47 can that came out of Draper.

01:09:00.410 --> 01:09:01.880
Worked a little
bit with Fairchild,

01:09:01.880 --> 01:09:05.029
going back and forth
across the country.

01:09:05.029 --> 01:09:08.689
Got involved in the
famous purple plague.

01:09:08.689 --> 01:09:10.500
I still don't know
what the hell it is.

01:09:13.790 --> 01:09:15.965
BATES: Purple plague,
I forgot about that.

01:09:15.965 --> 01:09:18.950
BRISS: My major assignment
was the Tray A, which

01:09:18.950 --> 01:09:21.950
looked pretty simple, but
now I understand why I

01:09:21.950 --> 01:09:23.779
used to curse these developers.

01:09:23.779 --> 01:09:26.330
Listening to you guys, how you
got involved in this thing.

01:09:26.330 --> 01:09:27.891
How do you think this stuff up?

01:09:30.800 --> 01:09:33.140
My job, as a
manufacturing engineer,

01:09:33.140 --> 01:09:36.870
was to try to put it
together and make it work.

01:09:36.870 --> 01:09:40.415
And by the way, I was one
of those engineers who

01:09:40.415 --> 01:09:43.779
were helping these old ladies,
and my acceptance rate was 23%.

01:09:46.790 --> 01:09:48.149
Maybe that's why I left--

01:09:48.149 --> 01:09:55.940
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:09:55.940 --> 01:09:57.477
BATES: We had big trash cans.

01:10:00.477 --> 01:10:02.310
BRISS: Those [INAUDIBLE]
were tough to weld.

01:10:02.310 --> 01:10:05.780
But getting back to
before get there,

01:10:05.780 --> 01:10:10.910
putting the Tray A
together with the diodes--

01:10:10.910 --> 01:10:13.100
and these were
miniatures, by the way.

01:10:18.380 --> 01:10:21.270
Putting them together
was a lot of fun.

01:10:21.270 --> 01:10:24.170
Again, you use a frying
pan, and eventually an oven,

01:10:24.170 --> 01:10:26.030
and we didn't know what
temperatures to use.

01:10:26.030 --> 01:10:28.613
It would screw up the resistors,
it would screw up the diodes,

01:10:28.613 --> 01:10:30.920
or the capacitors, whatever.

01:10:30.920 --> 01:10:34.200
Anything that could get
screwed up, we screwed up.

01:10:34.200 --> 01:10:36.065
But sooner or later
we got the hang of it,

01:10:36.065 --> 01:10:37.940
and we understood how
serious this thing was,

01:10:37.940 --> 01:10:39.860
and how complicated it was.

01:10:39.860 --> 01:10:42.830
And what it was going
to do was just amazing,

01:10:42.830 --> 01:10:44.959
absolutely amazing.

01:10:44.959 --> 01:10:47.000
And I think that's what
motivated a lot of people

01:10:47.000 --> 01:10:48.780
working a lot of hours.

01:10:48.780 --> 01:10:52.370
And I know the ladies
worked hard, but even

01:10:52.370 --> 01:10:53.930
before the ladies.

01:10:53.930 --> 01:10:57.350
Grunt engineers,
junior engineers,

01:10:57.350 --> 01:11:00.764
we spent a lot of
hours in there.

01:11:00.764 --> 01:11:02.180
To make a long
story short, we got

01:11:02.180 --> 01:11:05.270
the TL 47 cans and the purple
plague straightened out,

01:11:05.270 --> 01:11:06.471
and we started to build--

01:11:06.471 --> 01:11:07.970
MINDELL: What was
the purple plague?

01:11:07.970 --> 01:11:10.580
Purple plague was
something that happened--

01:11:10.580 --> 01:11:11.909
HALL: Gold aluminum.

01:11:11.909 --> 01:11:12.700
BRISS: What was it?

01:11:12.700 --> 01:11:13.540
HALL: Gold aluminum.

01:11:13.540 --> 01:11:14.040
BRISS: Yeah.

01:11:17.876 --> 01:11:19.730
But what really happened?

01:11:19.730 --> 01:11:22.050
I'm a mechanical guy, I
don't know anything about it.

01:11:22.050 --> 01:11:27.090
HALL: It pans into a different
alloy, which gets very brittle,

01:11:27.090 --> 01:11:27.920
and then cracked.

01:11:30.500 --> 01:11:33.230
BRISS: That was one
of our major problems.

01:11:33.230 --> 01:11:35.420
BATES: So we called the
other one purple plague?

01:11:35.420 --> 01:11:37.800
HALL: [INAUDIBLE],, this
[INAUDIBLE] effect,

01:11:37.800 --> 01:11:38.890
or something.

01:11:38.890 --> 01:11:42.589
Anyway, it got very
brittle and would crack--

01:11:42.589 --> 01:11:44.630
BRISS: Right, it didn't
last long when you put it

01:11:44.630 --> 01:11:48.133
on the shake table, even
when it was encapsulated.

01:11:55.020 --> 01:11:57.260
At any rate, we got
rid of the diodes,

01:11:57.260 --> 01:11:59.590
and we started to use
these TL 47 cans--

01:11:59.590 --> 01:12:03.010
I think that was
what they call them.

01:12:03.010 --> 01:12:03.920
What was that?

01:12:03.920 --> 01:12:05.750
HALL: That's TL 47.

01:12:05.750 --> 01:12:08.450
BRISS: And things
went pretty well,

01:12:08.450 --> 01:12:11.810
until we engineers and
the supervisors started

01:12:11.810 --> 01:12:15.350
to weld them, and we
learned a lot that way.

01:12:18.950 --> 01:12:21.960
We tried soldering
them, gold to gold.

01:12:21.960 --> 01:12:23.990
Silver soldering, in
a very miniature way,

01:12:23.990 --> 01:12:25.070
with shaky hands.

01:12:29.310 --> 01:12:33.990
Sooner or later, we got this
Tray A portion of it, which

01:12:33.990 --> 01:12:38.530
was just my job, my particular
project was all of these cans.

01:12:38.530 --> 01:12:41.250
And the other guys had Tray
Bs, but were still using

01:12:41.250 --> 01:12:45.938
the diodes other type of
electronic components.

01:12:50.820 --> 01:12:52.260
Let's see.

01:12:52.260 --> 01:12:55.230
We reduced the size,
I think, at that time.

01:12:55.230 --> 01:12:56.237
'63, '64.

01:12:59.040 --> 01:13:03.870
I'm not going to say 50%, but
a lot smaller than what it was.

01:13:03.870 --> 01:13:05.760
I believe that the
modules in the diodes

01:13:05.760 --> 01:13:09.210
were about a foot long, maybe
10 and 1/2 inches to a foot,

01:13:09.210 --> 01:13:10.530
and about an inch and 1/4 wide.

01:13:10.530 --> 01:13:11.030
You got one?

01:13:13.980 --> 01:13:15.017
BATES: Just so happens.

01:13:15.017 --> 01:13:16.200
BRISS: Just so happens.

01:13:16.200 --> 01:13:16.870
You got it.

01:13:16.870 --> 01:13:19.484
There she is right there.

01:13:19.484 --> 01:13:20.400
TURNER: Both versions.

01:13:20.400 --> 01:13:21.690
BRISS: They're beautiful.

01:13:21.690 --> 01:13:24.530
They are beautiful.

01:13:24.530 --> 01:13:27.210
I haven't seen one since--

01:13:27.210 --> 01:13:32.790
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:13:32.790 --> 01:13:38.830
POUNDSTONE: We left the cans and
went to flat packs, eventually.

01:13:38.830 --> 01:13:43.720
BRISS: Okay, these
components are--

01:13:43.720 --> 01:13:46.270
there they are,
they're right there.

01:13:46.270 --> 01:13:48.260
What's this, one generation
and then the other?

01:13:48.260 --> 01:13:50.010
TURNER: Yeah, the
bottom one is a Block 1.

01:13:50.010 --> 01:13:52.051
BRISS: That's the one I'm
[? working ?] [? on. ?]

01:13:52.051 --> 01:13:53.580
HALL: The bottom on has a TL 47.

01:13:53.580 --> 01:13:57.284
BRISS: It looks like one
of my welds in there, too.

01:13:57.284 --> 01:13:58.950
SPEAKER: That's why
he's got the module.

01:13:58.950 --> 01:14:02.110
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:14:09.010 --> 01:14:10.501
BATES: Is that what
we call a mule?

01:14:10.501 --> 01:14:11.500
HALL: No, that's a mule.

01:14:11.500 --> 01:14:12.740
BATES: Oh, those are mules.

01:14:12.740 --> 01:14:14.020
Okay.

01:14:14.020 --> 01:14:15.810
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:14:24.940 --> 01:14:27.090
BRISS: At any rate,
somehow we got it

01:14:27.090 --> 01:14:28.680
out of Sudbury,
which means that we

01:14:28.680 --> 01:14:33.280
were on the road to accepting
the guidance computer as it

01:14:33.280 --> 01:14:33.780
was.

01:14:33.780 --> 01:14:36.876
And at the same time,
I worked on the LEM--

01:14:36.876 --> 01:14:40.890
that was a Grumman
associate project.

01:14:40.890 --> 01:14:47.560
But we took the Apollo
project over to the G Building

01:14:47.560 --> 01:14:53.190
in Waltham, and my job was to
help set up manufacturing lines

01:14:53.190 --> 01:14:56.310
and processors here.

01:14:56.310 --> 01:15:01.070
And I've got pictures of
a bunch of ladies in there

01:15:01.070 --> 01:15:05.690
on the assembly floor,
that most of those people

01:15:05.690 --> 01:15:07.080
made me look good.

01:15:07.080 --> 01:15:09.870
Made me look real good.

01:15:09.870 --> 01:15:13.330
And they were all
hardworking people, too.

01:15:13.330 --> 01:15:15.840
I did enough the
damage on that program

01:15:15.840 --> 01:15:18.600
that they were going to
assign me to the Poseidon,

01:15:18.600 --> 01:15:21.110
as you know, with Raytheon.

01:15:21.110 --> 01:15:25.800
The particular
group I was in, we

01:15:25.800 --> 01:15:28.590
were hired with different
government numbers,

01:15:28.590 --> 01:15:31.230
contracts, I guess, programs.

01:15:31.230 --> 01:15:33.720
And my part of that
program was running out,

01:15:33.720 --> 01:15:36.220
and I was going to be
assigned to Poseidon.

01:15:36.220 --> 01:15:40.530
But around that time, in
1966, the Vietnam War was on,

01:15:40.530 --> 01:15:43.900
and I became a
peacenik, and I got out

01:15:43.900 --> 01:15:47.730
and I get out of the
industrial military complex

01:15:47.730 --> 01:15:49.630
and went to work for Polaroid.

01:15:49.630 --> 01:15:53.540
And I retired from Polaroid
about 10 years ago.

01:15:53.540 --> 01:15:54.540
And that's it.

01:15:57.670 --> 01:15:59.680
Compared to this
group, [INAUDIBLE],,

01:15:59.680 --> 01:16:00.520
it's an experience.

01:16:05.182 --> 01:16:07.390
HALL: I've got par of mine
written up here, because I

01:16:07.390 --> 01:16:10.840
want to hold to the script.

01:16:10.840 --> 01:16:12.974
I started from
Harvard, too, but when

01:16:12.974 --> 01:16:14.640
I was in the Harvard
physics department,

01:16:14.640 --> 01:16:17.950
the word digital
computer never came up.

01:16:17.950 --> 01:16:22.270
I didn't know what a decimal
and binary system was,

01:16:22.270 --> 01:16:24.400
I didn't know what
hexadecimal-- none of that stuff

01:16:24.400 --> 01:16:28.190
was ever mentioned at Harvard,
in the physics department.

01:16:28.190 --> 01:16:32.350
So when I got to
Draper Lab in 1952,

01:16:32.350 --> 01:16:34.300
I was working for
Hal Laning, and he

01:16:34.300 --> 01:16:39.700
was over his head
programming types of things

01:16:39.700 --> 01:16:42.160
with Whirlwind computers.

01:16:42.160 --> 01:16:45.510
So I began to be interested
in a fairly early.

01:16:45.510 --> 01:16:53.710
And by the middle '50s, when the
lab got involved with Polaris,

01:16:53.710 --> 01:16:56.970
I had run some
experiments about how

01:16:56.970 --> 01:17:00.080
to make certain types
of logic models.

01:17:00.080 --> 01:17:04.270
So I got grabbed from
the pile of people,

01:17:04.270 --> 01:17:08.790
and sort of headed up the
Polaris computer development.

01:17:08.790 --> 01:17:11.830
And since that went
very successful,

01:17:11.830 --> 01:17:18.160
I sort of inherited the Apollo
responsibility, when that came.

01:17:18.160 --> 01:17:22.690
When NASA awarded the
instrumentation lab contract

01:17:22.690 --> 01:17:25.240
for the Apollo
guidance system, they

01:17:25.240 --> 01:17:27.670
planned to follow in the
footsteps of the Polaris

01:17:27.670 --> 01:17:29.470
program, which they did.

01:17:29.470 --> 01:17:37.330
And they planned to set up a
group of support contractors

01:17:37.330 --> 01:17:40.120
to manufacture the
system, and we would

01:17:40.120 --> 01:17:42.380
have the design responsibility.

01:17:42.380 --> 01:17:45.250
And as I mentioned, I
inherited the responsibility

01:17:45.250 --> 01:17:49.650
from managing and the
guidance computer,

01:17:49.650 --> 01:17:54.080
since I had spent several
years on the Polaris program,

01:17:54.080 --> 01:18:00.670
and essentially knew the type
of job that we had on Apollo.

01:18:00.670 --> 01:18:04.420
So to follow this
plan we put together,

01:18:04.420 --> 01:18:08.410
after we received
the whole project,

01:18:08.410 --> 01:18:11.700
we put together a documentation
to support a bidders conference

01:18:11.700 --> 01:18:14.680
in early 1962.

01:18:14.680 --> 01:18:17.890
Alonso and Hanley
here, and I were

01:18:17.890 --> 01:18:20.140
the nucleus of the
evaluation team

01:18:20.140 --> 01:18:23.290
for the technical proposal.

01:18:23.290 --> 01:18:26.320
Fortunately, for me at
least, with Jack Poundstone's

01:18:26.320 --> 01:18:30.020
understanding and
experience with MIT

01:18:30.020 --> 01:18:34.030
on the Polaris program,
Raytheon put together

01:18:34.030 --> 01:18:36.730
a winning proposal.

01:18:36.730 --> 01:18:39.250
We had developed, by
that point, a very good

01:18:39.250 --> 01:18:40.940
working relationship with them.

01:18:40.940 --> 01:18:44.050
And with Jack's influence,
I maintained that

01:18:44.050 --> 01:18:47.860
throughout the Apollo program,
even though he evaporated.

01:18:47.860 --> 01:18:52.670
I think his heritage stayed
with us, fortunately.

01:18:52.670 --> 01:18:54.590
BLONDIN: He makes
a pretty big ghost.

01:18:54.590 --> 01:18:57.780
HALL: Yeah, right so you
felt his influence, too.

01:18:57.780 --> 01:19:02.641
Well it's hard to miss him,
floating around [INAUDIBLE]..

01:19:02.641 --> 01:19:04.015
BATES: He has a
certain presence.

01:19:06.940 --> 01:19:10.660
HALL: Also, somewhat related to
the overall development plan,

01:19:10.660 --> 01:19:12.250
there were turf
battles, two of them,

01:19:12.250 --> 01:19:15.370
actually, that had
surfaced within the lab.

01:19:15.370 --> 01:19:16.870
One dealt with
the responsibility

01:19:16.870 --> 01:19:19.540
for test equipment,
and the second

01:19:19.540 --> 01:19:21.610
with the responsibility
for the guidance system

01:19:21.610 --> 01:19:24.130
displays and controls.

01:19:24.130 --> 01:19:27.890
In both cases logic prevailed,
and the computer portion

01:19:27.890 --> 01:19:30.500
of those were split off, and
became the responsibility

01:19:30.500 --> 01:19:32.890
of the computer design team.

01:19:32.890 --> 01:19:35.360
I turned on the computer
test equipment design

01:19:35.360 --> 01:19:37.660
over to Raytheon,
knowing that they

01:19:37.660 --> 01:19:39.730
would follow the earlier
work and requirements

01:19:39.730 --> 01:19:42.240
of the computer design team.

01:19:42.240 --> 01:19:46.060
Then the computer part of the
systems' displays and controls

01:19:46.060 --> 01:19:49.120
would be more complicated.

01:19:49.120 --> 01:19:51.310
Astronauts and
human factors types

01:19:51.310 --> 01:19:54.670
within the lab and the
spacecraft contractors

01:19:54.670 --> 01:19:58.110
would provide requirements,
such as, color of the lights,

01:19:58.110 --> 01:20:01.440
the size of the light,
the shape of the numerics,

01:20:01.440 --> 01:20:04.060
and the placement of
the action of the keys,

01:20:04.060 --> 01:20:06.970
and all that kind of
foolishness that human factors

01:20:06.970 --> 01:20:08.910
has to work out.

01:20:08.910 --> 01:20:11.270
Again, Raytheon took a
very responsible role

01:20:11.270 --> 01:20:14.380
in the mechanical design
in what eventually

01:20:14.380 --> 01:20:18.240
became known as the DSKY.

01:20:18.240 --> 01:20:21.310
This arrangement set the
baseline of the design

01:20:21.310 --> 01:20:24.610
and production responsibility,
and I was fairly comfortable

01:20:24.610 --> 01:20:27.340
with the arrangement.

01:20:27.340 --> 01:20:32.060
And a lot of this was
occurring in 1962,

01:20:32.060 --> 01:20:34.330
which was a very busy year.

01:20:34.330 --> 01:20:37.620
In addition to the
bidders conference,

01:20:37.620 --> 01:20:40.460
I got my friend
synthesizing here,

01:20:40.460 --> 01:20:44.200
Dave, going on
integrated circuits,

01:20:44.200 --> 01:20:49.570
because I felt the core
transistor type of logic

01:20:49.570 --> 01:20:53.095
that Ray Alonso and Al
Hopkins were pursuing,

01:20:53.095 --> 01:20:57.320
the things that
[INAUDIBLE] have mentioned,

01:20:57.320 --> 01:21:00.200
was not the way to go.

01:21:00.200 --> 01:21:04.120
So I challenged Dave
to try to get the ICs.

01:21:04.120 --> 01:21:10.690
This was early 62, and I
placed a bet with him, yeah.

01:21:10.690 --> 01:21:15.340
And then, in addition, in
1962, many other things

01:21:15.340 --> 01:21:16.480
were involved.

01:21:16.480 --> 01:21:19.380
Duggan had to work out the
size and shape of things going

01:21:19.380 --> 01:21:21.130
in the spacecraft,
there wasn't much room

01:21:21.130 --> 01:21:23.980
with all this garbage.

01:21:23.980 --> 01:21:27.950
So all of that kept
things pretty busy in '62.

01:21:27.950 --> 01:21:31.960
And then, near the end,
I had the responsibility

01:21:31.960 --> 01:21:38.350
of facing Charles
Frick, and I'm trying

01:21:38.350 --> 01:21:40.740
to convince him to
change this contract

01:21:40.740 --> 01:21:43.600
to use integrated circuits,
because NASA had just

01:21:43.600 --> 01:21:46.660
finished negotiating the
contract with Raytheon,

01:21:46.660 --> 01:21:49.820
and, in fact, getting
the full go-ahead

01:21:49.820 --> 01:21:51.450
to go into integrated circuits.

01:21:51.450 --> 01:21:52.900
And he was all upset, too.

01:21:52.900 --> 01:21:57.190
So I had to face Charles
Frick, and apparently

01:21:57.190 --> 01:21:59.850
was able to convince him
that this was the way to go,

01:21:59.850 --> 01:22:01.060
with integrated circuits.

01:22:01.060 --> 01:22:04.430
And we got the approval
in December of '62,

01:22:04.430 --> 01:22:09.580
to go with integrated circuits.

01:22:09.580 --> 01:22:12.970
And even though I
was comfortable,

01:22:12.970 --> 01:22:16.420
various people within
NASA had some concerns.

01:22:16.420 --> 01:22:19.360
And they brought
Bellcomm on board

01:22:19.360 --> 01:22:22.270
to search out
potential problems.

01:22:22.270 --> 01:22:25.000
Bellcomm raised several
issues, but most important

01:22:25.000 --> 01:22:27.910
were the questionable
reliability of ICs

01:22:27.910 --> 01:22:29.980
and the capability
of the MIT design

01:22:29.980 --> 01:22:33.250
team to complete the
design on schedule.

01:22:33.250 --> 01:22:35.440
This led to computer
design studies

01:22:35.440 --> 01:22:38.350
for a backup development.

01:22:38.350 --> 01:22:41.350
The backup design
faded away when

01:22:41.350 --> 01:22:45.650
it became apparent to the
MIT design was progressing,

01:22:45.650 --> 01:22:48.150
so I won't go into the
details of that backup.

01:22:48.150 --> 01:22:52.330
But it got kind of messy at
times, from my point of view.

01:22:52.330 --> 01:22:56.110
Then another NASA concern
was a contractual arrangement

01:22:56.110 --> 01:23:00.625
with Raytheon as a
subcontractor to MIT,

01:23:00.625 --> 01:23:02.260
and Jack mentioned this.

01:23:04.920 --> 01:23:08.590
The introduction of the ICs
into the computer design

01:23:08.590 --> 01:23:12.020
and adding the test equipment
to the Raytheon contract,

01:23:12.020 --> 01:23:15.640
and a multiplicity of
other design changes,

01:23:15.640 --> 01:23:18.670
was resulting in
contractual problems.

01:23:18.670 --> 01:23:20.940
And as Jack mentioned,
he was a little too soft

01:23:20.940 --> 01:23:24.630
in the way he handled this,
from the production en

01:23:24.630 --> 01:23:26.420
of the contract.

01:23:26.420 --> 01:23:29.050
So NASA was pressuring
MIT's capability

01:23:29.050 --> 01:23:33.640
to control Raytheon's
contract, so they changed

01:23:33.640 --> 01:23:35.870
their arrangement and
put anything on as a sub

01:23:35.870 --> 01:23:40.340
to AC Spark Plug, who would be
the prime reporting to NASA.

01:23:40.340 --> 01:23:42.940
This change in contractual
responsibilities

01:23:42.940 --> 01:23:47.020
worked okay, even though Draper
Lab was extremely nervous

01:23:47.020 --> 01:23:50.040
for a long time
about that change.

01:23:50.040 --> 01:23:55.350
We felt we should have a more
responsible role in production.

01:23:55.350 --> 01:24:03.650
Then in 1963, there was major
progress in various areas.

01:24:03.650 --> 01:24:08.030
One was getting the
AGC-4 up and running,

01:24:08.030 --> 01:24:11.680
that was the first
real Apollo computer

01:24:11.680 --> 01:24:13.330
using integrated circuits.

01:24:13.330 --> 01:24:16.750
Dave had built one previously,
and that was sort of

01:24:16.750 --> 01:24:19.320
transferred into the AGC-4.

01:24:19.320 --> 01:24:22.430
Also, Raytheon built
their first version,

01:24:22.430 --> 01:24:25.590
which was called a AGB-4B,
which was essentially

01:24:25.590 --> 01:24:27.970
a copy with integrated circuits.

01:24:27.970 --> 01:24:32.710
And THEN by the end of '63,
they were cranking out modules

01:24:32.710 --> 01:24:36.380
for AGC-5, which was the
things that you talked about

01:24:36.380 --> 01:24:39.610
with the TL-47 cans,
and so forth, and would

01:24:39.610 --> 01:24:41.530
be the first flight
computer, which was up

01:24:41.530 --> 01:24:45.190
and running in '64.

01:24:45.190 --> 01:24:47.740
Then that was
followed with a set

01:24:47.740 --> 01:24:50.380
of what was called
implementation meetings, which

01:24:50.380 --> 01:24:53.650
Cline was very
familiar with, which

01:24:53.650 --> 01:24:55.750
put the autopilot
in the computer,

01:24:55.750 --> 01:24:57.800
and lots of other
responsibilities

01:24:57.800 --> 01:25:01.390
from the computer, which sort of
forced us to go into the Block

01:25:01.390 --> 01:25:03.566
2 design.

01:25:03.566 --> 01:25:04.690
POUNDSTONE: Thank goodness.

01:25:04.690 --> 01:25:08.800
HALL: And serious
expansion and logic

01:25:08.800 --> 01:25:13.240
required, which went to the flat
packs, and multi-layer boards,

01:25:13.240 --> 01:25:15.730
and so forth.

01:25:15.730 --> 01:25:20.110
And I don't want to say much
about my future after Apollo,

01:25:20.110 --> 01:25:21.630
because it sort
of went downhill,

01:25:21.630 --> 01:25:23.370
and I wasn't too happy with it.

01:25:25.725 --> 01:25:27.100
BATES: You put
water on the moon?

01:25:27.100 --> 01:25:28.200
HALL: I put water on the moon.

01:25:28.200 --> 01:25:28.900
BATES: No, remember?

01:25:28.900 --> 01:25:30.100
We were looking for
water on the moon.

01:25:30.100 --> 01:25:31.766
HALL: You were, but
I wasn't doing that.

01:25:31.766 --> 01:25:33.625
BATES: You were part
of the evaluation team.

01:25:33.625 --> 01:25:35.750
POUNDSTONE: If you would
have gotten fired like me,

01:25:35.750 --> 01:25:37.125
you'd have been
a lot better off.

01:25:37.125 --> 01:25:40.120
HALL: I would have been much
better off, that's right.

01:25:40.120 --> 01:25:43.280
But I had fun, I wouldn't
want all those manager

01:25:43.280 --> 01:25:44.030
responsibilities.

01:25:44.030 --> 01:25:45.880
HANLEY: We were still
working together, though.

01:25:45.880 --> 01:25:47.500
HALL: Yes, we were
still working together,

01:25:47.500 --> 01:25:48.999
but you were giving
me trouble, too.

01:25:51.520 --> 01:25:52.990
That's my history.

01:25:52.990 --> 01:25:57.290
I retired in 1988.

01:25:57.290 --> 01:25:58.220
HANLEY: Okay, next.

01:25:58.220 --> 01:25:59.960
My name is David Hanley.

01:25:59.960 --> 01:26:04.460
I guess you want a little bit of
some of the old history of what

01:26:04.460 --> 01:26:05.450
we were doing.

01:26:05.450 --> 01:26:09.200
I got a degree in
physics in '52,

01:26:09.200 --> 01:26:13.500
and went to work at
Sylvania on semiconductors.

01:26:13.500 --> 01:26:15.257
MINDELL: Where's
your degree from?

01:26:15.257 --> 01:26:16.090
HANLEY: What's that?

01:26:16.090 --> 01:26:18.710
MINDELL: Where from,
the degree in physics?

01:26:18.710 --> 01:26:20.690
HANLEY: RPI.

01:26:20.690 --> 01:26:27.810
And since everybody at that
time was into germanium,

01:26:27.810 --> 01:26:31.415
I had to melt my own silicon,
and I had barricade devices.

01:26:31.415 --> 01:26:35.840
I had a package of it go off,
lots of things like that.

01:26:35.840 --> 01:26:41.240
In '54, I joined the
instrumentation lab,

01:26:41.240 --> 01:26:45.950
and I was there for 33 years,
but, actually, I went back.

01:26:45.950 --> 01:26:50.880
So it could be 34, because
I went back part-time.

01:26:50.880 --> 01:26:54.440
I joined the Air
Force department,

01:26:54.440 --> 01:27:01.940
and we made a lot of analog
computers [INAUDIBLE]

01:27:01.940 --> 01:27:07.580
and work with big transmission
tubes, subminiature tubes,

01:27:07.580 --> 01:27:08.630
transistors.

01:27:11.960 --> 01:27:15.080
I got interested in
digital computers

01:27:15.080 --> 01:27:17.690
and did a survey
for the Air Force

01:27:17.690 --> 01:27:21.060
on all military
digital computers.

01:27:21.060 --> 01:27:24.990
It was either late '50s or '60s,
somewhere in that vicinity.

01:27:28.280 --> 01:27:33.380
Then on the next system,
we got the Veran computer,

01:27:33.380 --> 01:27:37.430
which was the GP
and DDA, and we had

01:27:37.430 --> 01:27:40.890
[INAUDIBLE] in the computer.

01:27:40.890 --> 01:27:45.200
So at the time I was still
in the Air Force department,

01:27:45.200 --> 01:27:47.420
Eldon would invite me
to hear presentations

01:27:47.420 --> 01:27:50.830
by Hal Laning on the Mars
reconnaissance and Apollo

01:27:50.830 --> 01:27:53.240
computer.

01:27:53.240 --> 01:27:55.910
I did a lot of
work on components.

01:27:55.910 --> 01:27:57.790
I played a lot,
working on the benches

01:27:57.790 --> 01:28:03.110
and with integrated circuits,
and all types of things,

01:28:03.110 --> 01:28:05.360
and with other components.

01:28:05.360 --> 01:28:07.730
Eldon wanted me to tell
a couple of stories

01:28:07.730 --> 01:28:13.010
of how the AGC
went to ICs and how

01:28:13.010 --> 01:28:16.500
it got the multi-layered board.

01:28:16.500 --> 01:28:20.930
At the time, Albert
Hopkins and Ramon Alonso

01:28:20.930 --> 01:28:24.080
were designing the AGC
with core magnetic logic.

01:28:24.080 --> 01:28:27.520
I guess Eldon explained
a little bit of that,

01:28:27.520 --> 01:28:32.690
and he told me a little bit
about the old history today.

01:28:32.690 --> 01:28:36.620
So Eldon wanted me to keep
current with the design

01:28:36.620 --> 01:28:40.700
and to see if it
could be implemented

01:28:40.700 --> 01:28:43.130
with integrated circuits.

01:28:43.130 --> 01:28:51.360
And after a while, I found it
so difficult that I bet Eldon

01:28:51.360 --> 01:28:56.510
that I could build an AGC
faster with integrated circuits

01:28:56.510 --> 01:28:58.400
directly.

01:28:58.400 --> 01:29:01.730
So he took my bet,
and he provided me

01:29:01.730 --> 01:29:05.600
with the ICs and one technician.

01:29:05.600 --> 01:29:08.350
I got the instruction set
from Hugh Blair-Smith,

01:29:08.350 --> 01:29:11.450
and, of course, that's all I
needed, because from there,

01:29:11.450 --> 01:29:15.740
I could put the rest of
the machine together.

01:29:15.740 --> 01:29:19.940
And as the months went by the
computer got bigger and bigger,

01:29:19.940 --> 01:29:21.650
and my need a little
bit more help.

01:29:21.650 --> 01:29:23.660
We did have a deadline.

01:29:23.660 --> 01:29:26.580
And that's when Eldon
got me Herb Thaler,

01:29:26.580 --> 01:29:29.750
and work on my machine.

01:29:32.770 --> 01:29:33.270
What?

01:29:33.270 --> 01:29:34.811
HALL: Herb Thaler
came from Raytheon.

01:29:34.811 --> 01:29:37.940
HANLEY: Yeah, came from
Raytheon as a resident.

01:29:37.940 --> 01:29:43.400
And that's how, eventually,
when we have the logic working,

01:29:43.400 --> 01:29:46.730
Eldon announced that the AGC
would be built with integrated

01:29:46.730 --> 01:29:49.040
circuits, and Herb
Thaler would be

01:29:49.040 --> 01:29:54.170
transferred to Albert Hopkins,
and they would build the AGC.

01:29:54.170 --> 01:30:02.240
I didn't know that Eldon went
to NASA, and all that stuff,

01:30:02.240 --> 01:30:06.710
I just found out a
lot of that recently.

01:30:06.710 --> 01:30:11.330
So I would leave most of
that explanation to Eldon.

01:30:11.330 --> 01:30:15.560
But I'll say that Eldon
did pay off his bet,

01:30:15.560 --> 01:30:16.700
so that's one thing.

01:30:19.280 --> 01:30:25.285
This is my version of how the
AGC got multi-layered boards.

01:30:25.285 --> 01:30:26.410
BATES: We all have our own.

01:30:26.410 --> 01:30:30.320
HANLEY: We all have [INAUDIBLE]
of how things happened.

01:30:36.110 --> 01:30:37.870
I was playing with
multi-layer boards

01:30:37.870 --> 01:30:44.900
on the side in the
process, but first I

01:30:44.900 --> 01:30:49.280
want to say how the IC
logic was interconnected.

01:30:49.280 --> 01:30:52.140
We've got a little bit
of the information here.

01:30:52.140 --> 01:30:55.540
The interconnection for the
logic on the Block 1, which

01:30:55.540 --> 01:30:59.530
is TL-47, and the
interconnection

01:30:59.530 --> 01:31:04.590
for the logic in the Block 2,
which was the fact pack, ICs

01:31:04.590 --> 01:31:08.130
had been done by Raytheon,
and up to that time

01:31:08.130 --> 01:31:12.755
was a nickel matrix, similar
to that was used on Polaris.

01:31:16.340 --> 01:31:22.350
While we were building
the AGC, Eldon and

01:31:22.350 --> 01:31:27.352
got a contract to build a
computer for the Air Force.

01:31:27.352 --> 01:31:29.850
I can remember that
was on a Friday,

01:31:29.850 --> 01:31:32.530
just before he was going
on a two-week vacation.

01:31:32.530 --> 01:31:33.960
That was a standard thing.

01:31:33.960 --> 01:31:38.790
He came in and told me that
besides working on an AGC,

01:31:38.790 --> 01:31:45.210
I had to build this
other computer,

01:31:45.210 --> 01:31:49.900
but he wanted to have it--

01:31:49.900 --> 01:31:52.860
well, first he told
me that I had a month

01:31:52.860 --> 01:31:57.180
to spend all the money,
for the components.

01:31:57.180 --> 01:32:02.020
I didn't know that he'd
been talking to Jim Lincoln,

01:32:02.020 --> 01:32:03.840
and Jim Lincoln
turned the job down,

01:32:03.840 --> 01:32:06.850
I hadn't heard about
that until later.

01:32:06.850 --> 01:32:13.440
And he said, the logic had
to be identical to the AGC,

01:32:13.440 --> 01:32:18.765
and it also had to be
mechanically set for the AGC.

01:32:18.765 --> 01:32:23.250
It had to have flat packs and it
had to have multi-layer boards.

01:32:23.250 --> 01:32:27.250
Well, that's a pretty
tall order for that time,

01:32:27.250 --> 01:32:30.010
since the AGC didn't even--

01:32:30.010 --> 01:32:31.800
they weren't talking
integrated circuits,

01:32:31.800 --> 01:32:35.970
they weren't talking
multi-layer boards.

01:32:35.970 --> 01:32:38.400
About a year later,
Hopkins and Taylor

01:32:38.400 --> 01:32:42.060
had finished the brass
board, which Raytheon

01:32:42.060 --> 01:32:44.910
had built the modules
with nickel matrix,

01:32:44.910 --> 01:32:47.850
and it checked out fine.

01:32:47.850 --> 01:32:51.180
Shortly thereafter, they
received the first prototype

01:32:51.180 --> 01:32:54.470
of the AGC from Raytheon.

01:32:54.470 --> 01:32:58.140
Albert and Herb found that the
prototype had too much noise

01:32:58.140 --> 01:33:00.200
and would not work.

01:33:00.200 --> 01:33:01.590
Eldon, again, was on vacation.

01:33:01.590 --> 01:33:09.150
So they called Eldon back from
vacation, and NASA, Raytheon,

01:33:09.150 --> 01:33:13.440
and the Apollo program office
were pretty upset at that time.

01:33:13.440 --> 01:33:17.100
When we are alone, Eldon
asked me where the components

01:33:17.100 --> 01:33:21.480
for the Air Force
computer were stored,

01:33:21.480 --> 01:33:24.390
and he took the set
of multi-layer boards,

01:33:24.390 --> 01:33:26.250
gave them to Raytheon--

01:33:26.250 --> 01:33:29.280
I guess it must have
been you, Jack--

01:33:29.280 --> 01:33:36.230
and within a week, Raytheon had
a new prototype all built up,

01:33:36.230 --> 01:33:39.330
that did work.

01:33:39.330 --> 01:33:44.310
And I was surprised.

01:33:44.310 --> 01:33:46.950
The Air Force
didn't say anything.

01:33:46.950 --> 01:33:49.250
NASA didn't say anything.

01:33:49.250 --> 01:33:51.630
Raytheon didn't say anything.

01:33:51.630 --> 01:33:55.510
Nobody did, they just took
the multi-layer boards,

01:33:55.510 --> 01:33:57.780
and that was it.

01:33:57.780 --> 01:34:00.264
I had to write an
[? SED ?] and a few things.

01:34:00.264 --> 01:34:01.680
POUNDSTONE: This
is the first time

01:34:01.680 --> 01:34:03.600
I ever heard that it
belonged to the Air Force.

01:34:03.600 --> 01:34:04.210
HANLEY: What's that?

01:34:04.210 --> 01:34:06.090
POUNDSTONE: I said, this
the first time I ever heard

01:34:06.090 --> 01:34:07.298
it belonged to the Air Force.

01:34:07.298 --> 01:34:08.757
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:34:09.950 --> 01:34:13.680
HANLEY: I have to
add one more thing.

01:34:13.680 --> 01:34:17.730
A little bit later you guys
made another set of modules,

01:34:17.730 --> 01:34:21.360
you gave them to Eldon,
Eldon gave them to me,

01:34:21.360 --> 01:34:23.550
and I put them in the
Air Force computer.

01:34:23.550 --> 01:34:25.830
POUNDSTONE: I see.

01:34:25.830 --> 01:34:27.210
HANLEY: Everybody was happy.

01:34:27.210 --> 01:34:28.918
POUNDSTONE: How did
we get paid for that?

01:34:30.487 --> 01:34:31.320
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:34:31.320 --> 01:34:33.035
POUNDSTONE: Ask Cline.

01:34:33.035 --> 01:34:35.341
No, I don't think
we ought to do that.

01:34:35.341 --> 01:34:36.840
HANLEY: I have to
add that there's--

01:34:36.840 --> 01:34:39.170
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:34:41.130 --> 01:34:45.340
HANLEY: There's a lot of these
stories that are hidden away,

01:34:45.340 --> 01:34:51.405
that maybe things were pushed
a little bit to get it to work.

01:34:51.405 --> 01:34:53.940
BRISS: It looks like all the
informal organizations always

01:34:53.940 --> 01:34:55.660
gets things done.

01:34:55.660 --> 01:34:58.850
BATES: It's the people,
not the organization.

01:34:58.850 --> 01:35:00.790
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:35:02.320 --> 01:35:05.730
FRASIER: Dave, I have a slightly
different slant on the story,

01:35:05.730 --> 01:35:07.290
it doesn't conflict
with your facts,

01:35:07.290 --> 01:35:10.620
but it adds a little bit
of around it, I'll get to.

01:35:10.620 --> 01:35:11.980
Unless you want me you to--

01:35:11.980 --> 01:35:14.000
MINDELL: Well, we have
one more person to go,

01:35:14.000 --> 01:35:15.530
and then we can get
right into that.

01:35:15.530 --> 01:35:16.660
FRASIER: OK.

01:35:16.660 --> 01:35:19.960
You want me to get
right into it, or wait?

01:35:19.960 --> 01:35:23.370
DUGGAN: By now,
it's all been said.

01:35:23.370 --> 01:35:24.880
HANLEY: I'm curious, really.

01:35:24.880 --> 01:35:26.220
MINDELL: Let Ed go, and then--

01:35:26.220 --> 01:35:26.845
HANLEY: Oh, Ed?

01:35:26.845 --> 01:35:27.900
All right, got it.

01:35:27.900 --> 01:35:29.947
DUGGAN: I've worked
on the program.

01:35:29.947 --> 01:35:30.780
HANLEY: What's that?

01:35:30.780 --> 01:35:33.405
DUGGAN: I've worked on a program
back there, and some of this--

01:35:33.405 --> 01:35:36.290
HANLEY: Oh, I know that.

01:35:36.290 --> 01:35:39.010
DUGGAN: We saw a lot of
each other, at times.

01:35:39.010 --> 01:35:43.080
My name is Ed Duggan, and
I apologize, first of all,

01:35:43.080 --> 01:35:46.050
for being late, but I tried
to cram too many things

01:35:46.050 --> 01:35:47.910
into this morning's
car trip around town,

01:35:47.910 --> 01:35:49.320
and it didn't work out too well.

01:35:52.710 --> 01:35:54.780
Let's see, I came
to the laboratory,

01:35:54.780 --> 01:35:59.320
and from what I gather, this
was the format that you had.

01:35:59.320 --> 01:36:01.370
Well, I graduated from
Yale in engineering,

01:36:01.370 --> 01:36:03.156
and then came the Sloan School.

01:36:03.156 --> 01:36:05.420
The choice was to
stay at Yale and be

01:36:05.420 --> 01:36:08.390
an architect for a year, which
is about the budget I had,

01:36:08.390 --> 01:36:10.400
or come to the Sloan and
get a master's, or kill

01:36:10.400 --> 01:36:12.260
myself trying.

01:36:12.260 --> 01:36:15.320
And the latter happened, and
I didn't finish my degree,

01:36:15.320 --> 01:36:18.110
but I had left for
what was then Thompson

01:36:18.110 --> 01:36:20.420
Products, in Cleveland.

01:36:20.420 --> 01:36:23.840
And they became
TRW, down the road.

01:36:23.840 --> 01:36:25.497
BATES: Thompson Ramo Woolridge.

01:36:25.497 --> 01:36:27.080
DUGGAN: Yeah, Thompson
Ramo Woolridge.

01:36:27.080 --> 01:36:29.360
They got funded, and
then pretty soon the tail

01:36:29.360 --> 01:36:31.010
started to wag the dog.

01:36:31.010 --> 01:36:33.637
I wasn't there for that
transition, but Dan Tost was.

01:36:33.637 --> 01:36:35.720
BATES: They had to work
with them on the Air Force

01:36:35.720 --> 01:36:37.680
side of the house, for
Jupiter versus Thor.

01:36:37.680 --> 01:36:39.680
DUGGAN: And this was
my factory training.

01:36:39.680 --> 01:36:42.920
They insisted, at that time,
that any new engineering hired

01:36:42.920 --> 01:36:45.770
onto the factory floor,
for what turned out

01:36:45.770 --> 01:36:48.520
to be the requirement was, until
we started complaining so hard,

01:36:48.520 --> 01:36:52.010
they were risking losing us.

01:36:52.010 --> 01:36:53.435
I worked second
shift for a year.

01:36:53.435 --> 01:36:55.310
Now Cleveland is the
worst place in the world

01:36:55.310 --> 01:36:56.950
to be as a young bachelor.

01:36:56.950 --> 01:36:59.690
If you keep him occupied
from 3:00 in the afternoon

01:36:59.690 --> 01:37:02.490
until midnight, the only thing
that save you is the bars

01:37:02.490 --> 01:37:03.825
stay open until 3:00.

01:37:03.825 --> 01:37:08.390
So it was kind of a grim first
year, but by complaining,

01:37:08.390 --> 01:37:16.790
I ended up getting assigned to a
project group, which basically,

01:37:16.790 --> 01:37:23.240
in that time-- this is circa
1952, '53, in that time frame--

01:37:23.240 --> 01:37:28.790
had the task under a fellow
name John Cofill, who some of us

01:37:28.790 --> 01:37:31.250
have known to come by.

01:37:31.250 --> 01:37:37.970
But anyway, this his
task was to move hardware

01:37:37.970 --> 01:37:39.650
from the research
and development

01:37:39.650 --> 01:37:43.700
group, whose motto was, "we
never had that problem",

01:37:43.700 --> 01:37:48.230
and into production, which
gets nothing but problems.

01:37:48.230 --> 01:37:51.530
And he was looking for
a young, energetic kid

01:37:51.530 --> 01:37:54.870
that didn't know any better,
who thought this could be done.

01:37:54.870 --> 01:37:58.040
And I got involved in it.

01:37:58.040 --> 01:37:59.900
And it was a marvelous place.

01:37:59.900 --> 01:38:03.050
It was rocket pumps for
the first motors that were

01:38:03.050 --> 01:38:05.520
used on some of these birds.

01:38:05.520 --> 01:38:08.890
And, in fact, I
saw the [INAUDIBLE]

01:38:08.890 --> 01:38:10.955
get blown up on that.

01:38:10.955 --> 01:38:13.820
The wiring worked wrong
on that [INAUDIBLE]..

01:38:13.820 --> 01:38:16.400
But anyway, after a
while, this was a facility

01:38:16.400 --> 01:38:18.500
that was owned by the
Navy during the war,

01:38:18.500 --> 01:38:21.690
and quite a few of the
inspectors carried over.

01:38:21.690 --> 01:38:24.200
And there was a
Navy culture there,

01:38:24.200 --> 01:38:26.750
and the inspection was tough.

01:38:26.750 --> 01:38:29.030
The misery, if you were
involved with the factory,

01:38:29.030 --> 01:38:31.000
was very high.

01:38:31.000 --> 01:38:34.010
And I decided life had to
be better somewhere, maybe

01:38:34.010 --> 01:38:37.250
if I finish that thesis, because
the fifth year was coming up,

01:38:37.250 --> 01:38:39.500
and that was it.

01:38:39.500 --> 01:38:41.960
So I quit, and this was
the third time I quit.

01:38:41.960 --> 01:38:43.770
We used to get raises that way.

01:38:43.770 --> 01:38:46.710
And went to Vermont on one--

01:38:46.710 --> 01:38:48.590
I got involved with
the magnetic drums

01:38:48.590 --> 01:38:51.290
up there for three months,
and then the crickets

01:38:51.290 --> 01:38:53.640
started to make me nervous
and I moved back to Cleveland

01:38:53.640 --> 01:38:54.950
with another raise.

01:38:54.950 --> 01:38:57.260
They moved me back and
forth a couple of times.

01:38:57.260 --> 01:39:02.390
But anyway, I came back to
MIT with the dedicated purpose

01:39:02.390 --> 01:39:05.180
of finishing that thesis,
and I needed a part-time job

01:39:05.180 --> 01:39:06.750
for the summer.

01:39:06.750 --> 01:39:09.590
And John Newton, who ran
the mechanical design

01:39:09.590 --> 01:39:13.880
part of the Draper Laboratory
that was involved in the Navy

01:39:13.880 --> 01:39:21.245
program, hired me to assist in
his production control activity

01:39:21.245 --> 01:39:22.760
and whatever.

01:39:22.760 --> 01:39:25.255
Run errands, and
that type of thing.

01:39:25.255 --> 01:39:26.630
And when I walked
into the place,

01:39:26.630 --> 01:39:29.150
I got there at 7:30 in the
morning, and Newton showed up,

01:39:29.150 --> 01:39:32.320
I think it was around 9:30.

01:39:32.320 --> 01:39:34.470
And I watched all the
secretaries arrive,

01:39:34.470 --> 01:39:39.150
and this was in April, May,
they all had shorts on.

01:39:39.150 --> 01:39:44.600
And I was tie and dark
suit, and the whole thing.

01:39:44.600 --> 01:39:46.090
And I couldn't believe it.

01:39:46.090 --> 01:39:47.970
And I finally got
involved in this,

01:39:47.970 --> 01:39:51.680
and to make a long story
short, when I finally read

01:39:51.680 --> 01:39:53.270
or realize that
I'd died and gone

01:39:53.270 --> 01:39:56.330
to Heaven, that was when
I sent a requisition down

01:39:56.330 --> 01:39:59.436
to purchasing, and the guy
came back with six numbers,

01:39:59.436 --> 01:40:02.060
and he said, quit bothering me,
and use these as you need them.

01:40:04.830 --> 01:40:06.830
If I'd done that
at TRW, my career

01:40:06.830 --> 01:40:09.470
would have been a lot
shorter than five years.

01:40:09.470 --> 01:40:15.650
But anyway, the was six months
got parlayed into 34 years.

01:40:15.650 --> 01:40:17.780
And I think, for
probably all of us--

01:40:17.780 --> 01:40:20.510
I can't speak for all
of us, but my guess

01:40:20.510 --> 01:40:23.480
would be that the Apollo
program was the M water

01:40:23.480 --> 01:40:25.580
mark of our careers.

01:40:25.580 --> 01:40:29.720
And it came at a
period of time when

01:40:29.720 --> 01:40:33.170
there was a national
purpose, it came well-funded.

01:40:33.170 --> 01:40:38.230
And, from my perspective
and the things that I do,

01:40:38.230 --> 01:40:40.760
namely the packaging
area, came at a time

01:40:40.760 --> 01:40:47.270
where we had experience in depth
with another well-inspired and

01:40:47.270 --> 01:40:52.790
well-run program, the Navy
missiles, which preceded it.

01:40:52.790 --> 01:40:56.710
And as far as packaging goes,
most of what we brought to--

01:40:56.710 --> 01:40:58.862
with the exception
of ICs and things

01:40:58.862 --> 01:41:01.320
were developed along the way,
and I'm not minimizing those,

01:41:01.320 --> 01:41:04.490
they were very important--

01:41:04.490 --> 01:41:07.630
the welded technology was the
one we came to the Apollo with.

01:41:10.630 --> 01:41:14.770
The development of that was done
through the laboratory, based

01:41:14.770 --> 01:41:18.810
on, as I recall it, early work.

01:41:18.810 --> 01:41:21.220
There were modules in
the Mark 1 guidance

01:41:21.220 --> 01:41:25.690
system for the Polaris,
which were basically

01:41:25.690 --> 01:41:28.000
col soldered joints.

01:41:28.000 --> 01:41:31.630
While that problem was being
worked on with Schenectady,

01:41:31.630 --> 01:41:38.260
in terms of quality control,
the secondary effort,

01:41:38.260 --> 01:41:41.220
through Sam Francis,
of Francis Associates,

01:41:41.220 --> 01:41:45.250
was doing thermal work across
the street for the Air Force,

01:41:45.250 --> 01:41:46.765
came up with a--

01:41:46.765 --> 01:41:48.420
I don't know what
it was unsolicited,

01:41:48.420 --> 01:41:50.350
or not-- but it was
a small proposal

01:41:50.350 --> 01:41:54.010
to look at the ability of using
the technique in the vacuum

01:41:54.010 --> 01:41:57.990
tube industry for making
vacuum tubes, that is, welding.

01:41:57.990 --> 01:42:00.850
And he basically
came in with a way

01:42:00.850 --> 01:42:05.230
of arranging the parts
involved in a NOR gate,

01:42:05.230 --> 01:42:07.450
so that you could
roll them together.

01:42:07.450 --> 01:42:12.660
And the program somehow
fell under Steve Cudlitz,

01:42:12.660 --> 01:42:15.526
who was looking for somebody
with vast welding experience.

01:42:15.526 --> 01:42:16.900
And, of course,
when he found out

01:42:16.900 --> 01:42:19.630
that I knew a lot
about welding from TRW,

01:42:19.630 --> 01:42:22.930
he never though of asking what
kind of welding or anything

01:42:22.930 --> 01:42:25.497
else, sufficient to
meet his requirements.

01:42:28.120 --> 01:42:32.060
So that there were
100 kVA [INAUDIBLE]

01:42:32.060 --> 01:42:34.120
welders doing turbine blades.

01:42:34.120 --> 01:42:38.560
He just transferred that
into two 15/1000 wires,

01:42:38.560 --> 01:42:40.480
went out and bought
me welding machines,

01:42:40.480 --> 01:42:43.247
which blew those joints out
faster than you could put them

01:42:43.247 --> 01:42:44.560
in.

01:42:44.560 --> 01:42:47.630
And when I got the arc
to be about this long,

01:42:47.630 --> 01:42:49.480
I decided that maybe
there was something

01:42:49.480 --> 01:42:52.640
wrong with these electrodes,
we ought to look elsewhere.

01:42:52.640 --> 01:42:55.420
So I got involved with
Gunnar Johnson at Raytheon,

01:42:55.420 --> 01:42:59.390
and the old Chapel Street, and
that was an odyssey in itself.

01:43:03.520 --> 01:43:05.290
But there was one
thing in that phase

01:43:05.290 --> 01:43:07.060
of the program on
the Polaris that I

01:43:07.060 --> 01:43:09.310
think is a significant
difference in the structure

01:43:09.310 --> 01:43:12.100
of the way the
management, the funding,

01:43:12.100 --> 01:43:14.200
and how it was controlled.

01:43:14.200 --> 01:43:17.240
And this added to my idea that
I certainly was in Heaven,

01:43:17.240 --> 01:43:21.520
and that is the Navy
had us send money

01:43:21.520 --> 01:43:24.370
from our contract
to whoever we bought

01:43:24.370 --> 01:43:27.890
from on a major development like
that, and we could control it.

01:43:27.890 --> 01:43:30.400
In other words, if they weren't
doing what we wanted, then

01:43:30.400 --> 01:43:32.350
we would say, well,
we'll get somebody else,

01:43:32.350 --> 01:43:33.190
and shut them off.

01:43:33.190 --> 01:43:35.290
And it's amazing
the attention you

01:43:35.290 --> 01:43:36.980
can develop with
that kind of a clout,

01:43:36.980 --> 01:43:40.000
and I had never had
that experience before.

01:43:40.000 --> 01:43:41.900
Whenever I went near a
vendor before at TRW,

01:43:41.900 --> 01:43:43.960
I had two purchasing
guys with me,

01:43:43.960 --> 01:43:47.221
and they wouldn't let me
stay overnight by myself.

01:43:47.221 --> 01:43:48.970
BATES: I remember the
first contract says,

01:43:48.970 --> 01:43:50.450
build it like they tell you to.

01:43:50.450 --> 01:43:56.860
DUGGAN: Yeah, it was
open-ended, but it was great.

01:44:00.580 --> 01:44:08.380
So we were able to come make a
prototype, which was a bench--

01:44:08.380 --> 01:44:11.024
one of them was a DDA, I think.

01:44:11.024 --> 01:44:12.690
HALL: Yeah, it was
the Polaris computer,

01:44:12.690 --> 01:44:17.415
at the time, which
was a DDA computer.

01:44:17.415 --> 01:44:18.969
BATES: The Mark 1.

01:44:18.969 --> 01:44:20.510
DUGGAN: Yeah, it
was a Mark 1, and it

01:44:20.510 --> 01:44:25.420
counted down the trajectory
and came up with an answer

01:44:25.420 --> 01:44:28.430
for a given set.

01:44:28.430 --> 01:44:30.970
And we had this
out on the display

01:44:30.970 --> 01:44:34.760
bench that was worked up, I
remember I got the wrong answer

01:44:34.760 --> 01:44:35.260
one time.

01:44:35.260 --> 01:44:37.060
But it was the same
solution, moving backwards.

01:44:37.060 --> 01:44:38.809
And fortunately, the
guy was demonstrating

01:44:38.809 --> 01:44:41.366
[INAUDIBLE] to some Navy brass.

01:44:41.366 --> 01:44:43.590
But anyway, that's
a whole other story.

01:44:43.590 --> 01:44:47.620
BATES: However, Raytheon was
not in on the Mark 1 program.

01:44:47.620 --> 01:44:50.410
They came in on Mark 2
on a competitive basis.

01:44:50.410 --> 01:44:53.455
Jack never believed that, but
it was a competitive basis.

01:44:53.455 --> 01:44:55.060
HALL: Competitive
basis, but again,

01:44:55.060 --> 01:44:59.330
since we had this [INAUDIBLE]
with you, and that--

01:44:59.330 --> 01:45:00.850
BATES: With the welded design.

01:45:00.850 --> 01:45:02.933
DUGGAN: Well, Chapel Street,
I raised so much hell

01:45:02.933 --> 01:45:05.830
down there one day, we
started getting people

01:45:05.830 --> 01:45:09.160
from Sudbury facility that were
getting interesting in what

01:45:09.160 --> 01:45:13.300
was going on, and what was this
making so much noise about?

01:45:13.300 --> 01:45:17.450
I could yell a lot
louder in those days.

01:45:17.450 --> 01:45:19.570
BATES: Actually,
from GE's standpoint,

01:45:19.570 --> 01:45:22.990
we were happy to get Raytheon
in doing the computer,

01:45:22.990 --> 01:45:26.140
because we had so much
trouble with light military,

01:45:26.140 --> 01:45:28.160
tying to get them
to do something.

01:45:28.160 --> 01:45:29.760
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:45:31.840 --> 01:45:33.400
BATES: I was at GE.

01:45:33.400 --> 01:45:37.657
I was the Polaris
program manager at GE.

01:45:37.657 --> 01:45:39.240
It was called the
ordnance department.

01:45:39.240 --> 01:45:44.400
It was under Hoyt, Jerry Hoyt.

01:45:44.400 --> 01:45:48.670
And the thing was, that when
they broke away from the Army

01:45:48.670 --> 01:45:53.440
in Huntsville, it went to GE
in Pittsville, the ordnance

01:45:53.440 --> 01:45:57.490
department, and the ordnance
department had the IMU,

01:45:57.490 --> 01:45:58.500
they did the IMU.

01:45:58.500 --> 01:46:02.140
The computer stuff
was done by military.

01:46:07.715 --> 01:46:09.820
MINDELL: Was this is
the same part of GE that

01:46:09.820 --> 01:46:12.580
was doing with the Mark 56?

01:46:12.580 --> 01:46:14.140
BATES: Yes.

01:46:14.140 --> 01:46:15.930
MINDELL: No, no,
the fire control--

01:46:15.930 --> 01:46:18.490
BATES: Yes.

01:46:18.490 --> 01:46:21.370
They were doing that in OP-1.

01:46:21.370 --> 01:46:25.325
Losing money on every
one they delivered.

01:46:25.325 --> 01:46:27.670
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:46:29.860 --> 01:46:31.470
HALL: Cline hasn't
finished his story.

01:46:31.470 --> 01:46:34.450
With his breadboard,
birth there's one story.

01:46:34.450 --> 01:46:36.850
What about Captain Harold?

01:46:36.850 --> 01:46:37.900
DUGGAN: Captain Harold?

01:46:37.900 --> 01:46:38.860
HALL: You don't
remember that one?

01:46:38.860 --> 01:46:39.910
DUGGAN: No, you tell me.

01:46:39.910 --> 01:46:42.100
HALL: Well, Captain
Harold, who was

01:46:42.100 --> 01:46:46.100
the Navy's boss on this whole
program, he was the big guy.

01:46:46.100 --> 01:46:50.530
And Ed had put this welded
computer out here on a bench,

01:46:50.530 --> 01:46:51.330
like this.

01:46:51.330 --> 01:46:54.160
And they brought Captain
Harold to look at this thing,

01:46:54.160 --> 01:46:55.540
because we were
trying to sell it

01:46:55.540 --> 01:46:58.130
to the Navy, this
approach Mark 2.

01:46:58.130 --> 01:47:00.550
And when you brought him
in and showed it to him,

01:47:00.550 --> 01:47:03.100
the thing blew up in his face.

01:47:03.100 --> 01:47:05.350
Got his attention.

01:47:05.350 --> 01:47:07.500
DUGGAN: That's right, he
was leaning over a module.

01:47:07.500 --> 01:47:10.090
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:47:11.020 --> 01:47:13.180
BATES: Captain Harold
was the guy in the Navy

01:47:13.180 --> 01:47:17.290
who came down to Huntsville, and
the first head of SP, Special

01:47:17.290 --> 01:47:19.450
Projects Office,
and one of the guys

01:47:19.450 --> 01:47:24.210
that worked for him was
a commander, Bill Kurtz,

01:47:24.210 --> 01:47:26.260
and he is the same guy
that worked on Apollo.

01:47:26.260 --> 01:47:28.330
So you can see, there
was an awful lot

01:47:28.330 --> 01:47:33.010
of interlacing with people, and
that's what made this thing go.

01:47:33.010 --> 01:47:36.610
It wasn't an organization,
and it wasn't a contract,

01:47:36.610 --> 01:47:38.880
it was people working together.

01:47:38.880 --> 01:47:40.630
MINDELL: I think
Harold was the guy who

01:47:40.630 --> 01:47:43.570
brought the Bureau of Ordnance
to Draper during the war.

01:47:43.570 --> 01:47:45.340
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:47:45.340 --> 01:47:47.214
BATES: That's right.

01:47:47.214 --> 01:47:49.380
DUGGAN: I remember coming
back from an early meeting

01:47:49.380 --> 01:47:52.080
at Raytheon where we were
discussing the type of things

01:47:52.080 --> 01:47:54.080
we were building, and
what we wanted to do them.

01:47:54.080 --> 01:47:56.055
It was close to that
Chapel Street experience,

01:47:56.055 --> 01:48:00.666
and he says, well, we got
their money noses to itch.

01:48:00.666 --> 01:48:03.040
Because now we started seeing
more and more people coming

01:48:03.040 --> 01:48:06.960
from down the road.

01:48:06.960 --> 01:48:09.820
Well, anyway, some
of the things I

01:48:09.820 --> 01:48:16.120
brought with me, maybe
shifting into electronics.

01:48:16.120 --> 01:48:18.310
First of all, the mechanical
problem, and secondly,

01:48:18.310 --> 01:48:21.510
the mule, which I
think you've seen.

01:48:21.510 --> 01:48:27.100
[INAUDIBLE] We ended up
with this little ULB,

01:48:27.100 --> 01:48:29.350
Universal Logic Block
is what I called it,

01:48:29.350 --> 01:48:31.090
and basically we
could build a fixture

01:48:31.090 --> 01:48:34.420
into this
injection-molded piece.

01:48:34.420 --> 01:48:38.170
And once we had a fairly
defined length of this thing,

01:48:38.170 --> 01:48:42.080
we could make injection
moldings for either end of it.

01:48:42.080 --> 01:48:44.350
Now it had to be two
injection moldings, simply

01:48:44.350 --> 01:48:46.870
because the hold-down
screw was not

01:48:46.870 --> 01:48:48.760
dead in the middle of
anything, so we had

01:48:48.760 --> 01:48:51.710
to offset one way or the other.

01:48:51.710 --> 01:48:55.690
And so the concept, which I've
been dedicated to for most

01:48:55.690 --> 01:48:58.480
of my packaging, and design
experience, for that matter,

01:48:58.480 --> 01:49:01.350
is if you can build the
tubing into the part,

01:49:01.350 --> 01:49:03.610
then you've got a way of
getting reproducibility

01:49:03.610 --> 01:49:04.870
that's fairly good.

01:49:04.870 --> 01:49:06.430
You've got other
issues, obviously,

01:49:06.430 --> 01:49:07.429
but you certainly have--

01:49:07.429 --> 01:49:09.640
the fixturing is there
for you, and it's

01:49:09.640 --> 01:49:11.090
repeatable, and so forth.

01:49:11.090 --> 01:49:13.399
And even with
electronics, I know

01:49:13.399 --> 01:49:15.440
that you could experience
a lot of value changes,

01:49:15.440 --> 01:49:17.620
but you could usually,
as a mechanical engineer,

01:49:17.620 --> 01:49:20.110
raising enough hell so the
sizes tried to stay the same,

01:49:20.110 --> 01:49:23.320
so that it gave a place to
have a fairly stable place

01:49:23.320 --> 01:49:25.470
to execute the design.

01:49:25.470 --> 01:49:32.780
Hal Laning got interested in
this, and we made a module.

01:49:32.780 --> 01:49:36.310
And Hal had an
interconnection program,

01:49:36.310 --> 01:49:37.900
which would do the
interconnection

01:49:37.900 --> 01:49:41.920
from the to/from list,
which is the signal list.

01:49:41.920 --> 01:49:46.240
And that signal list had all
of the connections required

01:49:46.240 --> 01:49:50.020
for that module, and he
could put it in his computer

01:49:50.020 --> 01:49:51.760
and come out with a matrix.

01:49:51.760 --> 01:49:54.760
If he knew how many lines
along the wires were,

01:49:54.760 --> 01:49:56.350
and how many vertical,
he could make

01:49:56.350 --> 01:49:58.990
the pretty established
points where they made

01:49:58.990 --> 01:50:01.440
an exit on this x-y matrix.

01:50:01.440 --> 01:50:03.370
And then the matter was
cutting out the blanks

01:50:03.370 --> 01:50:05.120
in between appropriately.

01:50:05.120 --> 01:50:07.720
And this was fully automated.

01:50:07.720 --> 01:50:09.760
One thing you didn't
have in that program,

01:50:09.760 --> 01:50:13.260
is where those circuits
were functional.

01:50:13.260 --> 01:50:16.362
So that when we fired
it up, half a flip-flop

01:50:16.362 --> 01:50:18.070
would be in one module,
and the other one

01:50:18.070 --> 01:50:19.500
would be down the street.

01:50:19.500 --> 01:50:20.375
And then you've got--

01:50:26.935 --> 01:50:29.310
the width of that pulse was
enormous, four micro-seconds,

01:50:29.310 --> 01:50:30.630
wasn't it?

01:50:30.630 --> 01:50:33.170
HALL: I think it was
one micro-second.

01:50:33.170 --> 01:50:35.680
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:50:35.680 --> 01:50:37.300
SPEAKER: What year was that?

01:50:37.300 --> 01:50:38.050
DUGGAN: What this?

01:50:38.050 --> 01:50:38.841
This type of thing?

01:50:41.230 --> 01:50:42.360
That's back in '61.

01:50:45.610 --> 01:50:47.830
Because I came in '58,
and I got involved

01:50:47.830 --> 01:50:50.110
in this in the early '60s.

01:50:50.110 --> 01:50:52.330
First one I made, all the
wires came out this way,

01:50:52.330 --> 01:50:54.550
and I had a problem,
and it was designed

01:50:54.550 --> 01:50:56.850
around Mark 1 guidance.

01:50:56.850 --> 01:50:58.660
And Cutliss was in
charge, and I figured

01:50:58.660 --> 01:51:02.500
this was my last day at
the instrumentation lab.

01:51:02.500 --> 01:51:06.020
And we got hypodermic needles,
I was the biggest buyer

01:51:06.020 --> 01:51:10.250
of needles until the
hippies showed up.

01:51:10.250 --> 01:51:12.140
We put them down
in, and you could

01:51:12.140 --> 01:51:13.850
weld with them on
the bottom, and then

01:51:13.850 --> 01:51:16.584
squeeze them on the top
and put solder on them.

01:51:16.584 --> 01:51:18.000
And then came the
problem of, what

01:51:18.000 --> 01:51:20.630
do you do about those wires
that kept just jumping out?

01:51:20.630 --> 01:51:24.650
So we put RTV on
either side, then just

01:51:24.650 --> 01:51:27.740
poured epoxy on it,
which immediately made

01:51:27.740 --> 01:51:30.560
all of those tubes insulators.

01:51:30.560 --> 01:51:33.255
And we then went to a dentist's
office and got an abrader,

01:51:33.255 --> 01:51:35.635
and abrade it off so we
could make connections

01:51:35.635 --> 01:51:37.290
to the computer.

01:51:37.290 --> 01:51:40.310
And the last time I saw this
Steve Cutliss was pounding it

01:51:40.310 --> 01:51:44.240
into the back end on one
of these Mark 1 cases,

01:51:44.240 --> 01:51:51.500
and Ed had a photographer from
internal media photographing

01:51:51.500 --> 01:51:53.820
that we had installed
it in the Mark 1 case.

01:51:53.820 --> 01:51:57.650
Therefore, the funding
would be given for it.

01:51:57.650 --> 01:52:00.890
All I wanted to do was
die, because we stacked

01:52:00.890 --> 01:52:02.690
all the memory
together, and it looked

01:52:02.690 --> 01:52:05.000
like the Dow Jones
average going up

01:52:05.000 --> 01:52:06.690
and down across
the top of it, it

01:52:06.690 --> 01:52:10.040
was the worst mess I ever saw.

01:52:10.040 --> 01:52:13.410
So I decided at that point,
we had to have a better way.

01:52:13.410 --> 01:52:15.680
And that's when I discovered
[? Malco ?] connectors,

01:52:15.680 --> 01:52:17.763
because they were being
used on the fire controls.

01:52:20.259 --> 01:52:21.800
That was a gift from
Heaven, and when

01:52:21.800 --> 01:52:23.440
we got to the Apollo
program, we were

01:52:23.440 --> 01:52:25.350
able to get those cut
in half in their size,

01:52:25.350 --> 01:52:26.864
so we can shrink them down, too.

01:52:32.460 --> 01:52:34.590
So Laning made a major
contribution there,

01:52:34.590 --> 01:52:36.687
and then he got more and
more interested in how

01:52:36.687 --> 01:52:38.890
you do wiring like
this, and he soon

01:52:38.890 --> 01:52:41.570
was able to contribute
dramatically.

01:52:41.570 --> 01:52:43.650
Another person
that came from MIT

01:52:43.650 --> 01:52:46.940
by virtue of flunking
out twice, I think,

01:52:46.940 --> 01:52:49.205
was of the brightest guys
I ever met, Bob Morse.

01:52:52.130 --> 01:52:58.691
And he had the ability to
program this these matrices

01:52:58.691 --> 01:53:00.065
such that we could
run automation

01:53:00.065 --> 01:53:01.332
in the test equipment.

01:53:01.332 --> 01:53:03.290
In other words, we could
do continuity testing,

01:53:03.290 --> 01:53:05.040
and so forth.

01:53:05.040 --> 01:53:09.060
And I remember we went out
to the wire wrap people,

01:53:09.060 --> 01:53:12.190
and then give them a deck,
which validated ourselves

01:53:12.190 --> 01:53:16.470
for these techniques, and then
they could take and wire wrap,

01:53:16.470 --> 01:53:18.683
and it was out in Wisconsin--

01:53:18.683 --> 01:53:20.280
TURNER: Michigan.

01:53:20.280 --> 01:53:22.035
I've got all the
features of that program

01:53:22.035 --> 01:53:26.695
that Morse wrote, which
is a very significant--

01:53:26.695 --> 01:53:28.940
DUGGAN: It was all on
punch cards, which--

01:53:28.940 --> 01:53:31.440
MINDELL: Do you have an
interconnected [INAUDIBLE]??

01:53:31.440 --> 01:53:33.010
TURNER: No, but I have--

01:53:33.010 --> 01:53:35.080
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:53:37.760 --> 01:53:42.140
TURNER: No, but I
know basically all

01:53:42.140 --> 01:53:46.750
of the features of that wrap
program, which were taken out,

01:53:46.750 --> 01:53:48.800
which was a brilliant job.

01:53:48.800 --> 01:53:51.066
DUGGAN: So we had the
automation for accuracy,

01:53:51.066 --> 01:53:52.190
that was one of the things.

01:53:52.190 --> 01:53:53.898
We knew that, with
all of its complexity,

01:53:53.898 --> 01:53:56.990
we had to have something other
than people checking it out.

01:53:56.990 --> 01:54:00.730
And secondly, the wire wrap
gave us a reproducibility

01:54:00.730 --> 01:54:02.750
that we could replicate,
and pretty soon we

01:54:02.750 --> 01:54:05.330
found that we could
drop signal nameplates

01:54:05.330 --> 01:54:07.370
over the back of
these planes, and plop

01:54:07.370 --> 01:54:10.460
the internal part, to keep
the wires from moving around.

01:54:10.460 --> 01:54:13.110
We got into trial trouble
with wire wrap once,

01:54:13.110 --> 01:54:16.670
and that was in the factory,
and it was at Raytheon.

01:54:16.670 --> 01:54:20.060
And it turned out to be,
basically, fumes in the air

01:54:20.060 --> 01:54:21.490
were attacking the
[? polyambic ?]

01:54:21.490 --> 01:54:24.200
coating on the wire, and they
just broke up into rings.

01:54:24.200 --> 01:54:26.620
And I don't know if you
remember that problem or not,

01:54:26.620 --> 01:54:28.810
but it was a cleaning
fluid that was--

01:54:28.810 --> 01:54:30.020
BATES: Broke up into rings?

01:54:30.020 --> 01:54:32.789
DUGGAN: Yeah, little ringlets
along the polyester that was--

01:54:32.789 --> 01:54:33.830
SPEAKER: Was that Apollo?

01:54:33.830 --> 01:54:35.800
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:54:44.540 --> 01:54:46.250
DUGGAN: It wasn't
in the production,

01:54:46.250 --> 01:54:47.916
but they were doing
the wrapping for us,

01:54:47.916 --> 01:54:50.960
that we didn't have any
capability within Draper.

01:54:50.960 --> 01:54:56.840
But prior to that, we were
using the source in Michigan.

01:54:56.840 --> 01:55:04.750
And I don't know, in some we've
had major, major contributions.

01:55:04.750 --> 01:55:08.600
The things I remember, having
a debate in that early program,

01:55:08.600 --> 01:55:10.880
too, about why
welding would someday

01:55:10.880 --> 01:55:13.640
make soldering obsolete, and as
soon as we saw that was wrong,

01:55:13.640 --> 01:55:16.040
we ended up with
multi-layer boards.

01:55:16.040 --> 01:55:19.470
And we put the appropriate
metalization on the top layer,

01:55:19.470 --> 01:55:21.286
so that we could
make weld joints.

01:55:21.286 --> 01:55:25.160
And the same thing we
had to do with resistors.

01:55:25.160 --> 01:55:27.974
So we did have to modify and
customize some of the parts.

01:55:27.974 --> 01:55:29.390
Fortunately, there
are only, what,

01:55:29.390 --> 01:55:32.990
three resistors, four
resistors in that switch,

01:55:32.990 --> 01:55:36.395
and a diode, and the lead
switch were already--

01:55:39.690 --> 01:55:45.120
that was [? Kovar? ?]
[? Kovar, ?] yeah.

01:55:45.120 --> 01:55:49.414
And I remember getting
the president of one

01:55:49.414 --> 01:55:51.080
of the companies,
because I only ordered

01:55:51.080 --> 01:55:53.690
three types of resistors,
and just wanted them forever.

01:55:53.690 --> 01:55:55.820
And he came in,
and then, when he

01:55:55.820 --> 01:55:58.545
realized what program
it was-- and again,

01:55:58.545 --> 01:56:00.260
I'm blocking on the
name of the company.

01:56:02.880 --> 01:56:03.980
HALL: The IRC, wasn't it?

01:56:03.980 --> 01:56:07.209
DUGGAN: No, it was a
big resistor company.

01:56:07.209 --> 01:56:09.000
But anyway, it doesn't
make any difference.

01:56:09.000 --> 01:56:10.580
We found out what program--

01:56:10.580 --> 01:56:11.220
BATES: Bradley?

01:56:11.220 --> 01:56:12.511
DUGGAN: Bradley, Allen Bradley.

01:56:12.511 --> 01:56:13.640
BATES: Allen Bradley.

01:56:13.640 --> 01:56:15.280
DUGGAN: I've got to find
out what you guys take

01:56:15.280 --> 01:56:16.279
for these memory blocks.

01:56:19.010 --> 01:56:20.390
BATES: They were in Hunstville.

01:56:20.390 --> 01:56:21.250
DUGGAN: Yeah.

01:56:21.250 --> 01:56:23.870
And he came in and he
was so impressed on what

01:56:23.870 --> 01:56:26.030
we were working for
whom in his garage,

01:56:26.030 --> 01:56:27.530
basically, when
were in Building 5,

01:56:27.530 --> 01:56:30.290
but he said he'd make
sure we got them.

01:56:30.290 --> 01:56:34.010
Once he spoke, we get all
the resistors we wanted.

01:56:34.010 --> 01:56:35.510
We then could dictate
to you people,

01:56:35.510 --> 01:56:37.051
we don't care what
you use for logic,

01:56:37.051 --> 01:56:40.400
as long as it includes this
parts list, and that's all you

01:56:40.400 --> 01:56:43.520
get, because we can't afford
to develop the other one.

01:56:43.520 --> 01:56:48.410
So it was that type of merging
with the mechanical things.

01:56:48.410 --> 01:56:51.590
I also remember the
incident the core logic,

01:56:51.590 --> 01:56:53.810
and at that point
in time, Apollo

01:56:53.810 --> 01:56:56.260
had been gone to the lab.

01:56:56.260 --> 01:56:58.340
I remember going to
those visits and thinking

01:56:58.340 --> 01:57:00.900
these guys are crazy, but
I'll go along with it,

01:57:00.900 --> 01:57:03.500
in case that ever happens.

01:57:03.500 --> 01:57:06.560
And they couldn't have it
all housed in any place,

01:57:06.560 --> 01:57:09.160
so we moved to what was
called the Apollo Hilton,

01:57:09.160 --> 01:57:12.450
I'm sure somebody
remembers that name.

01:57:12.450 --> 01:57:16.160
And the building was empty,
it had just been vacated.

01:57:16.160 --> 01:57:18.540
But they sent, naturally,
the mechanical group

01:57:18.540 --> 01:57:20.530
down there, so it
could be by itself

01:57:20.530 --> 01:57:23.100
and not bother anybody anymore.

01:57:23.100 --> 01:57:24.682
And we started to
lay that out, and I

01:57:24.682 --> 01:57:27.140
remember I was one of the people
that complained vigorously

01:57:27.140 --> 01:57:30.320
about how large this computer
would be, if we had to do this,

01:57:30.320 --> 01:57:34.956
because I frankly didn't
know how to handle it.

01:57:34.956 --> 01:57:38.030
And that, plus the
logic issues, we

01:57:38.030 --> 01:57:40.070
ended up with NOR
logic, which basically

01:57:40.070 --> 01:57:44.560
made it very much an
upgraded derivative

01:57:44.560 --> 01:57:47.170
of the work on Trident.

01:57:47.170 --> 01:57:49.790
That covers it broadly,
at least the area

01:57:49.790 --> 01:57:51.540
that I was involved in.

01:57:51.540 --> 01:57:54.840
MINDELL: Can you say a few words
about your work after Apollo?

01:57:54.840 --> 01:57:55.950
DUGGAN: After Apollo?

01:57:55.950 --> 01:57:56.450
Yeah.

01:57:59.270 --> 01:58:02.960
My stay on the Apollo program--

01:58:02.960 --> 01:58:06.630
By that time, the group that I
had, the group got quite large,

01:58:06.630 --> 01:58:11.210
and I think, at the time,
when Apollo was in full bloom,

01:58:11.210 --> 01:58:14.960
it was about 140
people, in that range.

01:58:14.960 --> 01:58:19.800
Then we had test labs and
things like this, plotting labs.

01:58:19.800 --> 01:58:20.840
Lots of draftsmen.

01:58:20.840 --> 01:58:23.990
But the strategy for going to
that size, and then shrinking

01:58:23.990 --> 01:58:27.560
was always to have residents
from all of the participating

01:58:27.560 --> 01:58:30.620
companies send manpower in,
and then we could send it back.

01:58:33.590 --> 01:58:35.840
I had a similar
program that I left--

01:58:35.840 --> 01:58:40.280
the Navy programs went on,
and I left a senior engineer

01:58:40.280 --> 01:58:42.260
in charge of that.

01:58:42.260 --> 01:58:46.240
And that program was
running into a problem

01:58:46.240 --> 01:58:50.090
at that point in time, and
I got called back and asked

01:58:50.090 --> 01:58:53.570
to work on it, because I
was basically not doing

01:58:53.570 --> 01:58:54.922
very much on the Apollo side.

01:58:54.922 --> 01:58:55.630
Things were done.

01:58:58.430 --> 01:59:00.300
So I moved back
onto Navy programs,

01:59:00.300 --> 01:59:04.170
and was there for
the [INAUDIBLE]

01:59:04.170 --> 01:59:07.690
and the ballistic
missile phases of it.

01:59:07.690 --> 01:59:10.220
And after that, we
became the group

01:59:10.220 --> 01:59:17.180
that was the supplier for
packaging on all of the--

01:59:17.180 --> 01:59:19.180
I can't say all
of the equipment,

01:59:19.180 --> 01:59:22.260
but we certainly did all the
Navy equipment, at that point,

01:59:22.260 --> 01:59:29.090
for [INAUDIBLE] and
for the laboratory.

01:59:29.090 --> 01:59:32.340
And we did flight
electronics for many of them,

01:59:32.340 --> 01:59:34.730
and then pretty soon it was
for the entire laboratory,

01:59:34.730 --> 01:59:36.750
after we reorganized.

01:59:36.750 --> 01:59:39.470
But then, when Russia decided
to prove to be an unreliable

01:59:39.470 --> 01:59:41.250
enemy, things
started to slow down,

01:59:41.250 --> 01:59:43.760
and it looked like
the last four years

01:59:43.760 --> 01:59:47.445
I wanted to retire,
reasonably early anyway,

01:59:47.445 --> 01:59:50.180
but I'd spent that time
laying off people that got

01:59:50.180 --> 01:59:53.570
overcome by the
economics of the society.

01:59:53.570 --> 01:59:59.256
I retired in '92, but it was
the greatest place in the world

01:59:59.256 --> 02:00:00.801
to work in that time frame.

02:00:03.960 --> 02:00:06.170
BATES: With all the high
technology that you're

02:00:06.170 --> 02:00:09.050
talking about here, one of the
things that always mystified,

02:00:09.050 --> 02:00:11.480
me when I went down to
Waltham, was the Dixie

02:00:11.480 --> 02:00:15.242
cup on the wire that
came out of the veil,

02:00:15.242 --> 02:00:17.360
wire going into the
wire wrap thing.

02:00:17.360 --> 02:00:21.250
Can somebody explain to me
why we had a Dixie Cup other

02:00:21.250 --> 02:00:25.310
than it kept the wire going
in there in a straight way.

02:00:25.310 --> 02:00:27.550
And who came up with the
idea about a Dixie Cup?

02:00:27.550 --> 02:00:30.190
POUNDSTONE: It was probably
a Raytheon person, I'm sure.

02:00:30.286 --> 02:00:31.500
HANLEY: Ed, do you know why?

02:00:31.500 --> 02:00:32.060
BLONDIN: [INAUDIBLE]

02:00:32.060 --> 02:00:33.434
BATES: That was
very interesting.

02:00:33.434 --> 02:00:36.500
I mean, every one of those
[INAUDIBLE] wire-wrap machines

02:00:36.500 --> 02:00:39.880
we had, and we had 10 of them,
finally, when we were through,

02:00:39.880 --> 02:00:42.020
and nine of them were
for commercial types

02:00:42.020 --> 02:00:43.580
of application.

02:00:43.580 --> 02:00:46.856
We only used one for
Polaris and Apollo.

02:00:46.856 --> 02:00:48.980
But every one of them had
a Dixie Cup on the thing.

02:00:48.980 --> 02:00:51.260
And I asked the guys,
why is the Dixie Cup?

02:00:51.260 --> 02:00:53.070
And the guy says, it works.

02:00:53.070 --> 02:00:53.630
DUGGAN: Yeah.

02:00:53.630 --> 02:00:54.570
That's a good reason.

02:00:54.570 --> 02:00:55.736
BATES: That's a good reason.

02:00:55.736 --> 02:00:57.410
And I thought that
was a good one, too,

02:00:57.410 --> 02:00:58.777
but who came up with it?

02:00:58.777 --> 02:00:59.360
We don't know.

02:00:59.360 --> 02:01:01.460
Maybe one of the girls
did it, or somebody else.

02:01:01.460 --> 02:01:03.168
TURNER: I think goes
back to Grand Haven.

02:01:03.233 --> 02:01:04.066
BATES: You think so?

02:01:04.066 --> 02:01:04.690
TURNER: Yeah.

02:01:04.690 --> 02:01:07.340
I was involved with Grand Haven,
but that was the old days.

02:01:07.340 --> 02:01:08.315
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:01:08.536 --> 02:01:10.910
BATES: You didn't have to have
a big one, or a small one,

02:01:10.910 --> 02:01:14.270
or a plastic one, or what it
was-- it was just a Dixie Cup.

02:01:14.270 --> 02:01:17.489
[INAUDIBLE] But that's
a high technology.

02:01:17.489 --> 02:01:19.030
DUGGAN: It's like
the plumber's code.

02:01:19.030 --> 02:01:20.760
It's what he knows
and doesn't tell you.

02:01:20.890 --> 02:01:21.973
BATES: Yeah, that's right.

02:01:21.973 --> 02:01:23.304
[LAUGHS]

02:01:23.304 --> 02:01:25.220
MINDELL: Well, as you
see, we have lunch here.

02:01:25.220 --> 02:01:28.041
And figure we'll just hand the
sandwiches around, and keep

02:01:28.041 --> 02:01:28.540
talking.

02:01:28.540 --> 02:01:30.439
BATES: Sounds like a good idea.

02:01:30.439 --> 02:01:31.730
MINDELL: And continue on with--

02:01:31.730 --> 02:01:33.860
we have a couple issues
already identified.

02:01:33.860 --> 02:01:36.500
This is sort of going further.

02:01:36.500 --> 02:01:40.020
We also have some
questions and stuff.

02:01:40.020 --> 02:02:04.210
These things are all
labeled [INAUDIBLE]

02:02:04.210 --> 02:02:06.060
POUNDSTONE: [INAUDIBLE]
to avoid kinks.

02:02:06.060 --> 02:02:08.017
Got a kink in it, it was gone.

02:02:08.017 --> 02:02:10.100
BLAIR-SMITH: Somehow not
scrape the insulation off

02:02:10.100 --> 02:02:11.420
the other wires.

02:02:11.420 --> 02:02:14.910
POUNDSTONE: Well, it
was enamel insulation.

02:02:14.910 --> 02:02:17.979
That wasn't too
big of a problem.

02:02:17.979 --> 02:02:20.270
Unless you kinked it-- it
would break if you kinked it.

02:02:20.270 --> 02:02:22.436
MINDELL: So I was wondering
whether these women were

02:02:22.436 --> 02:02:24.750
textile [INAUDIBLE].

02:02:24.750 --> 02:02:26.000
POUNDSTONE: I read your stuff.

02:02:26.000 --> 02:02:27.010
You talk about textile.

02:02:27.010 --> 02:02:29.176
I think there was a bigger
background from the watch

02:02:29.176 --> 02:02:30.079
industry.

02:02:30.079 --> 02:02:32.370
I think there were some of
them came from textile but--

02:02:32.370 --> 02:02:34.036
MINDELL: It's interesting
because Draper

02:02:34.036 --> 02:02:36.920
used to do a lot of
consulting [INAUDIBLE]..

02:02:36.920 --> 02:02:38.690
POUNDSTONE: But
since all [INAUDIBLE]

02:02:38.690 --> 02:02:44.645
right there in that
area, perhaps some

02:02:44.645 --> 02:02:47.080
of the Raytheon plants
in the northern parts

02:02:47.080 --> 02:02:50.220
of the state probably had woman
from the textile industry.

02:02:50.220 --> 02:02:54.650
But I think mostly these
came from [INAUDIBLE]..

02:02:54.650 --> 02:02:57.100
And it was a very--

02:02:57.100 --> 02:03:00.269
they had [INAUDIBLE].

02:03:00.269 --> 02:03:02.060
BRISS: Jack, who ended
up being the manager

02:03:02.060 --> 02:03:03.407
of that wire-wrap area?

02:03:03.407 --> 02:03:04.490
Remember there was a guy--

02:03:04.490 --> 02:03:04.870
POUNDSTONE: Charlie [INAUDIBLE].

02:03:04.870 --> 02:03:05.480
BRISS: What was it?

02:03:05.480 --> 02:03:06.230
POUNDSTONE: Charlie [INAUDIBLE].

02:03:06.230 --> 02:03:07.330
BRISS: Yeah, that's right.

02:03:07.330 --> 02:03:08.288
POUNDSTONE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:03:08.288 --> 02:03:09.680
BRISS: What a character

02:03:09.680 --> 02:03:12.086
POUNDSTONE: Big cigar.

02:03:12.086 --> 02:03:13.710
BRISS: I always
perceived him to have--

02:03:13.710 --> 02:03:15.040
he was in business for himself.

02:03:21.600 --> 02:03:24.880
SPEAKER: I haven't
seen this picture.

02:03:24.880 --> 02:03:28.194
I thought I heard you say
card reader about the--

02:03:28.194 --> 02:03:28.860
it was the tape.

02:03:29.450 --> 02:03:32.545
POUNDSTONE: No, [INAUDIBLE].

02:03:32.545 --> 02:03:34.880
When it was time for
a mission, you guys

02:03:34.880 --> 02:03:37.700
would write them
that crazy program,

02:03:37.700 --> 02:03:39.480
do all your simulations.

02:03:39.480 --> 02:03:41.750
And then at the last one,
you decided it was right,

02:03:41.750 --> 02:03:43.920
you'd ship us the deck of cards.

02:03:43.920 --> 02:03:46.465
We had three weeks--

02:03:46.465 --> 02:03:48.140
something like
that-- to get us--

02:03:48.140 --> 02:03:48.640
[INAUDIBLE]

02:03:48.640 --> 02:03:51.209
SPEAKER: After a while, we
started doing mylar tapes.

02:03:51.209 --> 02:03:53.000
POUNDSTONE: Well, that
may be after I left.

02:03:53.000 --> 02:03:53.500
SPEAKER: Maybe that was.

02:03:53.500 --> 02:03:55.000
Because that's what
this picture is.

02:03:55.000 --> 02:03:57.510
SPEAKER: Yeah, I think it was
mylar tape [? for ?] Block 2.

02:03:57.877 --> 02:03:59.960
POUNDSTONE: Well, it was
prior to when we started.

02:03:59.960 --> 02:04:01.930
Bill [INAUDIBLE]
was [INAUDIBLE]..

02:04:04.582 --> 02:04:05.415
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:04:05.415 --> 02:04:06.940
POUNDSTONE: That could be.

02:04:06.940 --> 02:04:09.816
Things change in life.

02:04:09.816 --> 02:04:11.690
SPEAKER: But the rope
[? cores ?] were tape--

02:04:11.690 --> 02:04:13.310
POUNDSTONE: Same
concept, anyway.

02:04:13.310 --> 02:04:15.090
SPEAKER: The rope
cores were tape wound.

02:04:15.090 --> 02:04:16.740
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:04:27.056 --> 02:04:28.680
POUNDSTONE: Yeah, I
guess you're right.

02:04:28.680 --> 02:04:29.680
The conversion to tapes.

02:04:29.680 --> 02:04:32.520
HALL: It made it a little
more permanent [INAUDIBLE]..

02:04:32.520 --> 02:04:34.200
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:04:40.570 --> 02:04:42.895
SPEAKER: Then it got the
process going [INAUDIBLE]

02:04:42.895 --> 02:04:46.472
POUNDSTONE: Well, I learned
something today, too.

02:04:46.472 --> 02:04:50.169
This is really nice
to hear these stories.

02:04:50.169 --> 02:04:51.210
SPEAKER: Yes, it sure is.

02:04:51.710 --> 02:04:54.470
POUNDSTONE: This is the first
time I ever heard you say,

02:04:54.470 --> 02:04:58.660
or [INAUDIBLE] say, that when
you put that first computer

02:04:58.660 --> 02:04:59.554
together--

02:04:59.554 --> 02:05:00.470
SPEAKER: Look at this.

02:05:00.470 --> 02:05:01.240
Isn't this [INAUDIBLE]?

02:05:01.240 --> 02:05:02.740
POUNDSTONE: You
integrated circuits.

02:05:02.740 --> 02:05:04.480
You found it was too noisy.

02:05:04.480 --> 02:05:05.980
I never heard that before.

02:05:05.980 --> 02:05:09.820
But meanwhile, back at the
ranch, I had this engineer.

02:05:09.820 --> 02:05:12.600
Remember Ernie [? Auger-- ?]
great big fat guy?

02:05:12.600 --> 02:05:14.255
SPEAKER: I remember
the name, yes.

02:05:14.255 --> 02:05:17.350
POUNDSTONE: Well, Ernie used
to come to me about three times

02:05:17.350 --> 02:05:20.330
a week, and say, Jack,
I've analyzed this circuit.

02:05:20.330 --> 02:05:24.080
And it's going to be noisy,
and it will never work.

02:05:24.080 --> 02:05:25.810
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:05:37.736 --> 02:05:39.360
SPEAKER: Well, it's
sort of interesting

02:05:39.360 --> 02:05:43.185
when you go around the table
is what certain people remember

02:05:43.185 --> 02:05:44.810
versus what some
other people remember.

02:05:45.370 --> 02:05:46.995
POUNDSTONE: Well,
certain people didn't

02:05:46.995 --> 02:05:48.330
bother to tell those people.

02:05:48.330 --> 02:05:49.290
[LAUGHS]

02:05:49.290 --> 02:05:52.789
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:05:52.789 --> 02:05:54.330
BRISS: After listening
to you people,

02:05:54.330 --> 02:05:55.830
I'm surprised it
got off the ground.

02:05:56.227 --> 02:05:57.340
POUNDSTONE: It is amazing.

02:05:57.340 --> 02:06:01.200
BRISS: What was amazing to
me, on the first flight,

02:06:01.200 --> 02:06:03.740
I had a copy of a flight plan.

02:06:03.740 --> 02:06:07.349
[INAUDIBLE] And it almost
swallowed that flight plan,

02:06:07.349 --> 02:06:08.515
which was seven years later.

02:06:08.515 --> 02:06:10.255
POUNDSTONE: It was [? bad. ?]

02:06:10.255 --> 02:06:12.390
BRISS: Well, I
mean, no adjustment,

02:06:12.390 --> 02:06:13.920
it wasn't modified that much.

02:06:17.360 --> 02:06:18.850
It was a high
point in my career.

02:06:18.850 --> 02:06:20.855
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:06:21.355 --> 02:06:23.580
POUNDSTONE: Well,
it was mine too,

02:06:23.580 --> 02:06:28.040
but I never even got an
invitation to go see a shot.

02:06:31.342 --> 02:06:32.550
SPEAKER: I saw a lot of them.

02:06:32.550 --> 02:06:34.320
SPEAKER: It's funny.

02:06:34.320 --> 02:06:37.700
You were born [? into it, ?]
went to a different program.

02:06:37.700 --> 02:06:39.840
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:06:41.790 --> 02:06:43.470
BATES: You got that from me.

02:06:43.470 --> 02:06:45.355
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:06:45.855 --> 02:06:48.095
BATES: You know what that is?

02:06:48.095 --> 02:06:49.470
There's very few
of those around.

02:06:49.470 --> 02:06:51.370
It's the logic module.

02:06:51.370 --> 02:06:55.380
The welded module.

02:06:55.380 --> 02:06:57.273
BRISS: It's not one I welded.

02:06:57.273 --> 02:06:58.273
BATES: Probably not, no.

02:07:00.965 --> 02:07:01.840
I had a lot of those.

02:07:01.840 --> 02:07:06.570
In fact, I still think
I got some in my drawer.

02:07:06.570 --> 02:07:09.459
I'll have to look
at them and see.

02:07:09.459 --> 02:07:11.500
But I don't know whether
you want to bring it up,

02:07:11.500 --> 02:07:13.500
but I don't [INAUDIBLE].

02:07:13.500 --> 02:07:17.680
The thing is that [INAUDIBLE]
was chosen as program manager

02:07:17.680 --> 02:07:21.960
at MIT, or at
Instumentation Labratory,

02:07:21.960 --> 02:07:24.510
because he wrote the proposal.

02:07:24.510 --> 02:07:27.350
And he submitted the proposal.

02:07:27.350 --> 02:07:29.380
And a lot of people
wanted to have

02:07:29.380 --> 02:07:31.710
Ralph as the program manager.

02:07:31.710 --> 02:07:35.839
But Doc made the decision, that
since Milt put a proposal in,

02:07:35.839 --> 02:07:37.380
and was the proposal
manager, that he

02:07:37.380 --> 02:07:38.660
should be the program manager.

02:07:38.660 --> 02:07:39.910
POUNDSTONE: That was his role.

02:07:40.410 --> 02:07:43.860
BATES: For some
reason, somebody got

02:07:43.860 --> 02:07:48.510
upset with Milt. And the
decision was, well, we

02:07:48.510 --> 02:07:50.100
need to get Ralph out.

02:07:50.100 --> 02:07:52.470
Ralph came to work for Raytheon.

02:07:52.470 --> 02:07:57.210
And then somebody
asked him to come back.

02:07:57.210 --> 02:08:00.480
And I think [? Hoak ?]
was in on that.

02:08:00.480 --> 02:08:02.090
I don't know who else.

02:08:02.090 --> 02:08:04.410
But [? Hoak ?] went to see Doc.

02:08:04.410 --> 02:08:07.860
And then the next thing I
know, Ralph was back at--

02:08:07.860 --> 02:08:09.150
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:08:14.320 --> 02:08:18.215
HALL: The full story
hasn't been very well told.

02:08:18.215 --> 02:08:19.590
SPEAKER: Maybe
it's a good thing.

02:08:19.590 --> 02:08:20.860
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:08:25.500 --> 02:08:28.380
HALL: But there's
more to Milt there.

02:08:28.380 --> 02:08:31.310
He was a key individual
on the Mars study,

02:08:31.310 --> 02:08:34.050
which was being done
with [INAUDIBLE]----

02:08:34.050 --> 02:08:39.206
who was the predecessor of
everybody else on Apollo.

02:08:39.206 --> 02:08:39.705
[INAUDIBLE]

02:08:39.705 --> 02:08:42.500
BATES: Yeah, thank God for him.

02:08:42.500 --> 02:08:44.100
If it hadn't been
for [INAUDIBLE]

02:08:44.100 --> 02:08:45.933
the program wouldn't
have ever [INAUDIBLE]..

02:08:45.933 --> 02:08:47.560
HALL: That's right.

02:08:47.560 --> 02:08:49.570
So choosing managers.

02:08:49.570 --> 02:08:51.760
And Ralph, of course,
was in the Navy program.

02:08:51.760 --> 02:08:55.780
So Milt had the in with
the NASA [INAUDIBLE]..

02:08:55.780 --> 02:08:57.770
It was obvious that Milt
had to be the program

02:08:57.770 --> 02:09:00.190
manager for a while.

02:09:00.190 --> 02:09:01.650
DUGGAN: But he
wrote the proposal?

02:09:01.650 --> 02:09:03.000
HALL: He wrote the
proposal, right.

02:09:03.000 --> 02:09:04.583
DUGGAN: 15 words,
if I remember right.

02:09:04.583 --> 02:09:06.527
HALL: Well, maybe
it was 13 and 1/2.

02:09:06.527 --> 02:09:08.360
BATES: But between him
and Cherry, traveling

02:09:08.360 --> 02:09:09.984
with him was a real pain.

02:09:09.984 --> 02:09:12.150
DUGGAN: I used to love him
on takeoffs, where he put

02:09:12.150 --> 02:09:13.473
that pendulum up to see--

02:09:13.912 --> 02:09:15.120
BATES: [LAUGHS] That's right.

02:09:15.120 --> 02:09:16.220
DUGGAN: --what the
angle was [INAUDIBLE]..

02:09:16.220 --> 02:09:18.150
And he'd time it
going down the runway.

02:09:19.325 --> 02:09:21.330
BATES: They used to
go through Chicago,

02:09:21.330 --> 02:09:23.850
because you could get a
meal from here to Chicago--

02:09:23.850 --> 02:09:26.565
a meal from Chicago to Houston.

02:09:26.565 --> 02:09:28.065
Then, they'd go out
and eat Mexican.

02:09:28.065 --> 02:09:28.856
HALL: That's right.

02:09:29.545 --> 02:09:30.420
BATES: Remember that?

02:09:30.420 --> 02:09:31.545
HALL: Oh, I sure do.

02:09:31.545 --> 02:09:34.170
BATES: You know, how they could
ever do it I really don't know.

02:09:34.170 --> 02:09:37.390
HALL: Well, I was involved
with those studies.

02:09:37.390 --> 02:09:40.390
[INAUDIBLE]

02:09:45.335 --> 02:09:46.710
BATES: The San
Jacinto Inn, yeah.

02:09:46.710 --> 02:09:47.490
DUGGAN: The San
Jacinto Inn, yeah.

02:09:47.490 --> 02:09:48.870
BATES: They'd eat oysters.

02:09:48.870 --> 02:09:50.700
HALL: And eat oysters.

02:09:50.700 --> 02:09:54.074
And Milt and [? Art ?]
[? Metzger ?] had a bet on who

02:09:54.074 --> 02:09:54.865
could eat the most.

02:09:56.102 --> 02:09:57.060
DUGGAN: I bet Milt won.

02:09:57.060 --> 02:09:58.380
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:09:58.380 --> 02:10:00.805
HALL: I think [? Metzger ?]
gave up at about 120.

02:10:01.540 --> 02:10:04.230
BATES: Yeah, I think
he went up to 150.

02:10:04.230 --> 02:10:05.670
HALL: I don't remember.

02:10:05.670 --> 02:10:07.378
DUGGAN: The best thing
thing on that trip

02:10:07.378 --> 02:10:10.970
though, Milt traveled a lot
with a martini in his hand.

02:10:10.970 --> 02:10:12.664
We were on our way
to the [INAUDIBLE]

02:10:12.664 --> 02:10:13.830
and going through the woods.

02:10:13.830 --> 02:10:16.200
And all of the gas fires from
the oil wells [INAUDIBLE]

02:10:16.200 --> 02:10:17.340
out onto the marsh.

02:10:17.340 --> 02:10:19.820
And you pull up next to
the battleship Texas.

02:10:19.820 --> 02:10:22.020
And he gets out and
looks at this battleship,

02:10:22.020 --> 02:10:24.690
which as far as he's concerned,
in the middle of this field.

02:10:28.490 --> 02:10:29.934
And he walked all around it.

02:10:29.934 --> 02:10:30.850
He couldn't go inside.

02:10:32.935 --> 02:10:35.846
HALL: Now they've got a
London Bridge down there.

02:10:36.395 --> 02:10:38.940
BATES: These guys talk about
Laning doing the software

02:10:38.940 --> 02:10:39.440
stuff.

02:10:39.440 --> 02:10:41.125
You know, Laning
was the guy that

02:10:41.125 --> 02:10:42.720
did all the [? Q ?] guidance?

02:10:42.720 --> 02:10:48.350
Matthews did the [INAUDIBLE]
minimum stuff, in Huntsville.

02:10:48.350 --> 02:10:50.050
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:10:50.734 --> 02:10:52.150
BATES: Polaris and
the rest of it.

02:10:52.150 --> 02:10:54.139
But Laning did all
the [? Q ?] guidance.

02:10:54.139 --> 02:10:56.305
For the Air Force side of
the house, and everything.

02:10:56.305 --> 02:10:58.380
HALL: Laning did a
lot on Apollo, too.

02:11:00.860 --> 02:11:02.110
BATES: Nobody gave him credit.

02:11:02.110 --> 02:11:04.120
Do you know Laning is
still coming into the lab?

02:11:06.339 --> 02:11:08.380
He's still coming into
the lab, and they have him

02:11:08.380 --> 02:11:09.260
as a consultant.

02:11:09.260 --> 02:11:13.960
And he's working what
they call on voucher.

02:11:13.960 --> 02:11:17.607
So he makes the same amount
of money he made when he left.

02:11:17.607 --> 02:11:19.270
HALL: Do you
remember when Laning

02:11:19.270 --> 02:11:21.780
talked about a very
early computer,

02:11:21.780 --> 02:11:24.699
and only having one instruction?

02:11:24.699 --> 02:11:25.490
I vaguely remember.

02:11:25.784 --> 02:11:27.950
BLAIR-SMITH: I remember
somebody talking about that.

02:11:27.950 --> 02:11:30.940
I know that was [INAUDIBLE]
academic concept.

02:11:30.940 --> 02:11:33.660
It was kind of a Turing machine.

02:11:33.660 --> 02:11:35.540
HALL: Something like that.

02:11:35.540 --> 02:11:37.080
I vaguely remember it.

02:11:37.080 --> 02:11:39.770
Back then it didn't have
any meaning to me at all.

02:11:39.770 --> 02:11:40.990
What difference does it make?

02:11:40.990 --> 02:11:44.060
[INAUDIBLE] computer
instruction was.

02:11:47.350 --> 02:11:50.020
BLAIR-SMITH: We got into that
kind of conversation because--

02:11:50.020 --> 02:11:52.457
so here's this little computer
with three instructions,

02:11:52.457 --> 02:11:53.290
that does something.

02:11:53.290 --> 02:11:56.440
What's the smallest number
that you can have, and still

02:11:56.440 --> 02:11:57.960
have a computer?

02:11:57.960 --> 02:12:02.030
And so that was his point,
that you could do it with one.

02:12:02.030 --> 02:12:03.654
HALL: I don't see
how that's possible.

02:12:03.654 --> 02:12:05.320
BLAIR-SMITH: Obviously,
putting together

02:12:05.320 --> 02:12:07.930
a branch is very
difficult, because you

02:12:07.930 --> 02:12:10.090
have to kind of
build up the address

02:12:10.090 --> 02:12:11.860
that you're branching
to bit by bit.

02:12:11.860 --> 02:12:12.910
It takes a long time.

02:12:16.360 --> 02:12:18.810
I could probably reconstruct
what they had to do it today,

02:12:18.810 --> 02:12:20.710
but it would take me a moment.

02:12:25.340 --> 02:12:28.620
HALL: Another thing
I heard recently,

02:12:28.620 --> 02:12:32.580
that people are claiming
that the minimum number

02:12:32.580 --> 02:12:35.215
of [? instructions ?]
they've been talking about.

02:12:35.215 --> 02:12:37.320
And they've come up
with numbers much higher

02:12:37.320 --> 02:12:39.870
than three, or even eight.

02:12:39.870 --> 02:12:41.190
Have you heard anything about--

02:12:41.190 --> 02:12:42.690
BLAIR-SMITH: No, I
can't say I have.

02:12:44.929 --> 02:12:47.220
POUNDSTONE: You had the first
[INAUDIBLE] machine, huh?

02:12:47.380 --> 02:12:48.130
BLAIR-SMITH: Yeah.

02:12:48.130 --> 02:12:51.150
Yes we did.

02:12:51.150 --> 02:12:52.030
That we did.

02:12:54.242 --> 02:12:56.450
POUNDSTONE: You just weren't
smart enough to name it?

02:12:56.450 --> 02:12:58.030
BLAIR-SMITH: That's right.

02:12:58.030 --> 02:13:00.532
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:13:00.532 --> 02:13:02.490
DUGGAN: When Laning saw
his first Polaris shot,

02:13:02.490 --> 02:13:06.653
he went down [INAUDIBLE].

02:13:06.653 --> 02:13:09.870
And said well, I thought
it was going to work.

02:13:09.870 --> 02:13:12.770
And they've got all of
this program running.

02:13:12.770 --> 02:13:14.340
BATES: At the start
of this program,

02:13:14.340 --> 02:13:15.840
Laning made a funny
statement to me.

02:13:15.840 --> 02:13:16.900
We were talking about--

02:13:21.510 --> 02:13:22.760
I can't think of his name now.

02:13:26.962 --> 02:13:28.420
They were talking
about [INAUDIBLE]

02:13:28.420 --> 02:13:30.230
and his phase-lock loop.

02:13:30.230 --> 02:13:32.670
And Laning made the statement,
if [INAUDIBLE] ever had

02:13:32.670 --> 02:13:35.714
to milk a cow, he'd do it
with a phase lock loops.

02:13:35.714 --> 02:13:36.702
[LAUGHTER]

02:13:36.702 --> 02:13:38.545
I thought that was really good.

02:13:38.545 --> 02:13:41.280
A quotable quote.

02:13:41.280 --> 02:13:42.830
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:13:43.710 --> 02:13:45.790
HALL: Very early in
the Polaris progam,

02:13:45.790 --> 02:13:51.030
I remember Laning had generated
a simulation [INAUDIBLE]

02:13:51.030 --> 02:13:57.270
cross product [? steering ?] was
the key to the Polaris program.

02:13:57.270 --> 02:14:00.010
And he came into me,
showing this diagram,

02:14:00.010 --> 02:14:02.870
that the thing actually
came down to 0.

02:14:02.870 --> 02:14:05.710
And he thought
that was so great.

02:14:05.710 --> 02:14:07.180
And I was the same way.

02:14:07.180 --> 02:14:10.470
I mean, it puzzled
me why he even

02:14:10.470 --> 02:14:11.760
thought it wouldn't do that.

02:14:16.180 --> 02:14:19.560
After all, Laning invented
this scheme, cross product

02:14:19.560 --> 02:14:22.960
[? steering, ?] [INAUDIBLE]
have to come down to 0,

02:14:22.960 --> 02:14:26.280
if he did, because he was
puzzled about the fact

02:14:26.280 --> 02:14:27.335
that it actually did.

02:14:34.770 --> 02:14:37.090
He's not given much credit
for the things he did.

02:14:37.590 --> 02:14:38.550
BATES: No, he wasn't.

02:14:38.550 --> 02:14:41.190
He was very quiet, and he did
what he was supposed to do.

02:14:41.190 --> 02:14:42.606
DUGGAN: [INAUDIBLE]
the back room.

02:14:42.606 --> 02:14:44.810
And he discovered Fortran
before Fortran was born.

02:14:44.810 --> 02:14:46.060
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:14:51.160 --> 02:14:54.170
HALL: Although that
report is on his site--

02:14:54.170 --> 02:14:55.200
Laning's report.

02:14:55.200 --> 02:14:57.050
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:15:11.632 --> 02:15:13.590
BATES: I always thought
it was [INAUDIBLE] that

02:15:13.590 --> 02:15:15.100
have a [INAUDIBLE].

02:15:25.222 --> 02:15:27.680
BLAIR-SMITH: Eldon, what do
you remember of the brief visit

02:15:27.680 --> 02:15:30.300
of George Wieser?

02:15:30.300 --> 02:15:34.260
He kind of came in to sort
of save the management

02:15:34.260 --> 02:15:35.460
of the program at one point.

02:15:35.460 --> 02:15:37.296
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:15:45.505 --> 02:15:47.130
BLAIR-SMITH: I'm not
sure about George,

02:15:47.130 --> 02:15:48.135
but his name was Wieser.

02:15:48.135 --> 02:15:49.260
HALL: No, it wasn't George.

02:15:49.260 --> 02:15:51.000
George [? Reiser ?]
worked for me.

02:15:51.000 --> 02:15:52.810
BLAIR-SMITH: Oh, I'm
sorry. [? Reiser. ?]

02:15:52.810 --> 02:15:55.825
This is with a "W."

02:15:55.825 --> 02:15:59.670
W-I-E-S-E-R. He was an advisor.

02:15:59.670 --> 02:16:04.130
He came in at some point,
because the project was--

02:16:04.130 --> 02:16:06.390
the way he explained
it to me later--

02:16:06.390 --> 02:16:08.580
I ran into him
recently at a funeral.

02:16:08.580 --> 02:16:10.790
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:16:17.612 --> 02:16:19.320
BLAIR-SMITH: But he
says that the project

02:16:19.320 --> 02:16:22.710
was in trouble, because we
were all these can-do guys.

02:16:22.710 --> 02:16:24.990
And people like astronauts
and NASA folks would say,

02:16:24.990 --> 02:16:27.105
well gee, we'd
like to have this,

02:16:27.105 --> 02:16:28.230
and we'd like to have that.

02:16:28.230 --> 02:16:30.910
And they'd always say,
yeah, can do, can do.

02:16:30.910 --> 02:16:34.020
And, of course, things started
falling behind schedule,

02:16:34.020 --> 02:16:36.340
every time we said that.

02:16:36.340 --> 02:16:41.549
So he was brought in, to create
a kind of project management

02:16:41.549 --> 02:16:42.309
discipline.

02:16:42.309 --> 02:16:43.600
POUNDSTONE: [INAUDIBLE] can do.

02:16:43.600 --> 02:16:44.820
BLAIR-SMITH: Yeah, right.

02:16:44.820 --> 02:16:46.980
SPEAKER: This wasn't the
MIT team [INAUDIBLE]..

02:16:46.980 --> 02:16:49.200
BLAIR-SMITH: I wondered
if you remembered him.

02:16:49.200 --> 02:16:54.809
I only remembered him because it
was very tempting to pronounce

02:16:54.809 --> 02:16:56.370
his name "Wiser."

02:16:56.370 --> 02:16:59.160
And when he came in, I decided
I'd better make up a limerick,

02:16:59.160 --> 02:17:02.969
to remind myself and everybody
how to pronounce it properly.

02:17:02.969 --> 02:17:08.360
So it came out something like,
a stern old advisor named Wieser

02:17:08.360 --> 02:17:11.309
popped a young engineer in the
[INAUDIBLE] when the august

02:17:11.309 --> 02:17:13.000
advisor was addressed
Doctor Wiser,

02:17:13.000 --> 02:17:15.765
what a stern old
advisor is Wieser.

02:17:15.765 --> 02:17:18.410
[LAUGHTER]

02:17:18.410 --> 02:17:21.299
HALL: No, I can't place
him at the present.

02:17:21.299 --> 02:17:24.076
SPEAKER: Was he from MIT,
or he wasn't part of that?

02:17:24.575 --> 02:17:25.410
POUNDSTONE: [? Could ?]
[? be ?] NASA.

02:17:25.410 --> 02:17:26.809
BLAIR-SMITH: He
was from outside.

02:17:26.809 --> 02:17:29.705
And I forget where he worked.

02:17:29.705 --> 02:17:31.080
POUNDSTONE: He
was hired by NASA.

02:17:31.080 --> 02:17:32.580
BLAIR-SMITH: But
that was his point,

02:17:32.580 --> 02:17:36.360
to be an outside management,
project management consultant.

02:17:36.360 --> 02:17:39.629
HALL: What period of time?

02:17:39.629 --> 02:17:43.570
BLAIR-SMITH: Well,
I would say '65.

02:17:43.570 --> 02:17:45.160
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:17:54.570 --> 02:17:56.610
BLAIR-SMITH: Same boat.

02:17:56.610 --> 02:17:58.660
Same boat I've had
them for 27 years.

02:17:58.660 --> 02:18:03.510
SPEAKER: [INAUDIBLE]

02:18:03.510 --> 02:18:05.879
BLAIR-SMITH: Well, anyhow,
it's a 35-foot [INAUDIBLE]..

02:18:12.270 --> 02:18:14.070
POUNDSTONE: Since
Albert is not here,

02:18:14.070 --> 02:18:16.500
has he gone by the wayside?

02:18:16.500 --> 02:18:18.330
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:18:22.552 --> 02:18:23.510
POUNDSTONE: Doing what?

02:18:23.510 --> 02:18:24.385
HALL: Making pottery.

02:18:25.220 --> 02:18:26.760
POUNDSTONE: Is it
pottery, or pot?

02:18:29.430 --> 02:18:35.341
HANLEY: He does hand
painting, on the pottery.

02:18:35.341 --> 02:18:37.799
POUNDSTONE: And they can't get
him to come to these things?

02:18:37.799 --> 02:18:39.240
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:18:43.290 --> 02:18:46.160
POUNDSTONE: I didn't see
him on any of the first two

02:18:46.160 --> 02:18:47.265
conferences.

02:18:52.180 --> 02:18:53.780
HALL: I never [INAUDIBLE].

02:18:59.376 --> 02:19:02.040
He was probably too far away.

02:19:02.040 --> 02:19:04.040
And I didn't expect you to come.

02:19:04.040 --> 02:19:07.590
[INAUDIBLE]

02:19:07.590 --> 02:19:15.340
I had no idea that you
[INAUDIBLE] inbox in Tennessee.

02:19:15.340 --> 02:19:17.809
And he came all the way here?

02:19:17.809 --> 02:19:19.906
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:19:23.296 --> 02:19:24.879
BATES: Do you want
me to sign up here?

02:19:24.879 --> 02:19:25.320
BROWN: Please.

02:19:25.320 --> 02:19:26.160
BATES: Or down below?

02:19:26.160 --> 02:19:27.500
BROWN: Just sign at
the top and date it.

02:19:27.500 --> 02:19:29.374
BATES: Do you want me
to sign it and date it,

02:19:29.374 --> 02:19:31.450
or do you want me to print it?

02:19:31.450 --> 02:19:35.379
You won't even understand.

02:19:35.379 --> 02:19:38.870
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:21:05.980 --> 02:21:08.890
BATES: Anybody got any of those
coins we used to have for the--

02:21:08.890 --> 02:21:11.600
given away to all
the contractors

02:21:11.600 --> 02:21:14.980
for working on the
Apollo program?

02:21:14.980 --> 02:21:19.226
They had the coins supposedly
totally made up with material

02:21:19.226 --> 02:21:20.350
from one of the spacecraft.

02:21:20.350 --> 02:21:22.110
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:21:35.102 --> 02:21:37.060
BATES: The most was
interesting ones were the--

02:21:37.060 --> 02:21:40.210
remember the wheel
that they made up?

02:21:40.210 --> 02:21:43.859
The Apollo computer
was like a slide rule.

02:21:43.859 --> 02:21:44.650
SPEAKER: A mission.

02:21:44.650 --> 02:21:45.650
BATES: A mission--

02:21:45.650 --> 02:21:47.620
SPEAKER: Mission wheel.

02:21:47.620 --> 02:21:49.930
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:22:20.500 --> 02:22:23.926
POUNDSTONE: I was in
attendance that day.

02:22:23.926 --> 02:22:26.300
HALL: I don't remember you,
but I don't remember anybody.

02:22:26.800 --> 02:22:28.980
POUNDSTONE: Ralph was
the spokesman for MIT,

02:22:28.980 --> 02:22:31.040
you'll recall.

02:22:31.040 --> 02:22:34.390
And [INAUDIBLE] was a very
famous computer conference.

02:22:34.390 --> 02:22:35.500
I'll never forget.

02:22:35.500 --> 02:22:39.610
You guys [INAUDIBLE] document,
that nobody could possibly

02:22:39.610 --> 02:22:41.350
understand--

02:22:41.350 --> 02:22:42.990
by design.

02:22:42.990 --> 02:22:45.520
And here was this
room full of people

02:22:45.520 --> 02:22:47.725
in the auditorium
at Rice University--

02:22:47.725 --> 02:22:51.990
every major contractor
in the country was there.

02:22:51.990 --> 02:22:54.400
And they all would
stand up and ask

02:22:54.400 --> 02:22:57.320
questions [INAUDIBLE]
typical contractor,

02:22:57.320 --> 02:22:59.500
TRW or an IBM or somebody.

02:22:59.500 --> 02:23:03.640
He would stand up and take five
minutes to ask some question--

02:23:03.640 --> 02:23:07.990
in paragraph so-and-so, blah,
blah, blah, what about--

02:23:07.990 --> 02:23:10.530
And Ralph would stand
there and listen.

02:23:10.530 --> 02:23:13.770
And he'd say, the answer
to your question is, no.

02:23:13.770 --> 02:23:15.920
Next question.

02:23:15.920 --> 02:23:17.910
Or the answer to your
question is, yes.

02:23:17.910 --> 02:23:19.030
Next question.

02:23:19.030 --> 02:23:23.760
That's all he ever
said, yes, or no.

02:23:23.760 --> 02:23:25.982
So nobody learned anything.

02:23:25.982 --> 02:23:27.440
BATES: I thought
it was interesting

02:23:27.440 --> 02:23:28.689
from a contractual standpoint.

02:23:28.689 --> 02:23:32.860
The initial contract
was for $1.9 million.

02:23:32.860 --> 02:23:35.109
They went down to
renegotiate it,

02:23:35.109 --> 02:23:36.400
and go through the whole thing.

02:23:36.400 --> 02:23:37.765
There were a few changes.

02:23:37.765 --> 02:23:40.596
It came back around $24 million.

02:23:40.596 --> 02:23:41.880
I thought that was pretty good

02:23:41.880 --> 02:23:48.395
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:23:48.895 --> 02:23:49.930
POUNDSTONE: We don't
want to talk about that

02:23:49.930 --> 02:23:51.070
with Cline Frasier around.

02:23:51.070 --> 02:23:53.294
BATES: No.

02:23:53.294 --> 02:23:54.460
He wasn't there at the time.

02:23:54.460 --> 02:23:55.543
POUNDSTONE: No, he wasn't.

02:23:55.543 --> 02:24:35.094
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:24:35.094 --> 02:24:36.760
POUNDSTONE: Plus, the
one down there you

02:24:36.760 --> 02:24:40.120
should really have known was
Max [INAUDIBLE],, or his wife.

02:24:40.120 --> 02:24:41.680
HALL: I didn't know his wife.

02:24:41.680 --> 02:24:43.221
POUNDSTONE: You
didn't know his wife?

02:24:43.221 --> 02:24:44.510
SPEAKER: Everybody else did.

02:24:44.510 --> 02:24:46.550
POUNDSTONE: She was the
biggest blonde buxom

02:24:46.550 --> 02:24:47.770
thing you ever saw.

02:24:47.770 --> 02:24:52.060
And she wrote a newspaper
called the Clear Lake, whatever

02:24:52.060 --> 02:24:52.750
it was.

02:24:52.750 --> 02:24:55.374
And she used to go
to all these parties,

02:24:55.374 --> 02:24:56.832
and pick up all
the dirt and put it

02:24:56.832 --> 02:24:59.006
in this Clear Lake newspaper.

02:24:59.006 --> 02:25:01.600
And then she'd get
absolutely socko drunk.

02:25:01.600 --> 02:25:03.080
OK?

02:25:03.080 --> 02:25:05.890
And her husband would
leave her, and she'd

02:25:05.890 --> 02:25:07.519
find somebody to take her home.

02:25:07.519 --> 02:25:09.310
I don't think there
was any fooling around.

02:25:09.310 --> 02:25:10.934
Because I took her
home once, and there

02:25:10.934 --> 02:25:12.140
wasn't any fooling around.

02:25:12.140 --> 02:25:13.360
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:25:18.532 --> 02:25:19.740
POUNDSTONE: He finally went--

02:25:19.740 --> 02:25:22.170
he was doing the antique
business at the same time.

02:25:26.190 --> 02:25:30.225
No, like I said, the only
thing that saved that program

02:25:30.225 --> 02:25:35.640
is that we had probably about
3 years where they really

02:25:35.640 --> 02:25:36.960
didn't bother us that much.

02:25:41.020 --> 02:25:43.630
Those first two or three
years were very important

02:25:43.630 --> 02:25:44.304
[INAUDIBLE].

02:25:44.304 --> 02:25:45.970
HALL: They weren't
really organized yet.

02:25:45.970 --> 02:25:47.595
POUNDSTONE: No, they
weren't organized.

02:25:47.595 --> 02:25:50.820
So were able to do what had
to be done, to figure out

02:25:50.820 --> 02:25:53.020
what the hell we
was going to do.

02:25:53.020 --> 02:25:54.460
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:26:56.190 --> 02:26:57.930
POUNDSTONE: I used
to have these.

02:26:57.930 --> 02:27:00.480
I have no idea where
they were [INAUDIBLE]..

02:27:00.480 --> 02:28:01.555
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:28:01.555 --> 02:28:03.430
POUNDSTONE: Cline, did
you know Dave Gilbert?

02:28:03.430 --> 02:28:04.792
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:34:32.214 --> 02:34:33.880
MINDELL: We've got a
couple of things we

02:34:33.880 --> 02:34:37.600
want to cover this afternoon.

02:34:37.600 --> 02:34:40.870
And one thing we'd like
to do is to still get

02:34:40.870 --> 02:34:42.640
Sandy to be able
to demo you some

02:34:42.640 --> 02:34:46.090
of the stuff on the
comment system on the web,

02:34:46.090 --> 02:34:50.500
so that you can at least
see what it may look like.

02:34:50.500 --> 02:34:51.940
But that also takes
valuable time,

02:34:51.940 --> 02:34:53.620
that we won't have for talking.

02:34:53.620 --> 02:34:55.245
And while we've got
you here, we really

02:34:55.245 --> 02:34:58.750
want to hear about the history.

02:34:58.750 --> 02:35:00.880
There are a couple of
things that came up,

02:35:00.880 --> 02:35:03.505
that were overtly flagged
for interesting discussions.

02:35:03.505 --> 02:35:05.425
And we have some notes
for other things.

02:35:05.425 --> 02:35:07.341
And then, Sandy also had
some other questions,

02:35:07.341 --> 02:35:10.600
on larger stuff that
we're interested in.

02:35:10.600 --> 02:35:14.220
Maybe one way to start off the
discussion for the afternoon

02:35:14.220 --> 02:35:20.810
is our question, which is
simply, what to you guys

02:35:20.810 --> 02:35:22.570
are the really
significant things here,

02:35:22.570 --> 02:35:24.400
that we ought to be looking at?

02:35:24.400 --> 02:35:28.420
Either because they were
had a long-term influence,

02:35:28.420 --> 02:35:31.430
or because they
were something that

02:35:31.430 --> 02:35:36.240
was uniquely difficult-- problem
that was solved, in this case.

02:35:36.240 --> 02:35:38.420
[INAUDIBLE] were
mentioning the networks

02:35:38.420 --> 02:35:40.930
of people from continuing
on from other projects,

02:35:40.930 --> 02:35:43.090
and other engineering
cultures-- which is

02:35:43.090 --> 02:35:45.110
a really important part of it.

02:35:45.110 --> 02:35:49.210
So we want to get
a sense for what

02:35:49.210 --> 02:35:53.320
you guys, forty years
later, find important

02:35:53.320 --> 02:35:55.330
about this particular project.

02:35:55.330 --> 02:35:59.110
And what, if you were
reading a history of it,

02:35:59.110 --> 02:36:02.710
you'd think ought to at least
be mentioned, if not explored

02:36:02.710 --> 02:36:04.120
in [INAUDIBLE].

02:36:04.120 --> 02:36:06.190
CLINE: Could I pick
up what [INAUDIBLE]..

02:36:06.190 --> 02:36:07.600
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:36:11.890 --> 02:36:13.930
CLINE: This has to
do with the switch

02:36:13.930 --> 02:36:16.430
from signal layers in
the computer design

02:36:16.430 --> 02:36:18.190
to multilayered boards.

02:36:18.190 --> 02:36:21.760
And as I remember the story
goes something like this.

02:36:21.760 --> 02:36:25.370
Being at NASA, we were required
to come up and negotiate

02:36:25.370 --> 02:36:28.210
the contract with MIT,
or go through the budget

02:36:28.210 --> 02:36:31.570
and figure out what they would
be allowed to spend money on.

02:36:31.570 --> 02:36:34.570
And in the spring
of every year, it

02:36:34.570 --> 02:36:36.970
was, I was a part of
the team, and we're

02:36:36.970 --> 02:36:38.300
going through all this stuff.

02:36:38.300 --> 02:36:40.690
And one of the items
on one of the line

02:36:40.690 --> 02:36:43.570
items in the budget for a
considerable amount of money

02:36:43.570 --> 02:36:47.470
was to work on
multilayer boards.

02:36:47.470 --> 02:36:49.030
And this is something
that apparently

02:36:49.030 --> 02:36:51.280
been going on for a while.

02:36:51.280 --> 02:36:54.730
And it was at this stage
where the block-2 computer had

02:36:54.730 --> 02:36:56.830
gone through class A release.

02:36:56.830 --> 02:36:59.080
So it was known to work.

02:36:59.080 --> 02:37:03.110
The drawings were a
class A release system.

02:37:03.110 --> 02:37:06.030
And my question was, well,
since we already decided

02:37:06.030 --> 02:37:08.050
on using signal layers--

02:37:08.050 --> 02:37:12.270
signal layers were being
used in the electronic CDU--

02:37:12.270 --> 02:37:16.700
why are we bothering to spend
any money on multilayer boards?

02:37:16.700 --> 02:37:19.740
And I never got any kind
of a satisfactory answer.

02:37:19.740 --> 02:37:23.050
And being under pressure
to keep the budget down,

02:37:23.050 --> 02:37:25.270
that was a thing
that got lined out.

02:37:25.270 --> 02:37:28.590
So there was no money authorized
to spend on multilayer boards.

02:37:28.590 --> 02:37:30.861
BATES: That doesn't
stop you, though.

02:37:30.861 --> 02:37:32.110
CLINE: Just-- we'll get there.

02:37:35.487 --> 02:37:37.070
That was in the early
spring, I think.

02:37:37.070 --> 02:37:41.030
And then in August, I believe,
there was a meeting up here

02:37:41.030 --> 02:37:42.800
at MIT-- a review meeting.

02:37:42.800 --> 02:37:46.570
Hugh Brady was there, some
other people from AC--

02:37:46.570 --> 02:37:50.746
probably some Raytheon folks,
and certainly MIT folks.

02:37:50.746 --> 02:37:52.870
And one of the things that
came out at that meeting

02:37:52.870 --> 02:37:57.610
was, oh by the way, guys,
the computer won't work.

02:37:57.610 --> 02:38:01.480
And this was at a time
when we were supposed

02:38:01.480 --> 02:38:06.760
to deliver a flight-configured
computer to Grumman

02:38:06.760 --> 02:38:09.700
in December, to go on
the flight table for them

02:38:09.700 --> 02:38:12.260
to verify control-system stuff.

02:38:12.260 --> 02:38:16.200
And knowing how these things go,
if it wasn't delivered Grumman

02:38:16.200 --> 02:38:18.850
was going to then blame any
schedule slip on the guidance

02:38:18.850 --> 02:38:21.130
system.

02:38:21.130 --> 02:38:25.990
So the other thing that gets
said at the meeting was,

02:38:25.990 --> 02:38:28.390
well gee, we didn't
really stop working

02:38:28.390 --> 02:38:34.420
on those multilayer boards, and
the logic is all in the boards.

02:38:34.420 --> 02:38:40.750
And so at that point, we decided
to switch to multilayer boards,

02:38:40.750 --> 02:38:45.220
and to keep the signal
layers for the computer that

02:38:45.220 --> 02:38:50.500
went to Grumman, by doubling
up the solution in the signal

02:38:50.500 --> 02:38:54.940
layers-- which would
reduce the capacitance.

02:38:54.940 --> 02:39:00.790
And keeping the computer tray
open, because on a flight table

02:39:00.790 --> 02:39:04.210
it didn't matter whether
the tray was closed or not.

02:39:04.210 --> 02:39:06.820
So it was that
sequence of events--

02:39:06.820 --> 02:39:09.310
it doesn't conflict with
what you said, Dave,

02:39:09.310 --> 02:39:12.160
but that's kind of how it went.

02:39:12.160 --> 02:39:20.014
And the other piece of that was
that Dick Batten and the crew--

02:39:20.014 --> 02:39:21.430
Dick was convinced
that we'd never

02:39:21.430 --> 02:39:23.230
go to the moon with
a block-2 computer.

02:39:23.230 --> 02:39:25.810
It would be a block-3 system.

02:39:25.810 --> 02:39:27.940
So the next problem
was that we discovered

02:39:27.940 --> 02:39:30.130
that we could deliver
this open computer

02:39:30.130 --> 02:39:32.140
to Grumman in December,
along with the [? IMU ?]

02:39:32.140 --> 02:39:33.512
and everything.

02:39:33.512 --> 02:39:37.360
But oh by the way, there was no
software to turn the system on.

02:39:37.360 --> 02:39:41.860
And so I think I must have
gotten on my hands and knees

02:39:41.860 --> 02:39:45.350
to Dick Batten and said,
even if it's not going to go

02:39:45.350 --> 02:39:46.600
to the moon, will you please--

02:39:46.600 --> 02:39:49.030
and even if Grumman is not
going to be ready for it.

02:39:49.030 --> 02:39:51.194
Because MIT had spies at
Grumman, and they said

02:39:51.194 --> 02:39:53.110
they wouldn't even have
the wiring in the room

02:39:53.110 --> 02:39:55.240
until February.

02:39:55.240 --> 02:39:56.740
Will you please
turn out a program,

02:39:56.740 --> 02:39:58.340
so that we can just
turn the thing on,

02:39:58.340 --> 02:40:01.290
so we could say we delivered.

02:40:01.290 --> 02:40:02.000
it.

02:40:02.000 --> 02:40:04.885
And one of the things that's
done for me over the years--

02:40:04.885 --> 02:40:06.430
that and one other thing--

02:40:06.430 --> 02:40:09.647
is to remember
that you don't want

02:40:09.647 --> 02:40:11.980
to be too harsh about just
shutting some of these things

02:40:11.980 --> 02:40:15.890
off-- because they
save your tail.

02:40:15.890 --> 02:40:18.100
HANLEY: Yeah, but
Eldon had it backed up.

02:40:18.100 --> 02:40:18.800
[LAUGHTER]

02:40:18.800 --> 02:40:20.974
[INAUDIBLE]

02:40:20.974 --> 02:40:23.390
CLINE: I didn't know who paid
for those boards in that six

02:40:23.390 --> 02:40:26.035
months intervening
time, nor do I care.

02:40:26.035 --> 02:40:27.380
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:40:27.380 --> 02:40:29.040
BATES: I learned
that a long time ago.

02:40:29.040 --> 02:40:29.540
CLINE: Yeah.

02:40:29.540 --> 02:40:31.580
As I say, nor do I care.

02:40:34.852 --> 02:40:35.810
MINDELL: OK, thank you.

02:40:38.540 --> 02:40:41.060
Any other--

02:40:41.060 --> 02:40:42.650
BATES: What was
your question again?

02:40:42.650 --> 02:40:43.940
MINDELL: My question
is really about what

02:40:43.940 --> 02:40:45.030
are the major themes.

02:40:45.030 --> 02:40:48.490
What are the things that
really stand out as--

02:40:48.490 --> 02:40:50.030
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:40:50.030 --> 02:40:53.840
MINDELL: What really, in
the end, made it successful?

02:40:53.840 --> 02:40:56.360
Technologically, what were
the really significant things

02:40:56.360 --> 02:40:59.375
which are often things that
people don't really expect?

02:40:59.375 --> 02:41:02.480
TURNER: I think what Ed
Duggan and I are probably

02:41:02.480 --> 02:41:06.590
closer to not only is opposed to
the evolution of the continuity

02:41:06.590 --> 02:41:07.760
of the people--

02:41:07.760 --> 02:41:10.640
which we've already talked
about-- but the continuity

02:41:10.640 --> 02:41:16.670
of some of the basic aspects
of the design concepts,

02:41:16.670 --> 02:41:17.830
which started.

02:41:17.830 --> 02:41:21.337
And there was a
continuity there.

02:41:21.337 --> 02:41:22.670
MINDELL: From Polaris, you mean?

02:41:22.670 --> 02:41:23.630
TURNER: From Polaris.

02:41:23.630 --> 02:41:27.770
Even though it went
from discrete components

02:41:27.770 --> 02:41:30.140
to something totally
different, there

02:41:30.140 --> 02:41:32.030
were a lot of
similarities, and a lot

02:41:32.030 --> 02:41:34.680
learned from that evolution.

02:41:34.680 --> 02:41:38.960
And how to partition,
for example, logic.

02:41:38.960 --> 02:41:42.800
You can start reducing, for
example, the number of wires--

02:41:42.800 --> 02:41:44.990
whatever the wires are--

02:41:44.990 --> 02:41:52.970
by putting some thought into
functionally grouping gates,

02:41:52.970 --> 02:41:55.850
that talk to each other.

02:41:55.850 --> 02:42:02.302
And also, how big
is a separable item?

02:42:02.302 --> 02:42:09.200
In other words, that was the
first welded discrete component

02:42:09.200 --> 02:42:10.880
computer--

02:42:10.880 --> 02:42:12.736
was totally welded.

02:42:12.736 --> 02:42:14.110
I mean, the whole
shooting match,

02:42:14.110 --> 02:42:15.234
you couldn't take it apart.

02:42:15.234 --> 02:42:17.180
It was a book shape.

02:42:17.180 --> 02:42:18.650
And it did work.

02:42:19.447 --> 02:42:20.780
DUGGAN: You can replace modules.

02:42:20.780 --> 02:42:21.400
TURNER: That's right.

02:42:21.400 --> 02:42:23.000
Oh, could you do
that in the book?

02:42:23.000 --> 02:42:23.240
DUGGAN: No.

02:42:23.240 --> 02:42:23.910
TURNER: I don't know,
not in the book.

02:42:23.910 --> 02:42:24.860
DUGGAN: No, not in the book.

02:42:24.860 --> 02:42:25.360
TURNER: OK.

02:42:25.360 --> 02:42:28.090
But that was one extreme.

02:42:28.090 --> 02:42:31.700
Then what was
eventually the design,

02:42:31.700 --> 02:42:35.990
that went on through Polaris,
were replaceable modules.

02:42:35.990 --> 02:42:37.650
And you could replace them.

02:42:37.650 --> 02:42:41.810
Another thing which
was learned was,

02:42:41.810 --> 02:42:48.380
again, try to make
everything physically alike--

02:42:48.380 --> 02:42:50.960
even from the
individual components up

02:42:50.960 --> 02:42:53.660
to the modules themselves.

02:42:53.660 --> 02:42:59.460
So that when they hit a
production line, for example,

02:42:59.460 --> 02:43:02.720
whoever is working on them,
it doesn't matter what

02:43:02.720 --> 02:43:04.580
specifically they do.

02:43:04.580 --> 02:43:06.690
They all look alike.

02:43:06.690 --> 02:43:09.000
So that's one thing.

02:43:14.230 --> 02:43:17.760
Let's see, I'm
losing my train here,

02:43:17.760 --> 02:43:22.360
but I'm trying to
think of more examples.

02:43:22.360 --> 02:43:28.600
Oh yeah, another one
was being able to change

02:43:28.600 --> 02:43:32.410
the function of
look-alike modules,

02:43:32.410 --> 02:43:34.460
based on where
they're plugged in.

02:43:34.460 --> 02:43:38.620
In other words, essentially
do a pin-program type

02:43:38.620 --> 02:43:45.130
of organization, where you
then, because of back wiring,

02:43:45.130 --> 02:43:48.800
you make the same
basic functional module

02:43:48.800 --> 02:43:51.840
do different functions
in different places.

02:43:51.840 --> 02:43:55.900
And that, again, was something
learned from Polaris.

02:43:55.900 --> 02:43:59.280
The only one in Polaris
that did that was a memory.

02:43:59.280 --> 02:44:01.100
But [INAUDIBLE] was done at all.

02:44:01.100 --> 02:44:04.360
And that was one of the things
that was carried into Apollo.

02:44:04.360 --> 02:44:08.780
Because in that one it was
organized by bit, rather than--

02:44:08.780 --> 02:44:11.620
let's say you had adders in one
group, and somewhere like that.

02:44:11.620 --> 02:44:13.960
There was a module
called bit module,

02:44:13.960 --> 02:44:15.780
which there were 16 of them--

02:44:15.780 --> 02:44:17.720
or 14.

02:44:17.720 --> 02:44:20.100
And that was a good
way to split them up.

02:44:20.100 --> 02:44:23.260
HALL: No, there was eight,
actually, in Block 2,

02:44:23.260 --> 02:44:25.540
because there was
two in one module.

02:44:25.540 --> 02:44:26.380
TURNER: Yeah, OK.

02:44:26.380 --> 02:44:27.550
Yeah, I'd forgotten that.

02:44:27.550 --> 02:44:28.960
HALL: Block 1 had 16.

02:44:28.960 --> 02:44:30.043
TURNER: It doesn't matter.

02:44:30.043 --> 02:44:32.930
The basic idea was to
do something like that.

02:44:32.930 --> 02:44:34.040
HALL: Standard module.

02:44:34.040 --> 02:44:36.190
TURNER: Yeah, standard module.

02:44:36.190 --> 02:44:39.490
And of course that was an
advantage, not only physically,

02:44:39.490 --> 02:44:41.540
that they looked at that.

02:44:41.540 --> 02:44:44.330
So that when they hit
the lineup at Raytheon,

02:44:44.330 --> 02:44:48.560
they could be built with the
same tooling, same operators

02:44:48.560 --> 02:44:51.040
or similar ones.

02:44:51.040 --> 02:44:52.570
HALL: This is standardization.

02:44:52.570 --> 02:44:55.120
That was one of the
key issues that I

02:44:55.120 --> 02:44:59.440
used to present to Charles Frick
justifying integrated circuits.

02:44:59.440 --> 02:45:01.720
Because in the old
version everything

02:45:01.720 --> 02:45:05.200
was different, the core
transistor type, [INAUDIBLE]..

02:45:05.200 --> 02:45:07.900
There wasn't any
commonality at all.

02:45:07.900 --> 02:45:10.765
And there was no way of getting
it in that kind of circuit.

02:45:10.765 --> 02:45:13.647
With integrated circuits,
you could do [INAUDIBLE]..

02:45:13.647 --> 02:45:14.230
TURNER: Right.

02:45:14.230 --> 02:45:17.110
I mean, you change.

02:45:17.110 --> 02:45:21.030
Even though they were basically
three-input NOR gates,

02:45:21.030 --> 02:45:23.590
you could have a
fan in capability

02:45:23.590 --> 02:45:26.950
just by taking a bunch of
them together and opening up

02:45:26.950 --> 02:45:30.350
the cluster resistor, or
whatever was necessary.

02:45:30.350 --> 02:45:32.950
You could do that
with the back part.

02:45:32.950 --> 02:45:34.810
POUNDSTONE: I'd like
to address that.

02:45:34.810 --> 02:45:37.380
Obviously, the key
technology issue

02:45:37.380 --> 02:45:41.650
was the integrated circuits,
as everybody has said.

02:45:41.650 --> 02:45:43.350
If we had stuck with
core transistors

02:45:43.350 --> 02:45:47.290
it never would have fit, even
if you could make it work.

02:45:47.290 --> 02:45:49.485
But I think the
other key technology

02:45:49.485 --> 02:45:53.490
and the thing that made
the thing a success was

02:45:53.490 --> 02:45:56.020
Eldon's determination
that we were going

02:45:56.020 --> 02:45:58.960
to use a fixed
memory, which ended up

02:45:58.960 --> 02:46:00.850
being the so-called core rope.

02:46:03.366 --> 02:46:06.700
The industry was going out
to the other way at the time.

02:46:06.700 --> 02:46:09.500
This was a time when Bill
Gates was getting started.

02:46:09.500 --> 02:46:12.200
And, typically, programs
were stored in some sort

02:46:12.200 --> 02:46:14.710
of an erasable memory form.

02:46:14.710 --> 02:46:17.580
I know when I go around the
country and talk to people,

02:46:17.580 --> 02:46:20.410
and said we had this thing
called the core rope memory,

02:46:20.410 --> 02:46:21.280
they just laughed.

02:46:21.280 --> 02:46:24.610
And said, there is no
way that can be done.

02:46:24.610 --> 02:46:29.227
Because programmers will never
stop changing and, you know--

02:46:29.227 --> 02:46:30.310
SPEAKER: Oh yes they will.

02:46:32.950 --> 02:46:36.700
POUNDSTONE: The core rope
then had two features that

02:46:36.700 --> 02:46:38.890
were very good for the program.

02:46:38.890 --> 02:46:40.960
First, its ultra reliability.

02:46:40.960 --> 02:46:43.480
There's no way that any
erasable memory could ever

02:46:43.480 --> 02:46:46.240
be as reliable as a
thing called a core rope.

02:46:46.240 --> 02:46:47.620
And the second
was the discipline

02:46:47.620 --> 02:46:50.350
that it forced on
these software people.

02:46:50.350 --> 02:46:54.340
Because when they were
told that today is the day

02:46:54.340 --> 02:46:58.130
you release the program
for mission so and so--

02:46:58.130 --> 02:47:01.180
because you've got to send
those cards, or tapes,

02:47:01.180 --> 02:47:04.800
or whatever it was to Raytheon,
to make these physical things.

02:47:04.800 --> 02:47:07.210
It was called a program.

02:47:07.210 --> 02:47:09.000
And then that's
going to go down to--

02:47:09.000 --> 02:47:11.390
we integrated at the
tape, as I recall.

02:47:11.390 --> 02:47:13.570
HALL: You won't put
any change [INAUDIBLE]..

02:47:13.570 --> 02:47:15.320
POUNDSTONE: There will
be no more changes.

02:47:15.320 --> 02:47:17.380
MINDELL: The programs are
actually manufactured.

02:47:17.380 --> 02:47:18.421
POUNDSTONE: That's right.

02:47:18.421 --> 02:47:21.070
And that put some discipline
in the software people.

02:47:21.070 --> 02:47:23.277
At that time it was
just-- they never had it.

02:47:23.277 --> 02:47:24.360
SPEAKER: They still don't.

02:47:24.360 --> 02:47:27.250
POUNDSTONE: They don't today.

02:47:27.250 --> 02:47:30.310
Well, when you use PROMs,
you've got the same problems.

02:47:30.310 --> 02:47:31.790
But we didn't have PROMs.

02:47:31.790 --> 02:47:32.998
HANLEY: There was constraint.

02:47:32.998 --> 02:47:35.110
It was an intellectual anathema.

02:47:35.110 --> 02:47:36.860
They just didn't want
to hear this at all.

02:47:36.860 --> 02:47:37.440
POUNDSTONE: No, that's right.

02:47:37.440 --> 02:47:38.930
HALL: Even within Draper Labs.

02:47:38.930 --> 02:47:39.970
POUNDSTONE: Oh no,
most people at Draper

02:47:39.970 --> 02:47:40.930
didn't believe it was
going to work, either.

02:47:40.930 --> 02:47:42.220
HALL: I was fighting
with them all the time.

02:47:42.220 --> 02:47:43.594
POUNDSTONE: But
the outside world

02:47:43.594 --> 02:47:45.080
thought we were
absolutely crazy.

02:47:45.080 --> 02:47:47.000
And I will give
Eldon the credit.

02:47:47.000 --> 02:47:50.120
He withstood an awful lot
of criticism for that.

02:47:50.120 --> 02:47:53.630
DUGGAN: Yeah, but they'd spent
so much money by that time.

02:47:53.630 --> 02:47:57.190
FRASIER: I didn't know enough
to think one way or another

02:47:57.190 --> 02:47:59.660
at that time.

02:47:59.660 --> 02:48:01.759
Well, there was a time
I probably thought

02:48:01.759 --> 02:48:03.050
it was not the right way to go.

02:48:03.050 --> 02:48:05.080
And then there was a time
I really appreciated it.

02:48:05.080 --> 02:48:06.704
POUNDSTONE: But look
at the precedents.

02:48:06.704 --> 02:48:08.677
I mean, Minuteman I
had a drum, right?

02:48:08.677 --> 02:48:09.260
SPEAKER: Yeah.

02:48:09.260 --> 02:48:11.343
POUNDSTONE: And of course,
Polaris, we were wired.

02:48:11.343 --> 02:48:12.290
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:48:12.290 --> 02:48:15.170
BATES: Didn't Minuteman-III
have a [? drum ?]

02:48:15.170 --> 02:48:15.987
HALL: Not even II.

02:48:15.987 --> 02:48:18.320
POUNDSTONE: We had already
gone through this on Polaris,

02:48:18.320 --> 02:48:19.400
with a wired program.

02:48:19.400 --> 02:48:23.310
But you know, the program was
16 words, or something rather.

02:48:23.310 --> 02:48:25.790
I mean, it was
ridiculously small.

02:48:25.790 --> 02:48:29.000
HALL: 16 words of memory, yeah.

02:48:29.000 --> 02:48:31.370
Mark II was 12 words of memory.

02:48:31.370 --> 02:48:35.150
POUNDSTONE: But anyway, the idea
of having a large fixed memory

02:48:35.150 --> 02:48:37.410
was just unheard of.

02:48:37.410 --> 02:48:41.256
And I think that's what was one
of the keys to make the program

02:48:41.256 --> 02:48:41.755
a success.

02:48:41.755 --> 02:48:44.005
BATES: What was the total
amount of memory at the end?

02:48:44.005 --> 02:48:44.900
It was only, what?

02:48:44.900 --> 02:48:46.770
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:48:46.770 --> 02:48:47.869
BATES: It was 30,000?

02:48:47.869 --> 02:48:48.410
HALL: 36,000.

02:48:48.410 --> 02:48:49.839
BATES: 36,000.

02:48:49.839 --> 02:48:50.630
HALL: 16-bit words.

02:48:50.630 --> 02:48:52.880
BATES: Yeah, 16-bit words.

02:48:52.880 --> 02:48:57.910
HALL: I saw an interesting
cartoon a few years back.

02:48:57.910 --> 02:49:02.210
This kid was standing on the
descent stage of the lab,

02:49:02.210 --> 02:49:05.390
looking up at the ascent
stage-- probably in a museum.

02:49:05.390 --> 02:49:11.180
And he said, that computer
in there has 64 megs of RAM.

02:49:11.180 --> 02:49:13.615
That won't even run Windows 98.

02:49:13.615 --> 02:49:15.807
[LAUGHTER]

02:49:15.807 --> 02:49:16.890
DUGGAN: That's quite true.

02:49:16.890 --> 02:49:18.749
It probably saved the program.

02:49:18.749 --> 02:49:19.540
HALL: That's right.

02:49:19.540 --> 02:49:20.780
It saved the program.

02:49:20.780 --> 02:49:24.660
If it had had 64 megs of RAM,
we'd have never gotten there.

02:49:24.660 --> 02:49:26.480
MINDELL: How about management?

02:49:26.480 --> 02:49:29.960
I mean, was there a formal
practice of systems engineering

02:49:29.960 --> 02:49:30.840
going on?

02:49:30.840 --> 02:49:31.640
SPEAKER: Of what?

02:49:31.640 --> 02:49:32.848
MINDELL: Systems engineering.

02:49:32.848 --> 02:49:34.280
Or were you all [INAUDIBLE]--

02:49:34.280 --> 02:49:36.150
I mean, NASA was
very [INAUDIBLE]

02:49:36.150 --> 02:49:38.080
BLAIR-SMITH: No, NASA
didn't have one either.

02:49:38.080 --> 02:49:41.260
BATES: Let me just say
this-- coming from von Braun,

02:49:41.260 --> 02:49:44.270
with that team concept
that they had there,

02:49:44.270 --> 02:49:47.510
I saw the same
team concept here.

02:49:47.510 --> 02:49:51.050
When you saw the oneness
of purpose, of what

02:49:51.050 --> 02:49:52.370
these guys wanted to do--

02:49:52.370 --> 02:49:56.630
a team concept of the
people working together,

02:49:56.630 --> 02:49:58.726
to achieve that one goal.

02:49:58.726 --> 02:50:01.100
When you've been talking about
the technology-- and that,

02:50:01.100 --> 02:50:03.380
of course, is
extremely important.

02:50:03.380 --> 02:50:05.150
But unless they had--

02:50:05.150 --> 02:50:06.920
as loose as a
situation as they had

02:50:06.920 --> 02:50:09.320
so that they could have
a oneness of purpose

02:50:09.320 --> 02:50:13.820
and not being forced to
do something by the--

02:50:13.820 --> 02:50:18.200
they were successful
regardless of the obstacles.

02:50:18.200 --> 02:50:23.180
Which I considered management,
other people, contracts,

02:50:23.180 --> 02:50:25.070
other things of this nature say.

02:50:25.070 --> 02:50:28.590
They had a freedom to go ahead
and make these decisions,

02:50:28.590 --> 02:50:29.420
following through.

02:50:29.420 --> 02:50:30.850
Maybe you had a
lot of accidents,

02:50:30.850 --> 02:50:34.130
had a lot of failures,
but they had the freedom

02:50:34.130 --> 02:50:36.980
to go ahead and have
one purpose, and do it.

02:50:36.980 --> 02:50:39.710
And the team that
we had with Raytheon

02:50:39.710 --> 02:50:43.580
and with Instrumentation
Laboratory, at that time

02:50:43.580 --> 02:50:46.010
was second to none.

02:50:46.010 --> 02:50:48.610
POUNDSTONE: We were
discussing that over lunch

02:50:48.610 --> 02:50:50.060
as a matter of fact.

02:50:50.060 --> 02:50:54.800
Cline may not like to hear
this, but the facts are--

02:50:54.800 --> 02:50:55.816
FRASIER: Be careful.

02:50:55.816 --> 02:50:56.590
[LAUGHTER]

02:50:57.019 --> 02:50:59.060
BLAIR-SMITH: At the time
we started this program,

02:50:59.060 --> 02:51:03.290
NASA was in a state of
disorganization, if you like.

02:51:03.290 --> 02:51:06.267
They had just moved to Houston.

02:51:06.267 --> 02:51:07.850
MINDELL: They were
expanding like mad.

02:51:07.850 --> 02:51:09.141
POUNDSTONE: Expanding like mad.

02:51:09.141 --> 02:51:10.980
They were concentrating
on Gemini,

02:51:10.980 --> 02:51:13.700
and this was sort
of a side issue.

02:51:13.700 --> 02:51:15.939
And if that was the
most fortunate thing

02:51:15.939 --> 02:51:16.730
that ever happened.

02:51:16.730 --> 02:51:22.340
Because it gave us two or three
years for us to figure out what

02:51:22.340 --> 02:51:24.280
needed to be done.

02:51:24.280 --> 02:51:26.460
Let's say it was a system
engineering phase--

02:51:26.460 --> 02:51:29.400
although we didn't really
call it that in those days.

02:51:29.400 --> 02:51:31.850
And it wasn't formalized,
or anything like that.

02:51:31.850 --> 02:51:34.790
But it was sort of
a phase of trying

02:51:34.790 --> 02:51:37.260
to take a first
cut at the thing,

02:51:37.260 --> 02:51:40.280
and try to get it down to a
size you might be able to get

02:51:40.280 --> 02:51:42.110
your arms around, anyway.

02:51:42.110 --> 02:51:44.130
Later on, particularly
when Joe Shea

02:51:44.130 --> 02:51:47.360
became the program manager, then
it started to get more formal.

02:51:47.360 --> 02:51:52.094
And we started getting the
Cliff Duncans and Cline

02:51:52.094 --> 02:51:54.260
Frasiers and these people
who would come in and hold

02:51:54.260 --> 02:51:55.040
review meetings.

02:51:55.040 --> 02:51:58.904
And you know, they were
worried about money.

02:51:58.904 --> 02:52:00.820
In the beginning, we
didn't worry about money.

02:52:00.820 --> 02:52:02.750
MINDELL: Ultimately,
from your point of view--

02:52:02.750 --> 02:52:03.260
POUNDSTONE: I beg your pardon.

02:52:03.260 --> 02:52:05.593
MINDELL: Was the formalization
of the management program

02:52:05.593 --> 02:52:06.650
a good thing, ultimately?

02:52:06.650 --> 02:52:08.701
POUNDSTONE: Oh, eventually
it had to happen--

02:52:08.701 --> 02:52:09.200
eventually.

02:52:09.200 --> 02:52:11.060
BATES: But you see,
keeping pointing

02:52:11.060 --> 02:52:14.450
out that those first three
years of developing the team,

02:52:14.450 --> 02:52:16.520
and developing the purpose--

02:52:16.520 --> 02:52:19.470
regardless of all
these outside things--

02:52:19.470 --> 02:52:23.720
gave you the flexibility
to come up with a design,

02:52:23.720 --> 02:52:27.770
and with what was
necessary to do the job.

02:52:27.770 --> 02:52:29.030
Could you ever do that again?

02:52:29.030 --> 02:52:30.440
I don't think that
could ever happen again.

02:52:30.440 --> 02:52:32.690
HALL: When you're talking
about formalization, though,

02:52:32.690 --> 02:52:35.190
I don't think even near
the end of this program it

02:52:35.190 --> 02:52:37.360
wasn't formalized like
modern-day programs.

02:52:37.360 --> 02:52:37.640
POUNDSTONE: Oh, no.

02:52:37.640 --> 02:52:39.014
It was never
formalized that way.

02:52:39.014 --> 02:52:40.440
HALL: It was so much freer.

02:52:40.440 --> 02:52:42.540
POUNDSTONE: It was never
run like an Air Force--

02:52:42.540 --> 02:52:43.820
HALL: It was much
freer, all along.

02:52:43.820 --> 02:52:45.361
POUNDSTONE:
--system-management type.

02:52:45.712 --> 02:52:47.420
HANLEY: I'd like to
say a little bit more

02:52:47.420 --> 02:52:51.950
about standardization,
and components,

02:52:51.950 --> 02:52:55.910
integrated circuits,
diodes, resistance--

02:52:55.910 --> 02:52:58.100
there was standardization.

02:52:58.100 --> 02:53:02.540
And Eldon most probably
knows more than most

02:53:02.540 --> 02:53:06.710
how the design engineers
couldn't do it.

02:53:06.710 --> 02:53:09.590
You couldn't live
with just one gate.

02:53:09.590 --> 02:53:13.600
You couldn't live with
one PNP and one NPN.

02:53:13.600 --> 02:53:15.330
OK?

02:53:15.330 --> 02:53:19.930
And you needed more power
or put enough power.

02:53:19.930 --> 02:53:22.220
But Eldon, you
could say something

02:53:22.220 --> 02:53:24.915
on what it took to get
the design engineer--

02:53:24.915 --> 02:53:26.109
HALL: You're doing fine.

02:53:26.109 --> 02:53:26.650
HANLEY: What?

02:53:26.650 --> 02:53:28.300
HALL: You're doing fine.

02:53:28.300 --> 02:53:31.500
HANLEY: They just claimed
they couldn't do it.

02:53:31.500 --> 02:53:34.794
POUNDSTONE: I mean, today that's
all written in a big manual.

02:53:34.794 --> 02:53:37.210
That says, you're supposed to
have a parts standardization

02:53:37.210 --> 02:53:39.585
list, and you're supposed to
do this, and that, and that.

02:53:39.585 --> 02:53:42.020
Back in those days the
parts standardization list

02:53:42.020 --> 02:53:44.470
was in Eldon's hip pocket.

02:53:44.470 --> 02:53:46.730
And he said, here are the
parts you're going to use,

02:53:46.730 --> 02:53:48.520
and here are the resistor
values you're going to use.

02:53:48.520 --> 02:53:50.770
And here are the capacitor
values you're going to use.

02:53:50.770 --> 02:53:52.790
And if you can't do it
that way, go figure out

02:53:52.790 --> 02:53:54.360
how to do it anyway.

02:53:54.360 --> 02:53:55.780
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:53:58.090 --> 02:54:00.450
POUNDSTONE: The engineers
would come in to him--

02:54:00.450 --> 02:54:02.700
now, I got to have this,
and I got to have that.

02:54:02.700 --> 02:54:04.000
And he'd tell them, go away.

02:54:04.000 --> 02:54:06.550
He had to be tough,
and be stubborn.

02:54:06.550 --> 02:54:08.560
And he was.

02:54:08.560 --> 02:54:10.750
You know, a guy can
always figure out a way

02:54:10.750 --> 02:54:12.200
if you tell him enough times.

02:54:12.200 --> 02:54:14.590
But it took that
sort of discipline.

02:54:14.590 --> 02:54:17.150
But it wasn't formalized.

02:54:17.150 --> 02:54:18.820
As I said, it was
in his hip pocket.

02:54:18.820 --> 02:54:20.293
HALL: It wasn't easy.

02:54:20.293 --> 02:54:21.200
[LAUGHS]

02:54:21.200 --> 02:54:26.180
TURNER: I think one good
example of how things ran was--

02:54:26.180 --> 02:54:28.750
it was a funny example,
but I think it fits.

02:54:28.750 --> 02:54:31.120
And that is, I remember
hearing over the general page,

02:54:31.120 --> 02:54:36.280
in the Apollo building one day,
the regularly scheduled Tuesday

02:54:36.280 --> 02:54:39.600
NASA meeting has been postponed
from Wednesday to Thursday.

02:54:39.600 --> 02:54:41.755
[LAUGHTER]

02:54:45.290 --> 02:54:47.884
BATES: But I do remember
those Friday meetings.

02:54:47.884 --> 02:54:49.300
POUNDSTONE: There
was another area

02:54:49.300 --> 02:54:50.860
where I think we pioneered.

02:54:50.860 --> 02:54:52.730
It hasn't been talked about.

02:54:52.730 --> 02:54:58.620
But I don't believe any
program ever worried as much--

02:54:58.620 --> 02:55:01.270
maybe we weren't as effective--
about reliability was

02:55:01.270 --> 02:55:04.540
complementary as that program.

02:55:04.540 --> 02:55:06.264
HALL: That one was different.

02:55:06.264 --> 02:55:08.430
POUNDSTONE: We did an awful
lot that pioneered that.

02:55:08.430 --> 02:55:10.090
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:55:15.160 --> 02:55:18.790
HANLEY: I think one thing
I really appreciated,

02:55:18.790 --> 02:55:22.690
that after we got
the flight specs,

02:55:22.690 --> 02:55:28.310
I heard the Raytheon managers.

02:55:28.310 --> 02:55:35.473
And I'm arguing about which lot
of IC's was the best lot, which

02:55:35.473 --> 02:55:36.910
should be used in what machine.

02:55:36.910 --> 02:55:39.410
POUNDSTONE: That brings up
a story I meant to tell,

02:55:39.410 --> 02:55:40.587
and I forgot.

02:55:40.587 --> 02:55:41.920
I don't know if Eldon remembers.

02:55:41.920 --> 02:55:44.120
Well, you must remember this.

02:55:44.120 --> 02:55:46.750
The challenge, once
we went to IC's, was

02:55:46.750 --> 02:55:48.760
here was a brand-new
component being

02:55:48.760 --> 02:55:50.440
made by a brand-new industry.

02:55:50.440 --> 02:55:53.740
And the total number
that we really needed

02:55:53.740 --> 02:55:55.190
wasn't all that many.

02:55:55.190 --> 02:55:57.910
We were really going to
build, what, 30 computers,

02:55:57.910 --> 02:55:58.880
or something rather.

02:55:58.880 --> 02:56:00.070
It really wasn't that many.

02:56:00.070 --> 02:56:00.986
To the semiconductor--

02:56:00.986 --> 02:56:03.390
HALL: Back to the beginning,
it was much less than that.

02:56:03.390 --> 02:56:04.880
POUNDSTONE: Yeah.

02:56:04.880 --> 02:56:08.470
So how do you get reliability
of a brand-new component, when

02:56:08.470 --> 02:56:10.730
you're not going
to build very many?

02:56:10.730 --> 02:56:11.769
So one of the ideas--

02:56:11.769 --> 02:56:13.810
I don't remember whether
it was Eldon's or mine--

02:56:13.810 --> 02:56:16.360
but I'm sure NASA
would have choked if we

02:56:16.360 --> 02:56:18.070
had to get them to bless it.

02:56:18.070 --> 02:56:20.320
We decided that in the
ground-support equipment--

02:56:20.320 --> 02:56:23.140
just test equipment we were
building for the factory--

02:56:23.140 --> 02:56:25.630
instead of building it out
of resistors and transistors,

02:56:25.630 --> 02:56:29.440
we built it out of the absolute
same integrated circuit.

02:56:29.440 --> 02:56:32.150
And then when we got a lot
in, and we tested the lot,

02:56:32.150 --> 02:56:34.480
if it was a little
bit shaky put it

02:56:34.480 --> 02:56:36.150
in the ground-support equipment.

02:56:36.150 --> 02:56:38.090
Take the good ones
and [INAUDIBLE]..

02:56:38.090 --> 02:56:40.030
DUGGAN: Didn't the
captive line concept

02:56:40.030 --> 02:56:41.900
evolve out of this idea?

02:56:41.900 --> 02:56:45.720
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:56:45.720 --> 02:56:49.480
HALL: Block 1 Polaris
had the captive line.

02:56:49.480 --> 02:56:53.660
And the fire control
used the same transistors

02:56:53.660 --> 02:56:56.740
as we chose for the
guidance computer.

02:56:56.740 --> 02:56:58.540
That was dictated by Del Cole.

02:56:58.540 --> 02:57:01.350
DUGGAN: That was a fairly
innovative step, I think.

02:57:01.350 --> 02:57:04.000
BATES: I think one of
the things that amplifies

02:57:04.000 --> 02:57:06.932
what they have just said
is with the core ropes.

02:57:06.932 --> 02:57:08.890
I don't know whether
anybody can remember this,

02:57:08.890 --> 02:57:11.110
but I was involved [INAUDIBLE].

02:57:11.110 --> 02:57:13.510
We had a situation where
one of the core ropes

02:57:13.510 --> 02:57:16.400
passed its acceptance.

02:57:16.400 --> 02:57:20.170
And there was no problem
with it, and it was rejected.

02:57:20.170 --> 02:57:24.190
And so I went down to see one
of these little old ladies,

02:57:24.190 --> 02:57:27.370
and said, hey, you know
it passed everything.

02:57:27.370 --> 02:57:32.500
That cost $75,000, and
you've scrapped the thing.

02:57:32.500 --> 02:57:34.670
I mean, why can't we use it?

02:57:34.670 --> 02:57:36.430
Well, what NASA
did is they brought

02:57:36.430 --> 02:57:40.330
in a lot of the astronauts
to go through the plants.

02:57:40.330 --> 02:57:43.300
Well, the astronauts
became the sons

02:57:43.300 --> 02:57:45.407
of these little old ladies.

02:57:45.407 --> 02:57:46.240
BRISS: That's right.

02:57:46.240 --> 02:57:48.040
BATES: And so this
little old lady

02:57:48.040 --> 02:57:51.670
looks up at me with this
face, and she says, you know,

02:57:51.670 --> 02:57:54.280
I built that and it passed.

02:57:54.280 --> 02:57:56.890
But I don't think it's too good.

02:57:56.890 --> 02:57:59.200
So you wouldn't want
me to pass something

02:57:59.200 --> 02:58:04.214
that I thought wasn't too good,
to pass on to one of our boys.

02:58:04.214 --> 02:58:06.414
[BLOWS RASPBERRY]
It got scrapped.

02:58:06.414 --> 02:58:07.580
BRISS: That's what they did.

02:58:07.580 --> 02:58:11.900
BATES: So this type of
philosophy was there.

02:58:11.900 --> 02:58:13.779
It was always the
man in the loop,

02:58:13.779 --> 02:58:15.320
that somebody is
going to be up there

02:58:15.320 --> 02:58:16.700
having to count on this thing.

02:58:16.700 --> 02:58:18.127
This isn't a missile
that we fire,

02:58:18.127 --> 02:58:20.210
and maybe it [BLOWS RASPBERRY]
goes up in the sky.

02:58:20.210 --> 02:58:21.560
You're talking about people.

02:58:21.560 --> 02:58:23.210
And NASA was smart.

02:58:23.210 --> 02:58:25.760
They took these astronauts,
and they brought them around.

02:58:25.760 --> 02:58:28.220
Not to meet just
management-- no.

02:58:28.220 --> 02:58:30.270
They brought them
down on the floor,

02:58:30.270 --> 02:58:33.860
to meet everybody
that was on the floor.

02:58:33.860 --> 02:58:37.141
And as I say, the other engineer
and the rest of it, that

02:58:37.141 --> 02:58:37.640
was fine.

02:58:37.640 --> 02:58:40.130
But these little old
ladies, let me tell you,

02:58:40.130 --> 02:58:42.740
they adopted every
one of those people--

02:58:42.740 --> 02:58:44.210
the astronauts.

02:58:44.210 --> 02:58:45.920
Maybe you know better than I do.

02:58:45.920 --> 02:58:47.502
BRISS: Absolutely right.

02:58:47.502 --> 02:58:49.460
And they were brought in
under the guise, well,

02:58:49.460 --> 02:58:51.772
you want to see how the
components are made.

02:58:51.772 --> 02:58:54.230
And they talked to the ladies
like they were their mothers.

02:58:54.230 --> 02:58:56.520
BATES: That's right, yeah.

02:58:56.520 --> 02:58:58.160
And what do you
think about this?

02:58:58.160 --> 02:59:01.040
And you know, how do you think
this thing is going together?

02:59:01.040 --> 02:59:03.140
And you know, they were
very much interested

02:59:03.140 --> 02:59:06.800
in every process that these
little old ladies were doing.

02:59:06.800 --> 02:59:09.230
BRISS: If they had a
suggestion-- as I mentioned,

02:59:09.230 --> 02:59:10.950
I was a floor engineer--

02:59:10.950 --> 02:59:12.530
you had to listen.

02:59:12.530 --> 02:59:16.170
And you get two or
three of them together,

02:59:16.170 --> 02:59:19.400
and had some general
agreement that maybe we

02:59:19.400 --> 02:59:20.960
should change this.

02:59:20.960 --> 02:59:23.550
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:59:23.550 --> 02:59:25.321
BRISS: No, you don't
ask the astronauts.

02:59:25.321 --> 02:59:27.780
[LAUGHTER]

02:59:27.780 --> 02:59:29.240
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:59:29.240 --> 02:59:31.040
BRISS: They have a
different agenda.

02:59:31.040 --> 02:59:33.150
MINDELL: If any of you
know, or have any leads,

02:59:33.150 --> 02:59:34.580
we would love to find
some of these women

02:59:34.580 --> 02:59:36.121
to talk to-- if
they're still around.

02:59:36.121 --> 02:59:38.190
BATES: Well, Ed was
saying that he knew.

02:59:38.190 --> 02:59:41.180
BLONDIN: I knew the
first-level supervisors.

02:59:41.180 --> 02:59:42.170
I know their names.

02:59:42.170 --> 02:59:46.500
And they all lived in and
around the Waltham area.

02:59:46.500 --> 02:59:50.424
Waltham was a great Italian
enclave at the time.

02:59:50.424 --> 02:59:51.590
They all have Italian names.

02:59:51.590 --> 02:59:54.010
It was Kitty Cicardo.

02:59:54.010 --> 02:59:57.017
POUNDSTONE: Well, she may know
if there's any of them alive.

02:59:57.017 --> 02:59:57.600
BLONDIN: Yeah.

02:59:57.600 --> 02:59:58.850
Eleanor Capadona.

02:59:58.850 --> 03:00:01.706
POUNDSTONE: I remember her.

03:00:01.706 --> 03:00:04.130
BLONDIN: Let's see,
I'll have her name

03:00:04.130 --> 03:00:07.930
in a second I don't have
the same memory I had 52--

03:00:07.930 --> 03:00:08.620
when I was 52.

03:00:08.620 --> 03:00:10.330
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:00:14.414 --> 03:00:16.330
MINDELL: I'm curious to
talk a little bit more

03:00:16.330 --> 03:00:18.880
about the part of the floor
that you have the pictures of.

03:00:18.880 --> 03:00:21.460
How many women would be
working on the Apollo program

03:00:21.460 --> 03:00:22.330
at any one point?

03:00:22.330 --> 03:00:24.730
And how big was this area?

03:00:24.730 --> 03:00:30.440
BLONDIN: Probably on the
ropes, up to 12 on a shift.

03:00:33.220 --> 03:00:34.720
POUNDSTONE: It
wasn't a huge number.

03:00:34.720 --> 03:00:39.862
BLONDIN: In the
working welding modules

03:00:39.862 --> 03:00:42.594
there could have been 30,
but they were scattered.

03:00:42.594 --> 03:00:44.510
BATES: Was that in
Sudbury, or was it Waltham?

03:00:44.510 --> 03:00:46.070
POUNDSTONE: I think
that's Sudbury.

03:00:46.070 --> 03:00:47.280
BATES: That's Sudbury.

03:00:47.280 --> 03:00:49.430
HANLEY: They didn't
only do that.

03:00:49.430 --> 03:00:53.040
When the people were making
IC's and other parts,

03:00:53.040 --> 03:00:57.400
we made sure the same
astronauts would go visit them.

03:00:57.400 --> 03:00:59.370
HALL: The astronauts
visited lots of places.

03:00:59.370 --> 03:01:05.510
BLONDIN: We might have had half
a dozen or so in [INAUDIBLE]..

03:01:05.510 --> 03:01:09.710
We had a special little group,
which was module repair,

03:01:09.710 --> 03:01:12.470
in a little room by themselves.

03:01:12.470 --> 03:01:16.976
There was a lady named
Mary Tangey who ran that.

03:01:16.976 --> 03:01:20.930
And these potted modules,
you would test them

03:01:20.930 --> 03:01:22.070
before you potted them.

03:01:22.070 --> 03:01:25.470
And then you'd test them
afterwards-- post-product test.

03:01:25.470 --> 03:01:30.020
And quite often they would fail,
because the potting stretched

03:01:30.020 --> 03:01:31.910
them.

03:01:31.910 --> 03:01:35.120
And then, what would you do
with this thing, that you

03:01:35.120 --> 03:01:37.320
had invested all this
time and money in--

03:01:37.320 --> 03:01:41.330
and all these components
that were so hard to come by?

03:01:41.330 --> 03:01:44.460
So they would send them
to Mary, and Mary's girls.

03:01:44.460 --> 03:01:46.980
There might have been,
like, three of them.

03:01:46.980 --> 03:01:48.830
And they used these demo tools.

03:01:48.830 --> 03:01:54.140
And they would pick
away the foam pot,

03:01:54.140 --> 03:01:59.720
until they exposed components.

03:01:59.720 --> 03:02:04.540
They would get directions
from the test engineers,

03:02:04.540 --> 03:02:07.450
who would guess--

03:02:07.450 --> 03:02:09.020
better than a guess--

03:02:09.020 --> 03:02:12.650
which component
had probably died.

03:02:12.650 --> 03:02:15.170
And they would expose it,
and then send it back.

03:02:15.170 --> 03:02:18.890
And the test people
would verify that.

03:02:18.890 --> 03:02:22.130
And then they would take
that component out--

03:02:22.130 --> 03:02:24.320
it's a difficult thing,
it's welded in there

03:02:24.320 --> 03:02:26.450
with strips of ribbon--

03:02:26.450 --> 03:02:28.250
and put a new one in--

03:02:28.250 --> 03:02:31.820
which makes you have to
weld ribbons together.

03:02:35.000 --> 03:02:39.920
And we'd test it, and
send it back for potting.

03:02:39.920 --> 03:02:44.150
And, mostly, they passed
the second time around.

03:02:44.150 --> 03:02:46.810
I referred to the
fact earlier that

03:02:46.810 --> 03:02:49.670
when I was put in
charge of Apollo

03:02:49.670 --> 03:02:53.670
in Milwaukee that we
were behind schedule.

03:02:53.670 --> 03:02:56.720
So one of the first things I
did was climb on an airplane

03:02:56.720 --> 03:02:59.780
and come out to
Raytheon, to figure out

03:02:59.780 --> 03:03:02.480
if they were doing anything
different than we were--

03:03:02.480 --> 03:03:05.300
because it was on
the same program.

03:03:05.300 --> 03:03:07.820
In those days there
was always people

03:03:07.820 --> 03:03:12.170
around who would say the
contract says, or NASA says.

03:03:12.170 --> 03:03:14.400
And you could never verify it.

03:03:14.400 --> 03:03:18.980
So I figured if I got
out and walked along

03:03:18.980 --> 03:03:22.730
the floor in Raytheon,
I could talk to people

03:03:22.730 --> 03:03:24.280
who were really doing things.

03:03:24.280 --> 03:03:27.800
And they showed me--

03:03:27.800 --> 03:03:30.380
what do you do when
these things fail?

03:03:30.380 --> 03:03:32.070
Well, we send them over to Mary.

03:03:32.070 --> 03:03:35.060
Well, I get back to
Milwaukee, and I found out

03:03:35.060 --> 03:03:39.560
that somebody at AC
had said, no, no, no,

03:03:39.560 --> 03:03:43.040
that can't be done without
ruining the module.

03:03:43.040 --> 03:03:46.150
Throw them away.

03:03:46.150 --> 03:03:48.480
And so we had--

03:03:48.480 --> 03:03:50.930
large quantities of
these things were

03:03:50.930 --> 03:03:54.470
hard to get
components, heat sinks.

03:03:54.470 --> 03:03:58.850
All this invested in, and they
were going in the trash can.

03:03:58.850 --> 03:04:04.640
And I came back, and I
said, aha, same spacecraft,

03:04:04.640 --> 03:04:11.180
same contractor, they're
doing it in Waltham Well,

03:04:11.180 --> 03:04:14.030
our guys had to come up
with improvement on that.

03:04:14.030 --> 03:04:16.770
Instead of the
dental picks, they

03:04:16.770 --> 03:04:21.615
went out and got a machine that
a dentist uses to abrade teeth.

03:04:21.615 --> 03:04:25.722
It was called a SS
White, I think, machine.

03:04:25.722 --> 03:04:31.190
And it would sandblast
ground up walnut shells.

03:04:31.190 --> 03:04:33.910
BATES: We tried that.

03:04:33.910 --> 03:04:34.690
We did that first.

03:04:38.140 --> 03:04:39.700
BLONDIN: That worked fine at AC.

03:04:42.227 --> 03:04:44.810
POUNDSTONE: Didn't that generate
a static charge, as I recall?

03:04:44.810 --> 03:04:45.080
BLONDIN: Yeah.

03:04:45.080 --> 03:04:46.520
I said, put it in at Raytheon.

03:04:46.520 --> 03:04:47.880
So we did.

03:04:47.880 --> 03:04:51.200
And with the integrated
circuits in the Raytheon module,

03:04:51.200 --> 03:04:55.520
we were generating a static
charge, and zapping the IC's.

03:04:55.520 --> 03:05:00.870
And Raytheon took great
pleasure in telling the AC,

03:05:00.870 --> 03:05:02.463
you're not so smart.

03:05:02.463 --> 03:05:03.450
[LAUGHTER]

03:05:03.450 --> 03:05:06.440
This thing you made us
do is blowing our IC's.

03:05:06.440 --> 03:05:11.430
So it was kind of an
interesting little story on--

03:05:14.140 --> 03:05:16.480
we couldn't get on schedule,
because we were throwing

03:05:16.480 --> 03:05:17.990
all these modules away.

03:05:17.990 --> 03:05:20.860
And Raytheon was--
I mean, this was not

03:05:20.860 --> 03:05:23.530
advanced technology--
dental picks.

03:05:23.530 --> 03:05:29.420
And things that would
dissolve the potting compound.

03:05:29.420 --> 03:05:31.060
People were saying,
what effect is

03:05:31.060 --> 03:05:33.320
that going to have on the
components-- that solvent?

03:05:36.320 --> 03:05:41.567
How Raytheon got away with
it, nobody at General Motors--

03:05:41.567 --> 03:05:43.150
POUNDSTONE: They
probably wrote an OD.

03:05:43.150 --> 03:05:43.990
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:05:43.990 --> 03:05:47.350
BRISS: Initially, we used a
very small soldering iron,

03:05:47.350 --> 03:05:49.960
to melt the foam.

03:05:49.960 --> 03:05:53.370
BLONDIN: Or [INAUDIBLE] you
were using a hot soldering iron.

03:05:53.370 --> 03:05:55.941
BRISS: Well, let me say--

03:05:55.941 --> 03:05:57.190
and that didn't work too well.

03:05:57.190 --> 03:06:00.960
And the ladies decided
that they'd just pick away.

03:06:00.960 --> 03:06:03.730
And the ladies decided that.

03:06:03.730 --> 03:06:04.680
I remember that.

03:06:04.680 --> 03:06:07.360
BATES: They did a
lot of deciding.

03:06:07.360 --> 03:06:08.740
POUNDSTONE: Yeah, they did.

03:06:08.740 --> 03:06:12.500
BLONDIN: That got
us back on schedule.

03:06:12.500 --> 03:06:14.922
That and a lot of other things.

03:06:14.922 --> 03:06:16.380
POUNDSTONE: In
fact, I think Battin

03:06:16.380 --> 03:06:19.170
wrote A Funny Thing Happened
on the Way to the Moon.

03:06:19.170 --> 03:06:20.950
And one of the
references in there

03:06:20.950 --> 03:06:25.957
is to the independence of the
little old ladies in Waltham.

03:06:25.957 --> 03:06:28.290
MINDELL: What other kinds of
things did they contribute?

03:06:28.290 --> 03:06:31.520
Do you remember other examples?

03:06:31.520 --> 03:06:34.420
BRISS: Well, I don't want it
contributed to the program,

03:06:34.420 --> 03:06:36.850
but they were
high-seniority ladies.

03:06:36.850 --> 03:06:39.100
They knew what they were doing.

03:06:39.100 --> 03:06:41.470
They worked
[? probably ?] on it.

03:06:41.470 --> 03:06:43.889
I mean, believe me, they were
old enough to be my mother,

03:06:43.889 --> 03:06:44.930
and I was in my 30s then.

03:06:44.930 --> 03:06:48.350
And they just felt confident
they knew what they were doing.

03:06:48.350 --> 03:06:51.160
They worked on World
War II microwave stuff.

03:06:51.160 --> 03:06:53.650
They worked on
the tool division.

03:06:53.650 --> 03:06:55.792
They knew what they were doing.

03:06:55.792 --> 03:06:57.250
MINDELL: They
weren't watch makers.

03:06:57.250 --> 03:06:58.000
They were making--

03:06:58.000 --> 03:06:59.770
BRISS: Not the ones in my group.

03:06:59.770 --> 03:07:00.950
Not the ones in my group.

03:07:00.950 --> 03:07:02.812
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:07:02.812 --> 03:07:04.520
DUGGAN: Like the
Westinghouse experience,

03:07:04.520 --> 03:07:07.420
that they go back to in the
industrial engineering field--

03:07:07.420 --> 03:07:09.660
where they told how
they lowered the lights.

03:07:09.660 --> 03:07:13.012
And Westinghouse did a famous
experiment [INAUDIBLE]..

03:07:13.012 --> 03:07:14.470
And the more they
dimmed them down,

03:07:14.470 --> 03:07:17.110
the more output came out,
even though they couldn't see

03:07:17.110 --> 03:07:18.475
what the hell they were doing.

03:07:18.475 --> 03:07:19.480
[LAUGHTER]

03:07:19.480 --> 03:07:21.154
And it was basically
the attention

03:07:21.154 --> 03:07:22.570
they were getting
from management.

03:07:22.570 --> 03:07:29.620
And here you are on a highly
visible and patriotic almost

03:07:29.620 --> 03:07:32.970
crusade to get to the moon
before the Russians did.

03:07:32.970 --> 03:07:35.560
BRISS: Well, the program
then proceeded to be--

03:07:35.560 --> 03:07:36.530
and I certainly did--

03:07:36.530 --> 03:07:37.780
the cream-of-the-crop program.

03:07:37.780 --> 03:07:39.238
You guys are talking
about Polaris.

03:07:39.238 --> 03:07:40.200
It wasn't known then.

03:07:40.200 --> 03:07:42.630
It was a program
or two before that.

03:07:42.630 --> 03:07:44.170
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:07:48.760 --> 03:07:50.400
DUGGAN: There was
that national focus,

03:07:50.400 --> 03:07:53.680
that kind of coalesced
things, I think.

03:07:53.680 --> 03:07:57.310
POUNDSTONE: We had this
policy that the girls who

03:07:57.310 --> 03:07:59.320
worked on ropes, that's
all they could do,

03:07:59.320 --> 03:08:01.516
because they got good at ropes.

03:08:01.516 --> 03:08:02.890
And we didn't want
them to go off

03:08:02.890 --> 03:08:04.330
doing a bunch of
other things and not

03:08:04.330 --> 03:08:06.080
be able to get them
when you needed ropes.

03:08:06.080 --> 03:08:08.890
I remember when we got to
that phase of the program

03:08:08.890 --> 03:08:14.020
when you only had three or four
weeks to make a set of ropes,

03:08:14.020 --> 03:08:18.070
I think we had about 12 rope
machines, or something like 10.

03:08:18.070 --> 03:08:19.360
I forget how many.

03:08:19.360 --> 03:08:22.120
But as I recall,
we paid those women

03:08:22.120 --> 03:08:28.090
to sit there and wait until
the deck of cards or the tape

03:08:28.090 --> 03:08:28.660
came out.

03:08:28.660 --> 03:08:31.270
And they would be sitting
there knitting for two or three

03:08:31.270 --> 03:08:32.320
weeks.

03:08:32.320 --> 03:08:34.240
And then the deck of
cards would come out,

03:08:34.240 --> 03:08:38.290
or the program would come
out, and they'd go like hell.

03:08:38.290 --> 03:08:40.210
HANLEY: Could I just
interrupt a minute?

03:08:40.210 --> 03:08:43.030
Because I'm on that subject.

03:08:43.030 --> 03:08:50.440
You remember when they found
a software problem at the very

03:08:50.440 --> 03:08:53.920
last minute, and they
had to redo a rope?

03:08:53.920 --> 03:08:55.140
Maybe you'll remember, too.

03:08:55.140 --> 03:08:56.390
SPEAKER: I do.

03:08:56.390 --> 03:08:57.725
HANLEY: And NASA went--

03:08:57.725 --> 03:08:59.600
POUNDSTONE: I was
probably gone [INAUDIBLE]..

03:08:59.600 --> 03:09:02.380
HANLEY: --trying to find out
how long it will take to redo

03:09:02.380 --> 03:09:04.540
the ropes, and so forth.

03:09:04.540 --> 03:09:08.740
And every rope you ever had had
so many failures afterwards.

03:09:08.740 --> 03:09:12.100
And then it went into
rework, and so forth.

03:09:12.100 --> 03:09:15.920
I want somebody who knows
the story better than I do.

03:09:15.920 --> 03:09:17.100
SPEAKER: Maybe Cline does.

03:09:17.100 --> 03:09:17.740
FRASIER: I'm not sure.

03:09:17.740 --> 03:09:18.400
Go ahead.

03:09:18.400 --> 03:09:19.940
HANLEY: You remember?

03:09:19.940 --> 03:09:23.260
FRASIER: I remember a
time or two where the--

03:09:23.260 --> 03:09:25.210
HANLEY: This was only one time.

03:09:25.210 --> 03:09:27.040
FRASIER: --things
were really late.

03:09:27.040 --> 03:09:29.380
HANLEY: Very, very late.

03:09:29.380 --> 03:09:32.320
BLONDIN: I remember
that very well.

03:09:32.320 --> 03:09:35.650
I was running a manufacturing on
an Apollo program at the time,

03:09:35.650 --> 03:09:37.120
at Waltham.

03:09:37.120 --> 03:09:39.880
And for some reason
or other the ropes

03:09:39.880 --> 03:09:44.865
that had already been made
had to be reprogrammed.

03:09:44.865 --> 03:09:46.240
POUNDSTONE: Software
glitch, huh?

03:09:46.240 --> 03:09:49.120
BLONDIN: There was something
that had some reason--

03:09:49.120 --> 03:09:50.915
and I don't know what it was.

03:09:50.915 --> 03:09:56.650
And we were down to normal
cycle on those things was--

03:09:56.650 --> 03:09:59.837
with all the rework, it
was like eight weeks,

03:09:59.837 --> 03:10:00.670
something like that.

03:10:00.670 --> 03:10:00.925
HANLEY: Right.

03:10:00.925 --> 03:10:02.030
Something of that order.

03:10:02.030 --> 03:10:04.450
BLONDIN: And these
ropes were required out

03:10:04.450 --> 03:10:06.160
in less than three.

03:10:06.160 --> 03:10:07.720
I remember that.

03:10:07.720 --> 03:10:11.320
But none of them failed.

03:10:11.320 --> 03:10:14.000
HANLEY: That's the point I
want to make for these fellows.

03:10:14.000 --> 03:10:15.208
BLONDIN: None of them failed.

03:10:15.208 --> 03:10:18.740
HANLEY: Those ropes were
fabricated on the first time,

03:10:18.740 --> 03:10:20.030
and there were no failures.

03:10:20.030 --> 03:10:22.990
And that's the first time
they ever had no failures.

03:10:22.990 --> 03:10:24.950
BLONDIN: They just
paid more attention.

03:10:24.950 --> 03:10:29.480
HANLEY: And the gals
used to fight and argue--

03:10:29.480 --> 03:10:32.950
OK, you've had it
for so many hours.

03:10:32.950 --> 03:10:37.630
And this sort of
emphasizes the fact

03:10:37.630 --> 03:10:43.770
that people do identify with
it, and that it really works.

03:10:43.770 --> 03:10:46.570
And I thought you
guys would remember.

03:10:46.570 --> 03:10:49.240
POUNDSTONE: I think
it was after I left.

03:10:49.240 --> 03:10:51.250
BLONDIN: I remember
it very well.

03:10:51.250 --> 03:10:53.930
Nobody would believe
it could be done.

03:10:53.930 --> 03:10:55.690
We had all kinds of people--

03:10:55.690 --> 03:10:57.520
I remember production
control saying, well,

03:10:57.520 --> 03:11:02.950
we'll put up charts that show
what the schedule points are

03:11:02.950 --> 03:11:04.000
[INAUDIBLE].

03:11:06.740 --> 03:11:10.610
And they happily
raced through that.

03:11:10.610 --> 03:11:12.350
HANLEY: And they
did it perfectly--

03:11:12.350 --> 03:11:12.980
BLONDIN: They did perfectly.

03:11:12.980 --> 03:11:14.021
HANLEY: --the first time.

03:11:14.021 --> 03:11:17.150
BLONDIN: And we
shipped right on time.

03:11:17.150 --> 03:11:19.050
HANLEY: I think you
were ahead of time.

03:11:19.050 --> 03:11:21.550
BLONDIN: What you were referring
to was called the Hawthorne

03:11:21.550 --> 03:11:22.050
effect.

03:11:22.050 --> 03:11:24.170
Remember that?

03:11:24.170 --> 03:11:27.860
It came from Western
Electric, Hawthorne plant,

03:11:27.860 --> 03:11:29.740
outside of Chicago.

03:11:29.740 --> 03:11:32.000
And a famous experiment
was brought up

03:11:32.000 --> 03:11:35.390
in industrial psychology,
where they started off

03:11:35.390 --> 03:11:39.390
to see if increased lighting
would increase production.

03:11:39.390 --> 03:11:41.770
So they needed a control group.

03:11:41.770 --> 03:11:44.720
With the control group they
kept the lighting the same,

03:11:44.720 --> 03:11:48.780
stepped up the lights
on the test group.

03:11:48.780 --> 03:11:52.560
And production increased
on both of them.

03:11:52.560 --> 03:11:55.390
And they did it again, upped it.

03:11:55.390 --> 03:11:57.350
And it increased on
both of them again.

03:11:57.350 --> 03:12:01.010
Then they started lowering
the light on the control,

03:12:01.010 --> 03:12:03.560
till they got it down
where you could barely see.

03:12:03.560 --> 03:12:06.870
And production kept
going up on both of them.

03:12:06.870 --> 03:12:13.500
And the scientific
conclusion from that

03:12:13.500 --> 03:12:18.080
was if you give
people attention it's

03:12:18.080 --> 03:12:22.545
more important than the
lighting, or anything else.

03:12:22.545 --> 03:12:26.567
If they think what they're
doing is critical--

03:12:26.567 --> 03:12:29.150
FRASIER: There's one other thing
about that set of experiments

03:12:29.150 --> 03:12:32.320
that doesn't get reported in
all the management literature,

03:12:32.320 --> 03:12:33.980
and in the management classes.

03:12:33.980 --> 03:12:37.610
But it is in the original
papers, from the early '30s.

03:12:37.610 --> 03:12:41.360
And that is that the way people
got paid in the big plant

03:12:41.360 --> 03:12:43.820
was there was
incentive pay, but it

03:12:43.820 --> 03:12:47.630
was based on the output
of large groups of people.

03:12:47.630 --> 03:12:50.925
So if they had big output,
then people got more.

03:12:50.925 --> 03:12:52.550
And they put them in
this special room.

03:12:52.550 --> 03:12:58.052
They put production measurements
on every single machine,

03:12:58.052 --> 03:12:59.885
and they changed it to
individual pay rates.

03:13:02.670 --> 03:13:04.200
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:13:07.382 --> 03:13:11.790
TURNER: And the other thing I
think, Jack, is an example of--

03:13:11.790 --> 03:13:15.960
at least in my experience
in both the industrial one

03:13:15.960 --> 03:13:17.670
and also at the
lab-- and that is

03:13:17.670 --> 03:13:21.870
the importance of
project-oriented groups

03:13:21.870 --> 03:13:23.710
rather than matrix.

03:13:23.710 --> 03:13:27.030
I have never seen a
matrix organization work.

03:13:27.030 --> 03:13:28.847
FRASIER: Me either.

03:13:28.847 --> 03:13:30.680
SPEAKER: We tried like
hell for a long time.

03:13:30.680 --> 03:13:33.852
POUNDSTONE: [INAUDIBLE]

03:13:33.852 --> 03:13:35.310
BATES: I got a
paper I'll send you.

03:13:35.310 --> 03:13:38.450
It was done by my boss
up at GE in Pittsfield.

03:13:38.450 --> 03:13:40.100
And it was put in
just that way--

03:13:40.100 --> 03:13:42.480
it's the people, not
the organization.

03:13:42.480 --> 03:13:45.320
And it's only a
one-and-a-half-page thing,

03:13:45.320 --> 03:13:49.140
but it succinctly points out
the fact that you can put any

03:13:49.140 --> 03:13:51.690
organization you want in
there, and they'll either be

03:13:51.690 --> 03:13:53.640
successful or they'll fail.

03:13:53.640 --> 03:13:56.540
It says the main thing is to
get the people to work together,

03:13:56.540 --> 03:13:59.720
in a oneness of purpose,
and work as a team.

03:13:59.720 --> 03:14:00.720
BLONDIN: What you have--

03:14:03.557 --> 03:14:05.640
that was another thing we
had to do in management.

03:14:05.640 --> 03:14:13.300
We had to recognize when
to break up a program team.

03:14:13.300 --> 03:14:15.760
Well, you'd say, why
would you want to do that?

03:14:15.760 --> 03:14:20.760
Well, whenever you introduced
a new program coming

03:14:20.760 --> 03:14:23.640
out of engineering
it was much tougher

03:14:23.640 --> 03:14:26.520
to handle than a program
that had been shaken down

03:14:26.520 --> 03:14:27.570
through the years--

03:14:27.570 --> 03:14:28.332
much tougher.

03:14:32.490 --> 03:14:42.760
With the program orientation,
all the varsity players

03:14:42.760 --> 03:14:45.950
were on the whole program.

03:14:45.950 --> 03:14:48.750
It had become second nature.

03:14:48.750 --> 03:14:51.840
Everybody was way, way,
way up the learning curve.

03:14:51.840 --> 03:14:56.600
And then you put all the
brand new, green people

03:14:56.600 --> 03:15:00.200
on this terribly difficult thing
to build out of engineering.

03:15:00.200 --> 03:15:03.140
Where if someone needed
to be able to make

03:15:03.140 --> 03:15:05.720
a judgment that this is
unbuildable, and have it

03:15:05.720 --> 03:15:06.990
stick--

03:15:06.990 --> 03:15:09.870
and to have an operator
do that, she had to have,

03:15:09.870 --> 03:15:14.030
or he had to have a
tremendous amount of prestige.

03:15:14.030 --> 03:15:15.830
And the only ones
who had that prestige

03:15:15.830 --> 03:15:18.110
were the ones in
the old program.

03:15:18.110 --> 03:15:22.190
So it didn't take me long
as a manager of a plant

03:15:22.190 --> 03:15:23.690
with about 17 programs--

03:15:23.690 --> 03:15:25.447
POUNDSTONE: To starting
taking our people

03:15:25.447 --> 03:15:27.030
BLONDIN: --to start
taking our people.

03:15:27.030 --> 03:15:27.755
That's right.

03:15:27.755 --> 03:15:28.460
[LAUGHTER]

03:15:28.460 --> 03:15:30.107
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:15:30.107 --> 03:15:32.190
BRISS: And there's a certain
amount of pride, too,

03:15:32.190 --> 03:15:34.080
they got out of a serving
on a [? group-- ?]

03:15:34.080 --> 03:15:35.450
a prototype program.

03:15:35.450 --> 03:15:37.405
BLONDIN: Absolutely.

03:15:37.405 --> 03:15:38.780
DUGGAN: There is
one other point.

03:15:38.780 --> 03:15:42.550
And it's off the
personal relations,

03:15:42.550 --> 03:15:45.710
which I think is paramount in
any kind of a team activity.

03:15:45.710 --> 03:15:48.560
But in a lot of these things
we've described today,

03:15:48.560 --> 03:15:52.490
we keep referring to
materials that we changed,

03:15:52.490 --> 03:15:54.470
or it caused us problems--
or things that we

03:15:54.470 --> 03:15:57.650
did that involved new material.

03:15:57.650 --> 03:15:59.492
Personally, I thought
we had the world beat

03:15:59.492 --> 03:16:01.700
when we figured out we could
weld two wires together,

03:16:01.700 --> 03:16:02.680
and [INAUDIBLE] just go.

03:16:02.680 --> 03:16:03.555
You know what I mean?

03:16:03.555 --> 03:16:06.680
But the material science
aspect of this thing

03:16:06.680 --> 03:16:08.230
began to take on dimensions.

03:16:08.230 --> 03:16:11.090
And what was extraordinary
is the sophistication level

03:16:11.090 --> 03:16:13.760
went up, and it moved
along with every program

03:16:13.760 --> 03:16:14.750
I've been on since.

03:16:14.750 --> 03:16:17.690
I mean, plated wire memory
was a good classic example.

03:16:17.690 --> 03:16:19.400
And we lived through
that collectively,

03:16:19.400 --> 03:16:22.450
or at least a good
portion of us did.

03:16:22.450 --> 03:16:26.360
Where a lot of the
development was now

03:16:26.360 --> 03:16:28.180
beyond the area
of the electronic,

03:16:28.180 --> 03:16:31.630
or the packaging guy and the
shop, and everything else.

03:16:31.630 --> 03:16:35.090
It was [INAUDIBLE] these little
cells were doing plating.

03:16:35.090 --> 03:16:39.565
And a good one was on the
core rope, when we got RTV.

03:16:39.565 --> 03:16:41.690
And remember, if you
overloaded that thing the heat

03:16:41.690 --> 03:16:43.800
would expand it, and
bang all the wires?

03:16:43.800 --> 03:16:46.480
And this got to be a major
kind of problem for a while.

03:16:46.480 --> 03:16:47.889
It would get diagnosed.

03:16:47.889 --> 03:16:50.180
I remember I learned that
you don't drill a whole tight

03:16:50.180 --> 03:16:52.094
and then fill it up
with potting compound

03:16:52.094 --> 03:16:53.510
and let it just
pressurize itself,

03:16:53.510 --> 03:16:55.580
because strange things happen.

03:16:55.580 --> 03:16:57.400
And it expands.

03:16:57.400 --> 03:17:02.590
And respect for thermal control
is another serious thing

03:17:02.590 --> 03:17:03.440
with electronics.

03:17:03.440 --> 03:17:06.710
And where does it go, and how
do you get it out of here?

03:17:06.710 --> 03:17:08.750
And then, God bless,
those connectors

03:17:08.750 --> 03:17:11.565
are a major pain in the neck.

03:17:11.565 --> 03:17:13.690
BRISS: Science is always
going to be a major player

03:17:13.690 --> 03:17:15.530
in whatever's in the future.

03:17:15.530 --> 03:17:19.300
But when it comes
down to the trenches,

03:17:19.300 --> 03:17:22.550
it's not as important
from a technical aspect.

03:17:22.550 --> 03:17:24.740
But you don't get it done.

03:17:24.740 --> 03:17:27.139
DUGGAN: No, I'm not
conflicting with what you say.

03:17:27.139 --> 03:17:29.680
But I think it is important to
identify that sometimes you go

03:17:29.680 --> 03:17:31.630
into these things and think
it's just a matter of using

03:17:31.630 --> 03:17:34.030
the standard things-- reviews--
and find out you get back

03:17:34.030 --> 03:17:35.750
bitten by something from--

03:17:35.750 --> 03:17:37.960
BLONDIN: This wasn't
[INAUDIBLE] a computer.

03:17:37.960 --> 03:17:42.050
It was a module
that went in the LM.

03:17:42.050 --> 03:17:45.260
And we were getting
a corona [INAUDIBLE]..

03:17:45.260 --> 03:17:48.329
And this thing was
potted and very densely.

03:17:48.329 --> 03:17:49.620
SPEAKER: [INAUDIBLE] amplifier.

03:17:49.620 --> 03:17:50.520
BLONDIN: Yeah.

03:17:50.520 --> 03:17:51.770
Dense potting.

03:17:51.770 --> 03:17:55.770
And we would pot it, put
it in a vacuum chamber,

03:17:55.770 --> 03:17:58.970
draw the vacuum
chamber down, and wait

03:17:58.970 --> 03:18:02.270
for x number of minutes--

03:18:02.270 --> 03:18:04.130
until we saw all
the bubbles gone.

03:18:04.130 --> 03:18:06.960
And then check in again
for a half an hour.

03:18:06.960 --> 03:18:12.880
And then say, OK,
this is ready to go.

03:18:12.880 --> 03:18:14.332
There's no gas.

03:18:14.332 --> 03:18:16.480
But where's the
corona effect coming

03:18:16.480 --> 03:18:18.600
from if there's no gas in it?

03:18:18.600 --> 03:18:26.940
So we took a device--

03:18:26.940 --> 03:18:31.310
a blender, like you
use for a frappe.

03:18:31.310 --> 03:18:37.776
And we put that on the bottom
of this vacuum chamber.

03:18:37.776 --> 03:18:41.395
And we put that module in there.

03:18:41.395 --> 03:18:44.970
And when we didn't see
any more bubbles any more,

03:18:44.970 --> 03:18:46.340
we turned that on.

03:18:46.340 --> 03:18:48.660
That thing foamed.

03:18:48.660 --> 03:18:51.410
I mean, it was like beer--

03:18:51.410 --> 03:18:54.800
like Guinness stout.

03:18:54.800 --> 03:18:58.250
Tiny bubbles, froth
on the top of it.

03:18:58.250 --> 03:19:01.970
And then of course in the vacuum
chamber the froth collapsed.

03:19:01.970 --> 03:19:05.450
We said, how the hell
did we miss that?

03:19:05.450 --> 03:19:09.660
Well, we had thought that
the vacuum alone had enough

03:19:09.660 --> 03:19:13.320
[? poop ?] to pull all those
bubbles out, but it didn't.

03:19:13.320 --> 03:19:15.220
And after that, then
the problem went away.

03:19:15.220 --> 03:19:18.060
DUGGAN: A lot of times that
out gassing was corrosive, too.

03:19:18.060 --> 03:19:20.060
I mean, other things
were impacted by it.

03:19:20.060 --> 03:19:21.560
BLONDIN: I remember
around watching

03:19:21.560 --> 03:19:22.970
that for the first time.

03:19:22.970 --> 03:19:26.866
And seeing this, like someone
pouring a Guinness stout,

03:19:26.866 --> 03:19:28.802
and this foam rising.

03:19:28.802 --> 03:19:30.900
DUGGAN: You've got a
whole new career path.

03:19:30.900 --> 03:19:33.620
BLONDIN: And [? I said, ?]
unbelievable.

03:19:33.620 --> 03:19:38.660
TURNER: Does anybody remember
the irradiated poly-olefin

03:19:38.660 --> 03:19:43.140
insulation that would detonate?

03:19:43.140 --> 03:19:45.560
This was a wire.

03:19:45.560 --> 03:19:47.300
You must remember this, Cline.

03:19:47.300 --> 03:19:52.070
Down in the Reliability
Building down the street,

03:19:52.070 --> 03:19:56.190
somebody discovered that
this great wire insulation--

03:19:56.190 --> 03:19:59.500
which was very good for cut
through, and good electrically.

03:19:59.500 --> 03:20:01.640
It was called a
radiated poly-olefin

03:20:01.640 --> 03:20:04.160
and it had some double
bonds or something.

03:20:04.160 --> 03:20:09.200
And they were running it in
pure oxygen, in a test chamber--

03:20:09.200 --> 03:20:12.140
and getting it hot,
and stuff like that.

03:20:12.140 --> 03:20:15.560
After a certain point,
it would exchange.

03:20:15.560 --> 03:20:18.740
And it would explode.

03:20:18.740 --> 03:20:22.600
And it blew the lid off
the test chamber, really.

03:20:22.600 --> 03:20:23.600
SPEAKER: I believe that.

03:20:23.600 --> 03:20:25.910
TURNER: Maybe this never
got back to you guys.

03:20:25.910 --> 03:20:28.490
But they decided maybe
that's not a good idea.

03:20:28.490 --> 03:20:30.980
FRASIER: I didn't know
everything that was going on.

03:20:30.980 --> 03:20:34.280
TURNER: That was
George Nail's people.

03:20:34.280 --> 03:20:35.870
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:20:35.870 --> 03:20:38.190
SPEAKER: [INAUDIBLE] was
another exciting experience,

03:20:38.190 --> 03:20:39.759
choosing [INAUDIBLE].

03:20:39.759 --> 03:20:42.050
FRASIER: I was going to say,
if I switch gears a minute

03:20:42.050 --> 03:20:44.960
from the truly technical--
the technical stuff was

03:20:44.960 --> 03:20:48.560
really important, and had really
excellent technical people.

03:20:48.560 --> 03:20:54.890
But from a far distant view,
one of the absolute essential

03:20:54.890 --> 03:20:58.670
things was that there
were checks and balances

03:20:58.670 --> 03:20:59.900
in the program.

03:20:59.900 --> 03:21:07.220
Technically, organizationally,
and the way things were set up,

03:21:07.220 --> 03:21:09.200
most things that were
done got outside review

03:21:09.200 --> 03:21:11.490
of some kind or another.

03:21:11.490 --> 03:21:17.000
And like you heard a couple
of times ago on the software,

03:21:17.000 --> 03:21:19.970
NASA had people that were
going over the software.

03:21:19.970 --> 03:21:23.105
They had software being
checked at North American,

03:21:23.105 --> 03:21:24.899
and at Grumman in a way.

03:21:24.899 --> 03:21:26.690
There were lots of
mistakes found that way,

03:21:26.690 --> 03:21:28.880
in just that oversight.

03:21:28.880 --> 03:21:31.430
Similarly, there
was a certain amount

03:21:31.430 --> 03:21:35.690
of tension between
the Instrumentation

03:21:35.690 --> 03:21:41.480
Lab, and Raytheon,
and AC Electronics--

03:21:41.480 --> 03:21:44.780
and certainly among all
those with the spacecraft

03:21:44.780 --> 03:21:46.730
contractors.

03:21:46.730 --> 03:21:50.530
And those tensions
brought out things

03:21:50.530 --> 03:21:53.070
that you never would
have found any other way.

03:21:53.070 --> 03:21:55.970
And my experience,
since then particularly,

03:21:55.970 --> 03:21:58.010
has been seeing
where there aren't

03:21:58.010 --> 03:22:01.031
those checks and balances
how long things get hidden.

03:22:01.031 --> 03:22:02.780
POUNDSTONE: You mean
because there was not

03:22:02.780 --> 03:22:04.050
one prime contractor?

03:22:04.050 --> 03:22:05.510
FRASIER: Well, no, it's
not because there was not

03:22:05.510 --> 03:22:07.460
one prime contractor,
but it was because there

03:22:07.460 --> 03:22:09.590
was no way of checking
and balancing.

03:22:09.590 --> 03:22:11.930
So that if somebody--

03:22:11.930 --> 03:22:15.410
engineers, especially--
if they have trouble,

03:22:15.410 --> 03:22:17.204
they say, no, we don't
have any trouble.

03:22:17.204 --> 03:22:19.370
And they figure that if
they cover it up long enough

03:22:19.370 --> 03:22:21.100
they'll fix it, so
nobody will find out.

03:22:21.100 --> 03:22:23.915
Well, a lot of things
you don't get fixed.

03:22:23.915 --> 03:22:26.420
And because there are all
these checks and balances

03:22:26.420 --> 03:22:29.330
these things get found
much sooner, and in time

03:22:29.330 --> 03:22:31.730
to get fixed, than
they would have

03:22:31.730 --> 03:22:33.740
in almost any other program.

03:22:33.740 --> 03:22:36.617
BLONDIN: Case in point,
security at Logan Airport.

03:22:36.617 --> 03:22:38.700
FRASIER: Case in point,
security at Logan Airport.

03:22:38.700 --> 03:22:40.610
BLONDIN: No checks and balances.

03:22:40.610 --> 03:22:42.134
[INAUDIBLE]

03:22:42.134 --> 03:22:43.550
FRASIER: One of
the other things I

03:22:43.550 --> 03:22:45.890
think was key to
getting there on time

03:22:45.890 --> 03:22:51.620
was that managers who didn't
fit for whatever reason,

03:22:51.620 --> 03:22:55.250
or didn't perform, got changed.

03:22:55.250 --> 03:22:58.460
And they got changed
faster than they

03:22:58.460 --> 03:23:01.970
do in many certainly
many of the organizations

03:23:01.970 --> 03:23:05.790
I've worked with since-- both
government and industrial.

03:23:05.790 --> 03:23:11.810
And in addition to technology,
having good management

03:23:11.810 --> 03:23:13.040
is absolutely key.

03:23:13.040 --> 03:23:15.830
Because on a program
like this, just

03:23:15.830 --> 03:23:19.490
having good technical
people won't make it work.

03:23:19.490 --> 03:23:23.520
And so you look at the number
of changes that went on at AC,

03:23:23.520 --> 03:23:27.530
the number changes that went on
at NASA, the number of changes

03:23:27.530 --> 03:23:29.170
that went on at Raytheon.

03:23:29.170 --> 03:23:31.840
There were, I think,
a few changes even

03:23:31.840 --> 03:23:33.750
at the Instrumentation Lab.

03:23:33.750 --> 03:23:35.190
It's those kind of things.

03:23:35.190 --> 03:23:37.650
People don't always
fit in particular jobs.

03:23:37.650 --> 03:23:40.246
MINDELL: Why did it
happen on this program?

03:23:40.246 --> 03:23:42.930
It doesn't happen
in many programs.

03:23:42.930 --> 03:23:45.440
FRASIER: Well, I think it
was because there was, first

03:23:45.440 --> 03:23:48.690
of all, so much visibility
about things not getting done.

03:23:48.690 --> 03:23:52.230
And the program had an absolute
date, and a strong commitment

03:23:52.230 --> 03:23:53.220
from everyone.

03:23:53.220 --> 03:23:54.860
Nobody wanted to fail.

03:23:54.860 --> 03:23:56.730
And they weren't going
to allow themselves

03:23:56.730 --> 03:24:00.720
to fail because somebody
else wasn't doing a good job.

03:24:00.720 --> 03:24:06.030
BLONDIN: You had the
ultimate time constraint.

03:24:06.030 --> 03:24:10.270
Kennedy had said, we will
get to the moon this decade.

03:24:10.270 --> 03:24:13.890
This program had more visibility
than any other program.

03:24:13.890 --> 03:24:18.060
You could sweep a lot of things
under the rug other places.

03:24:18.060 --> 03:24:19.950
And if you didn't have
one weapons system,

03:24:19.950 --> 03:24:20.960
you'd have another one.

03:24:23.960 --> 03:24:28.340
The thought of an astronaut
perishing because something

03:24:28.340 --> 03:24:31.110
didn't work properly.

03:24:31.110 --> 03:24:34.820
And then you had the
more mundane things,

03:24:34.820 --> 03:24:37.310
like incentive contracts.

03:24:37.310 --> 03:24:41.660
So that you could
work very hard,

03:24:41.660 --> 03:24:47.480
and that was
ultimately up to you

03:24:47.480 --> 03:24:50.330
to say how much money
you were going to get.

03:24:50.330 --> 03:24:54.110
And it would be
like the New England

03:24:54.110 --> 03:25:01.270
Patriots having their players
play, and then every quarter--

03:25:01.270 --> 03:25:06.225
every time 25% of the
games have been played,

03:25:06.225 --> 03:25:10.020
then the coach decides how
much he's going to pay you.

03:25:10.020 --> 03:25:13.150
Well, anybody who's getting
in your way of earning

03:25:13.150 --> 03:25:18.210
your incentive fee, the
fact that he's a buddy,

03:25:18.210 --> 03:25:19.862
that doesn't cut much.

03:25:19.862 --> 03:25:20.362
[LAUGHTER]

03:25:20.362 --> 03:25:23.410
You know, get out of my way.

03:25:23.410 --> 03:25:27.830
And because the general
manager would raise hell

03:25:27.830 --> 03:25:29.520
if you didn't make--

03:25:29.520 --> 03:25:31.650
FRASIER: The incentive
structure of the contract

03:25:31.650 --> 03:25:33.120
was really important.

03:25:33.120 --> 03:25:37.180
And I got into the job I had
after that had been decided.

03:25:37.180 --> 03:25:39.630
So I had nothing to do
with putting it in place.

03:25:39.630 --> 03:25:43.800
This was part of putting it
all under AC Electronics.

03:25:43.800 --> 03:25:48.690
But the way it was set up,
because of this incentive--

03:25:48.690 --> 03:25:50.175
this award fee part--

03:25:50.175 --> 03:25:53.400
one of the requirements
was that you couldn't just

03:25:53.400 --> 03:25:56.310
wait till the end of the program
and then decide how much they

03:25:56.310 --> 03:25:57.060
were going to get.

03:25:57.060 --> 03:25:59.780
You had to give progress
reports along the way.

03:25:59.780 --> 03:26:02.900
And I initially thought,
boy, what a pain in the neck.

03:26:02.900 --> 03:26:04.780
And you know, I don't
like doing this at all.

03:26:04.780 --> 03:26:06.571
But looking back it
made a real difference.

03:26:06.571 --> 03:26:11.910
Because it was initially
monthly, and then quarterly,

03:26:11.910 --> 03:26:14.010
but we had to do--

03:26:14.010 --> 03:26:17.670
NASA, we had to go
up to AC Electronics,

03:26:17.670 --> 03:26:19.350
see the top management there.

03:26:19.350 --> 03:26:23.550
And then we had to go through
the list of things, that said,

03:26:23.550 --> 03:26:26.310
here's what we agreed
was important to get

03:26:26.310 --> 03:26:28.320
done the last time around.

03:26:28.320 --> 03:26:29.460
Here's what's been done.

03:26:29.460 --> 03:26:30.960
Here's the things
that we think were

03:26:30.960 --> 03:26:33.420
done better than we expected,
and here's the things were

03:26:33.420 --> 03:26:35.610
done worse than we expected.

03:26:35.610 --> 03:26:38.160
And be able to back
every one of those up.

03:26:38.160 --> 03:26:42.480
And so we'd go through this
about a three-hour meeting

03:26:42.480 --> 03:26:43.350
periodically.

03:26:43.350 --> 03:26:46.260
And we'd have the AC
Electronics program

03:26:46.260 --> 03:26:50.130
manager and the senior
functional managers there--

03:26:50.130 --> 03:26:53.030
and Paul Blasingame, the VP
in charge of the whole plant,

03:26:53.030 --> 03:26:54.780
as well as [? John ?]
[? Atwood. ?]

03:26:54.780 --> 03:26:57.630
And so what this
did was the fact

03:26:57.630 --> 03:26:59.970
that what happened
in the meeting

03:26:59.970 --> 03:27:01.450
wasn't the important thing.

03:27:01.450 --> 03:27:05.250
What the important thing was
was that there was the meeting,

03:27:05.250 --> 03:27:09.180
and that we insisted
that there weren't

03:27:09.180 --> 03:27:13.090
going to be any surprises about
what went on in the meeting.

03:27:13.090 --> 03:27:15.720
So you already knew what
was going to be discussed.

03:27:15.720 --> 03:27:18.990
His people knew what was
going to be discussed.

03:27:18.990 --> 03:27:23.566
On our side, I insisted that
nobody could just put something

03:27:23.566 --> 03:27:24.690
in because they were angry.

03:27:24.690 --> 03:27:28.835
For an example, one of my
guys put in something about AC

03:27:28.835 --> 03:27:30.210
hasn't done
something they wanted

03:27:30.210 --> 03:27:33.570
them to do, with regard to
Raytheon and the computer.

03:27:33.570 --> 03:27:38.722
So my reaction was, OK, how
did you tell them to do it?

03:27:38.722 --> 03:27:39.680
Well, there's no paper.

03:27:39.680 --> 03:27:40.805
Well, they called somebody.

03:27:40.805 --> 03:27:43.992
I said, that doesn't count.

03:27:43.992 --> 03:27:45.450
But it was the
discipline of having

03:27:45.450 --> 03:27:48.690
to do the report that led me
to that kind of behavior, led

03:27:48.690 --> 03:27:50.580
to learning of my guys.

03:27:50.580 --> 03:27:54.720
And it really
worked like a charm.

03:27:54.720 --> 03:27:57.950
And we carried that--

03:27:57.950 --> 03:28:00.360
some of the work we're
doing with EMC Corporation

03:28:00.360 --> 03:28:03.210
in Hopkinton and they have
a sole-source supplier

03:28:03.210 --> 03:28:05.730
called Seagate, because they
can't get anybody else to make

03:28:05.730 --> 03:28:08.070
really good disc drives.

03:28:08.070 --> 03:28:09.600
And there was a
long period of time

03:28:09.600 --> 03:28:12.630
where that was a
troubled relationship,

03:28:12.630 --> 03:28:14.290
for one reason or another.

03:28:14.290 --> 03:28:17.730
So we took this model, that
seemed to work with AC,

03:28:17.730 --> 03:28:21.090
and we modified it a little
bit and put it in place there.

03:28:21.090 --> 03:28:23.520
And that's been really working
for about five or six years

03:28:23.520 --> 03:28:24.019
now.

03:28:24.019 --> 03:28:29.100
So I think it was some of
those management innovations

03:28:29.100 --> 03:28:34.710
that people put in place that
really made a difference, too.

03:28:34.710 --> 03:28:38.110
POUNDSTONE: Let me
expand on that a little.

03:28:38.110 --> 03:28:43.560
Cline's point was the
visibility provided

03:28:43.560 --> 03:28:45.870
good oversight and
rapid feedback,

03:28:45.870 --> 03:28:49.380
to get things corrected.

03:28:49.380 --> 03:28:52.020
Although you deny it,
I still think the fact

03:28:52.020 --> 03:28:56.100
that NASA did not
have North American

03:28:56.100 --> 03:29:00.060
as a single contractor, and
everybody else was under them,

03:29:00.060 --> 03:29:02.120
gave you that visibility.

03:29:02.120 --> 03:29:04.200
FRASIER: I didn't
mean to deny that.

03:29:04.200 --> 03:29:05.382
I was referring to the--

03:29:05.382 --> 03:29:07.590
POUNDSTONE: That's sort of
the form in the Air Force.

03:29:07.590 --> 03:29:09.570
I've worked on some of
these Air Force programs

03:29:09.570 --> 03:29:13.530
where you're a third-tier
sub, and the Air Force

03:29:13.530 --> 03:29:15.030
has no idea what you're doing.

03:29:15.030 --> 03:29:16.950
And people can bury things.

03:29:16.950 --> 03:29:20.100
So the Air Force holds
these wonderful meetings

03:29:20.100 --> 03:29:24.010
with 150 second
lieutenants listening,

03:29:24.010 --> 03:29:26.910
but they don't
accomplish anything.

03:29:26.910 --> 03:29:27.770
so I think that--

03:29:27.770 --> 03:29:29.130
FRASIER: Then they
change over every year.

03:29:29.130 --> 03:29:30.840
POUNDSTONE: Yeah, the
contract structure

03:29:30.840 --> 03:29:33.840
that was set up with
NASA gave you people

03:29:33.840 --> 03:29:35.070
that kind of visibility.

03:29:35.070 --> 03:29:36.264
FRASIER: I agree completely.

03:29:36.264 --> 03:29:37.805
I thought you were
asking different--

03:29:37.805 --> 03:29:39.721
HALL: [INAUDIBLE] getting
some responsibility.

03:29:39.721 --> 03:29:43.780
So in the Air Force situation,
those Air Force guys

03:29:43.780 --> 03:29:45.390
don't have any
responsibility anyway,

03:29:45.390 --> 03:29:46.540
so they sort of sleep
in the meetings.

03:29:46.540 --> 03:29:48.206
HANLEY: They sleep,
and they play cards.

03:29:48.206 --> 03:29:50.347
HALL: In NASA, they had
some responsibility too.

03:29:50.347 --> 03:29:51.180
HANLEY: That's true.

03:29:51.180 --> 03:29:53.040
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:29:55.000 --> 03:29:59.370
BLONDIN: Your typical Air
Force review would be a senior

03:29:59.370 --> 03:30:03.240
officer and a whole bunch
of-- somebody said second

03:30:03.240 --> 03:30:06.020
lieutenants--

03:30:06.020 --> 03:30:08.060
a whole bunch of them.

03:30:08.060 --> 03:30:09.310
And the meeting would open.

03:30:09.310 --> 03:30:12.010
And these guys had
everything to say.

03:30:12.010 --> 03:30:14.790
And it would drag
on and on, and on.

03:30:14.790 --> 03:30:17.070
And it was non sequitur.

03:30:17.070 --> 03:30:19.860
The Navy, like
NASA, would come in

03:30:19.860 --> 03:30:22.560
and there would be a top guy.

03:30:22.560 --> 03:30:24.615
There would be a four striper--

03:30:24.615 --> 03:30:26.975
on rare occasions an admiral.

03:30:26.975 --> 03:30:29.360
And if they had a
lieutenant commander,

03:30:29.360 --> 03:30:32.020
it was to carry the
captain's briefcase.

03:30:32.020 --> 03:30:32.970
That was it.

03:30:32.970 --> 03:30:35.640
And it was like the
bridge of a ship.

03:30:35.640 --> 03:30:39.690
You know, captain's on the
bridge, everybody else shut up.

03:30:39.690 --> 03:30:44.600
I made a presentation one could
time to Admiral Julian Lake,

03:30:44.600 --> 03:30:48.630
surrounded down in naval ACS.

03:30:48.630 --> 03:30:54.690
And I wanted to prove
to him that we could--

03:30:54.690 --> 03:30:58.210
we, Raytheon-- could
handle the production

03:30:58.210 --> 03:31:02.640
on this jamming system,
as opposed to Hughes--

03:31:02.640 --> 03:31:05.910
had huge production facilities.

03:31:05.910 --> 03:31:07.890
And I went through a
half-an-hour presentation

03:31:07.890 --> 03:31:09.140
with slides.

03:31:09.140 --> 03:31:12.150
And when I was done-- that was
the Air Force, the first place.

03:31:12.150 --> 03:31:14.490
I got through slide
one and someone

03:31:14.490 --> 03:31:17.280
would have started raising
non-sequitur questions.

03:31:17.280 --> 03:31:19.320
I went through the whole thing.

03:31:19.320 --> 03:31:22.410
Everybody at that table
had their eye focused

03:31:22.410 --> 03:31:25.910
on Admiral Lake, not me.

03:31:25.910 --> 03:31:28.230
And I was giving
the presentation.

03:31:28.230 --> 03:31:30.120
It was all over.

03:31:30.120 --> 03:31:32.890
And I said, any questions?

03:31:32.890 --> 03:31:35.970
And Admiral Julian Lake
said, I don't have any.

03:31:35.970 --> 03:31:37.310
Anyone else?

03:31:37.310 --> 03:31:38.226
Dead.

03:31:38.226 --> 03:31:40.058
[LAUGHTER]

03:31:40.058 --> 03:31:42.580
If that had been
the Air Force, you

03:31:42.580 --> 03:31:44.250
would have had a
Congressional debate.

03:31:44.250 --> 03:31:46.170
Why they did that, I don't know.

03:31:46.170 --> 03:31:52.290
I guess it was the bridge of
a ship versus fighter planes.

03:31:52.290 --> 03:31:53.790
And NASA was the same way.

03:31:53.790 --> 03:31:59.330
They sent in their head honcho.

03:31:59.330 --> 03:32:01.860
And you just wait, you know?

03:32:01.860 --> 03:32:03.890
You listen to the argument.

03:32:03.890 --> 03:32:06.480
And then you say, I think
we ought to do this.

03:32:06.480 --> 03:32:07.610
And that'd be it.

03:32:07.610 --> 03:32:09.940
OK, salute and execute.

03:32:12.760 --> 03:32:15.530
We were glad to get off the
frying pan, because none

03:32:15.530 --> 03:32:17.890
of those decisions were easy.

03:32:17.890 --> 03:32:21.750
I mean, you had to
get there in the '60s,

03:32:21.750 --> 03:32:25.210
or you were going to
embarrass the country.

03:32:25.210 --> 03:32:30.120
The thing better work, or
you're to fry an astronaut.

03:32:30.120 --> 03:32:34.430
Your corporation's
reputation is at stake.

03:32:34.430 --> 03:32:38.730
Every corporation had huge
ads saying, we're on Apollo.

03:32:38.730 --> 03:32:40.490
And they would take them out.

03:32:40.490 --> 03:32:42.256
I used to shudder
when I saw them.

03:32:42.256 --> 03:32:45.690
I said, my God, this
thing better work.

03:32:45.690 --> 03:32:48.650
BLAIR-SMITH: Bill Tindall was
always very good in that role,

03:32:48.650 --> 03:32:51.770
as the NASA presence.

03:32:51.770 --> 03:32:55.490
In terms of software reviews,
he ran them like that.

03:32:55.490 --> 03:32:57.710
He followed everything,
understood everything.

03:32:57.710 --> 03:33:01.100
And if there was anything fishy,
he'd let you know in a second.

03:33:01.100 --> 03:33:04.370
BLONDIN: It was a different
group than the other NASA--

03:33:04.370 --> 03:33:07.470
an entirely different group.

03:33:07.470 --> 03:33:10.005
The kinds of guys
they had in there--

03:33:10.005 --> 03:33:15.140
and I ended up working
for a couple of them--

03:33:15.140 --> 03:33:18.770
for example, Joe Shea.

03:33:18.770 --> 03:33:22.890
They weren't the old Foggy
Bottom-esque types, Cline.

03:33:22.890 --> 03:33:29.220
They were guys who thought
quickly, could make decisions.

03:33:29.220 --> 03:33:33.280
And they were bound and
determined to get to the moon.

03:33:33.280 --> 03:33:35.630
POUNDSTONE: What other
questions do you have?

03:33:35.630 --> 03:33:37.110
MINDELL: Sandy
had some, I think.

03:33:37.110 --> 03:33:39.450
BROWN: I was kind of
curious about what happens

03:33:39.450 --> 03:33:40.770
after the Apollo project.

03:33:40.770 --> 03:33:42.190
What happens to the team?

03:33:42.190 --> 03:33:43.695
We've talked a lot about some
of the new techniques that

03:33:43.695 --> 03:33:45.690
were developed-- the
material science aspect.

03:33:45.690 --> 03:33:47.340
What happens after this?

03:33:47.340 --> 03:33:50.530
What happens to the little
old ladies, for example?

03:33:50.530 --> 03:33:51.925
HANLEY: They retired.

03:33:51.925 --> 03:33:53.800
BRISS: No, we moved them
onto other programs.

03:33:53.800 --> 03:33:55.440
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:33:57.104 --> 03:33:59.520
DUGGAN: Well, these things get
projected forward, I think.

03:33:59.520 --> 03:34:02.920
[INAUDIBLE] put in your
toolkit for the next proposal.

03:34:06.050 --> 03:34:08.640
POUNDSTONE: Certainly from the
contractor's point of view,

03:34:08.640 --> 03:34:10.840
the lessons that were
learned from Apollo

03:34:10.840 --> 03:34:13.360
were just applied to
the next generation.

03:34:13.360 --> 03:34:13.890
HALL: Yeah.

03:34:13.890 --> 03:34:17.537
They went into the Navy
programs, and [INAUDIBLE]

03:34:17.537 --> 03:34:18.870
POUNDSTONE: The Navy started it.

03:34:18.870 --> 03:34:22.800
Apollo was just a
second-generation Navy program

03:34:22.800 --> 03:34:23.440
forward.

03:34:23.440 --> 03:34:25.940
DUGGAN: They picked up a lot
of the things we learned Apollo

03:34:25.940 --> 03:34:29.360
too, including [INAUDIBLE].

03:34:29.360 --> 03:34:31.640
But we had Dave Gold in
those programs, which he

03:34:31.640 --> 03:34:33.400
was a single-minded individual.

03:34:33.400 --> 03:34:36.290
He'd make decisions on the
spot, sometimes with a lot

03:34:36.290 --> 03:34:37.980
of [INAUDIBLE].

03:34:37.980 --> 03:34:40.260
POUNDSTONE: From an
individual's point of view,

03:34:40.260 --> 03:34:45.630
as you heard here, in
some cases the careers

03:34:45.630 --> 03:34:48.310
continued along
Apollo or NASA lines,

03:34:48.310 --> 03:34:51.240
and other places the careers
moved somewhere else.

03:34:51.240 --> 03:34:56.950
And like I said, some who stayed
on Apollo too long, like Eldon,

03:34:56.950 --> 03:34:58.686
it didn't work out too well.

03:34:58.686 --> 03:35:00.060
BLAIR-SMITH: Well,
Eldon, how did

03:35:00.060 --> 03:35:03.930
it come about that
once we kind of had

03:35:03.930 --> 03:35:10.440
to give up being architects of
spacecraft computer systems,

03:35:10.440 --> 03:35:12.720
and we got into fault
tolerance, can you

03:35:12.720 --> 03:35:14.460
trace how that came about?

03:35:14.460 --> 03:35:18.270
HALL: Yeah, it actually started
in Apollo, near the end--

03:35:18.270 --> 03:35:20.330
before there was any
flights or anything,

03:35:20.330 --> 03:35:26.032
and before computers could
be accepted as reliable,

03:35:26.032 --> 03:35:32.820
Paul Ebersol gave us a contract
to build a dual computer.

03:35:32.820 --> 03:35:35.210
I wanted to figure out what
the name of that thing was.

03:35:35.210 --> 03:35:36.150
POUNDSTONE: Dual
computer program.

03:35:36.150 --> 03:35:37.983
SPEAKER: That was the
dual computer program.

03:35:37.983 --> 03:35:39.130
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:35:44.370 --> 03:35:46.060
BLAIR-SMITH: DCA,
they called it.

03:35:46.060 --> 03:35:47.545
HALL: Yeah, DCA.

03:35:47.545 --> 03:35:52.130
It was strap-down IMU,
and a dual computer DCA.

03:35:52.130 --> 03:35:56.835
That combination was sort
of in the background,

03:35:56.835 --> 03:36:01.470
in case the mainline thing
didn't work, or something.

03:36:01.470 --> 03:36:05.730
Well, Ebersol supported
that, and pushed it.

03:36:05.730 --> 03:36:07.395
Well, first of all,
the Apollo computer

03:36:07.395 --> 03:36:09.670
had fault tolerance, in a sense.

03:36:09.670 --> 03:36:11.820
PANELIST: Yes, it did.

03:36:11.820 --> 03:36:13.860
HALL: So we had fault
tolerance there,

03:36:13.860 --> 03:36:16.470
and then the DCA started
a different configuration

03:36:16.470 --> 03:36:18.130
of fault tolerance.

03:36:18.130 --> 03:36:21.250
That led to other forms
of fault tolerance,

03:36:21.250 --> 03:36:23.700
like multi processing
and so forth.

03:36:23.700 --> 03:36:26.070
A lot of that was
still supported

03:36:26.070 --> 03:36:32.670
by NASA, sort of looking
towards the shuttle.

03:36:32.670 --> 03:36:37.170
Even though eventually we lost
all contributions, or almost

03:36:37.170 --> 03:36:39.170
everything to the shuttle.

03:36:39.170 --> 03:36:45.160
But that's where multi
processor concepts got started.

03:36:45.160 --> 03:36:47.430
So does that answer
your question, sort of?

03:36:47.430 --> 03:36:48.180
BLAIR-SMITH: Yeah.

03:36:48.180 --> 03:36:50.790
Well, in any case, I
was just bringing it up

03:36:50.790 --> 03:36:54.270
partly because-- you know, what
became of people afterwards?

03:36:54.270 --> 03:36:58.517
In our case, we
took the same bunch

03:36:58.517 --> 03:37:00.600
of people-- really, I
think, substantially changed

03:37:00.600 --> 03:37:01.740
direction.

03:37:01.740 --> 03:37:06.420
It was very clear that
after Apollo that NASA

03:37:06.420 --> 03:37:08.820
felt, perhaps correctly,
that the industry was

03:37:08.820 --> 03:37:11.760
able to build spacecraft
computers-- which they

03:37:11.760 --> 03:37:13.780
obviously hadn't been before.

03:37:13.780 --> 03:37:16.410
And so perhaps in some
sense we taught the world

03:37:16.410 --> 03:37:17.400
how to do that.

03:37:17.400 --> 03:37:20.610
HALL: Yeah, so we
became obsolete.

03:37:20.610 --> 03:37:23.640
BLAIR-SMITH: We did ourselves
out of a job that way.

03:37:23.640 --> 03:37:28.140
POUNDSTONE: Because we were
non profit, couldn't compete.

03:37:28.140 --> 03:37:30.600
HALL: Dave Gold
used to say, the guy

03:37:30.600 --> 03:37:34.170
that makes the mistakes
always get more attention--

03:37:34.170 --> 03:37:37.140
because everybody comes in and
helps him solve the mistake.

03:37:37.140 --> 03:37:39.980
BATES: But then on the
Navy side of the house,

03:37:39.980 --> 03:37:42.600
we kept that design
agency concept--

03:37:42.600 --> 03:37:44.566
even to today, on
the Trident program.

03:37:44.566 --> 03:37:46.440
DUGGAN: There were some
major commercial spin

03:37:46.440 --> 03:37:48.100
offs out of the Apollo.

03:37:50.147 --> 03:37:52.230
POUNDSTONE: Well, there
were some major companies,

03:37:52.230 --> 03:37:54.370
like Intermetrics with Miller.

03:37:54.370 --> 03:37:56.217
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:37:56.217 --> 03:37:57.800
SPEAKER: That's an
interesting aspect.

03:37:57.800 --> 03:37:59.040
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:37:59.040 --> 03:38:00.748
SPEAKER: [? Alonzo. ?]
Remember that one?

03:38:00.748 --> 03:38:02.280
BATES: What about
the space group

03:38:02.280 --> 03:38:04.330
over here under Eisenhower?

03:38:04.330 --> 03:38:05.937
That moved off.

03:38:05.937 --> 03:38:07.520
I mean, there were
a lot of spin offs.

03:38:07.520 --> 03:38:10.740
In fact, Doc made the
comment at one time--

03:38:10.740 --> 03:38:14.432
his job was to
expand technology.

03:38:14.432 --> 03:38:16.140
HALL: Yeah, that was
Doc's whole concept.

03:38:16.140 --> 03:38:19.860
BATES: And then he got pissed
off when everybody left.

03:38:19.860 --> 03:38:21.150
HALL: Not really.

03:38:21.150 --> 03:38:21.930
SPEAKER: No, he
wasn't, not really.

03:38:21.930 --> 03:38:24.263
BATES: Well, I don't know
whether it was Doc or somebody

03:38:24.263 --> 03:38:26.160
else, because we
ran into a situation

03:38:26.160 --> 03:38:28.440
where other companies
were starting up.

03:38:28.440 --> 03:38:29.940
And they were taking people off.

03:38:29.940 --> 03:38:32.160
And they said, well, why
are you hiring these people?

03:38:32.160 --> 03:38:34.870
And I got caught in the
middle of one of them,

03:38:34.870 --> 03:38:37.410
where they were
setting up a company.

03:38:37.410 --> 03:38:39.222
I think it was even
Miller's company.

03:38:39.222 --> 03:38:40.680
And they said,
well, why didn't you

03:38:40.680 --> 03:38:42.780
let them know, because they
would keep them happy there?

03:38:42.780 --> 03:38:44.560
Well, they didn't want
to be happy there.

03:38:44.560 --> 03:38:48.680
HALL: That was after Doc
was sort of going downhill.

03:38:48.680 --> 03:38:51.360
Dave Driscoll got
very upset at people.

03:38:51.360 --> 03:38:53.391
BATES: That was the guy.

03:38:53.391 --> 03:38:55.265
SPEAKER: Well, we had
other internal politics

03:38:55.265 --> 03:38:56.139
that were driving it.

03:38:56.139 --> 03:38:58.550
POUNDSTONE: That might
make an interesting part

03:38:58.550 --> 03:39:01.860
of your report, if you
could sort of identify

03:39:01.860 --> 03:39:05.550
all of the spin
off companies that

03:39:05.550 --> 03:39:07.440
were formed out of this group.

03:39:07.440 --> 03:39:11.267
SPEAKER: What the degradation
curve was, and where they went.

03:39:11.267 --> 03:39:13.350
CLINE: There's one spin
off from this program that

03:39:13.350 --> 03:39:15.777
is I don't think
generally recognized,

03:39:15.777 --> 03:39:17.610
and I certainly have
never seen it in print.

03:39:21.050 --> 03:39:23.970
One of the problems we have
with the Apollo computer was--

03:39:23.970 --> 03:39:28.080
I think this was probably one
of the first developments that

03:39:28.080 --> 03:39:31.690
went through extensive
random vibration testing.

03:39:31.690 --> 03:39:34.650
And we found lots of
problems in the computer,

03:39:34.650 --> 03:39:37.554
and in the relays,
and everything else.

03:39:37.554 --> 03:39:39.470
POUNDSTONE: We didn't
have relays in Poseidon.

03:39:39.470 --> 03:39:40.386
That's why we didn't--

03:39:40.386 --> 03:39:42.420
FRASIER: OK, you
didn't have flat packs.

03:39:42.420 --> 03:39:47.220
So they found contamination
in the flat packs.

03:39:47.220 --> 03:39:49.736
And they were, as it turned
out, the cleanest flat packs

03:39:49.736 --> 03:39:50.610
that could be bought.

03:39:50.610 --> 03:39:52.580
And we tried lots
of other places.

03:39:52.580 --> 03:39:53.972
And still you had contamination.

03:39:53.972 --> 03:39:56.430
The little silicone particle
if they got in the right spot,

03:39:56.430 --> 03:39:57.390
would cause a--

03:39:57.390 --> 03:39:59.096
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:39:59.096 --> 03:40:00.220
FRAISER: These are silicon.

03:40:00.220 --> 03:40:01.440
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:40:04.970 --> 03:40:06.870
FRASIER: This
would cause hiccups

03:40:06.870 --> 03:40:09.270
in the computer,
which is not too good.

03:40:09.270 --> 03:40:12.480
And that got sorted
out through attention

03:40:12.480 --> 03:40:15.510
to cleaning the [INAUDIBLE]
random vibration screening.

03:40:15.510 --> 03:40:19.990
Then there was-- maybe '68,
or something like that--

03:40:19.990 --> 03:40:24.450
one of the guys at Autonetics
had jobs like yours--

03:40:24.450 --> 03:40:25.950
come around to see
the NASA folks,

03:40:25.950 --> 03:40:28.040
and try to see if
there's programs.

03:40:28.040 --> 03:40:30.390
But meets in my
office in Houston.

03:40:30.390 --> 03:40:32.856
And we were talking,
and he said,

03:40:32.856 --> 03:40:34.230
I really shouldn't
tell you this,

03:40:34.230 --> 03:40:36.240
but I asked him about
the Minuteman-II.

03:40:36.240 --> 03:40:40.810
He said, the last nine
shots, we had seven failures.

03:40:40.810 --> 03:40:43.090
And these are the
first nine shots.

03:40:43.090 --> 03:40:44.250
We had seven failures.

03:40:44.250 --> 03:40:45.640
I said, my God, what happened?

03:40:45.640 --> 03:40:47.400
He said, well, it
gets up to staging.

03:40:47.400 --> 03:40:50.220
This thing stages, and
the computer goes crazy

03:40:50.220 --> 03:40:52.740
and it blows up.

03:40:52.740 --> 03:40:56.956
From that conversation,
I told him

03:40:56.956 --> 03:40:58.580
what were were doing
on Apollo and what

03:40:58.580 --> 03:41:00.200
you guys had found out.

03:41:00.200 --> 03:41:02.232
And I expected the way
things go that that

03:41:02.232 --> 03:41:03.690
would just totally
die, it wouldn't

03:41:03.690 --> 03:41:04.890
get through the system.

03:41:04.890 --> 03:41:07.690
Apparently, this is a
real crisis at Autonetics.

03:41:07.690 --> 03:41:10.260
And the next step was
they came to Raytheon.

03:41:10.260 --> 03:41:14.400
Raytheon screened their modules,
teach them how to screen.

03:41:14.400 --> 03:41:17.430
And that's what got the
Minuteman-II program back

03:41:17.430 --> 03:41:18.687
online.

03:41:18.687 --> 03:41:21.270
BLONDIN: That's a case in point,
though, that never could have

03:41:21.270 --> 03:41:23.570
happened on Apollo--
where you could have

03:41:23.570 --> 03:41:26.640
had seven out of nine failures.

03:41:26.640 --> 03:41:27.957
HALL: I couldn't afford that.

03:41:27.957 --> 03:41:29.790
POUNDSTONE: They couldn't
fly one out of it.

03:41:29.790 --> 03:41:30.600
HALL: That's right.

03:41:30.600 --> 03:41:34.680
BLONDIN: It just-- it
kept everybody focused.

03:41:34.680 --> 03:41:36.740
BRISS: That's that
man-on-board stuff again.

03:41:36.740 --> 03:41:39.360
POUNDSTONE: In my mind, as
much as I hate to admit it,

03:41:39.360 --> 03:41:44.010
I think that issue of the
contamination in the flat packs

03:41:44.010 --> 03:41:45.780
was a--

03:41:45.780 --> 03:41:47.580
we were all at fault in there.

03:41:47.580 --> 03:41:51.642
We never thought
about weightlessness.

03:41:51.642 --> 03:41:52.350
HALL: About what?

03:41:52.350 --> 03:41:53.580
POUNDSTONE: Weightlessness.

03:41:53.580 --> 03:41:54.770
HALL: Oh yes, we did.

03:41:54.770 --> 03:41:56.990
POUNDSTONE: I don't recall
thinking [INAUDIBLE]..

03:41:56.990 --> 03:41:59.406
HALL: But what could we with
it sitting here on the earth?

03:41:59.406 --> 03:42:02.500
I was very concerned about what
was going to those particles.

03:42:02.500 --> 03:42:05.450
BATES: Well, what was the
one with the solder ball?

03:42:05.450 --> 03:42:06.930
HALL: Apollo 14.

03:42:06.930 --> 03:42:09.140
BATES: No, no, this
was inside a flat pack.

03:42:09.140 --> 03:42:10.941
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:42:10.941 --> 03:42:11.940
HALL: It was in a relay.

03:42:11.940 --> 03:42:13.260
Apollo 12.

03:42:13.260 --> 03:42:16.270
BATES: When they
sealed the thing--

03:42:16.270 --> 03:42:18.720
North American had a
problem when they sealed--

03:42:18.720 --> 03:42:20.310
I thought it was a flat pack.

03:42:20.310 --> 03:42:22.490
They had a little solder
ball on top of it.

03:42:22.490 --> 03:42:25.020
And when they wanted vibration
the solder ball fell off.

03:42:25.320 --> 03:42:26.970
HALL: I thought you meant
an actual [INAUDIBLE]..

03:42:26.970 --> 03:42:28.230
POUNDSTONE: You're
talking about a hybrid.

03:42:28.230 --> 03:42:29.130
BATES: Yeah, OK.

03:42:29.130 --> 03:42:30.550
POUNDSTONE: The solder hybrids.

03:42:30.550 --> 03:42:38.140
HALL: Apollo 12, the main
panel DSKY at the launch pad

03:42:38.140 --> 03:42:40.350
signaled what was
normally the test

03:42:40.350 --> 03:42:43.819
for detecting these shorts--
all eights on the DSKY.

03:42:43.819 --> 03:42:45.360
They were just about
ready to launch,

03:42:45.360 --> 03:42:48.260
and that signaled all
eights several times, which

03:42:48.260 --> 03:42:51.980
proved there was some sort of a
contamination in those relays.

03:42:51.980 --> 03:42:54.910
Yet that thing flew
without any problem.

03:42:54.910 --> 03:42:57.700
So you sort of
answered the question,

03:42:57.700 --> 03:42:59.490
are these things
going to float around?

03:42:59.490 --> 03:43:01.009
Yes, we--

03:43:01.009 --> 03:43:02.550
POUNDSTONE: What we
didn't do, Eldon,

03:43:02.550 --> 03:43:06.700
we didn't investigate
passivation of semiconductor

03:43:06.700 --> 03:43:09.130
surfaces back in those days.

03:43:09.130 --> 03:43:10.130
HALL: Not seriously, no.

03:43:10.250 --> 03:43:11.490
POUNDSTONE: That's right.

03:43:11.490 --> 03:43:13.980
Today that's a standard
in the industry, where

03:43:13.980 --> 03:43:16.270
we needed it back in Apollo.

03:43:16.270 --> 03:43:17.920
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:43:21.420 --> 03:43:27.010
HALL: And the particles
got static charge

03:43:27.010 --> 03:43:28.405
and would stick to things.

03:43:28.905 --> 03:43:29.696
POUNDSTONE: I know.

03:43:29.696 --> 03:43:32.480
But if you were
passivated, they wouldn't.

03:43:32.480 --> 03:43:32.990
HALL: What?

03:43:32.990 --> 03:43:34.490
BLAIR-SMITH: If you
had passivated--

03:43:34.490 --> 03:43:35.965
we had unpassivated surfaces.

03:43:38.950 --> 03:43:41.860
HANLEY: You can contaminate the
goddamn surface pretty easily,

03:43:41.860 --> 03:43:42.794
too.

03:43:42.794 --> 03:43:44.460
HALL: That's what we
were worried about.

03:43:44.460 --> 03:43:45.900
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:43:45.900 --> 03:43:48.441
CLINE: [INAUDIBLE] out of some
of these meetings, [INAUDIBLE]

03:43:48.441 --> 03:43:51.020
I'm going down [INAUDIBLE].

03:43:51.020 --> 03:43:53.445
BATES: They went till 2 or
3 o'clock in the morning.

03:43:53.445 --> 03:43:55.320
POUNDSTONE: Well,
the other issue,

03:43:55.320 --> 03:44:01.060
where perhaps it was a failing
of the lack of formal system

03:44:01.060 --> 03:44:03.520
engineering, the fact
you guys never really

03:44:03.520 --> 03:44:06.460
had a spec for what
you were building.

03:44:06.460 --> 03:44:08.410
HALL: That was beneficial.

03:44:08.410 --> 03:44:10.900
POUNDSTONE: There was one
place that was not beneficial.

03:44:10.900 --> 03:44:18.220
As I recall, you never
had a spec for fire

03:44:18.220 --> 03:44:19.280
until after the accident.

03:44:19.280 --> 03:44:20.580
HALL: Until after the accident.

03:44:20.580 --> 03:44:20.800
POUNDSTONE: That's right.

03:44:20.800 --> 03:44:22.250
HALL: Neither did anybody
else on the [? staff. ?]

03:44:22.250 --> 03:44:23.180
FRASIER: I think
North American had--

03:44:23.180 --> 03:44:23.810
POUNDSTONE: No.

03:44:23.810 --> 03:44:25.371
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:44:25.371 --> 03:44:27.120
FRASIER: I take a
different point of view.

03:44:27.120 --> 03:44:30.430
Fire was something that
concerned NASA from day one,

03:44:30.430 --> 03:44:35.740
particularly because they were
going to be in 5 psi oxygen.

03:44:35.740 --> 03:44:38.020
So people did pay some
attention to that.

03:44:38.020 --> 03:44:40.360
And there were
numbers of tests done

03:44:40.360 --> 03:44:45.807
at zero g's in C-135's,
in 100% oxygen.

03:44:45.807 --> 03:44:47.140
And I remember seeing the movie.

03:44:47.140 --> 03:44:48.681
Seeing [INAUDIBLE]
the fire gets lit,

03:44:48.681 --> 03:44:50.510
and the fire goes out quickly.

03:44:50.510 --> 03:44:56.560
And so the spreading
of fire in zero g

03:44:56.560 --> 03:45:01.000
was not considered that
it was going to happen,

03:45:01.000 --> 03:45:02.190
even if you got something.

03:45:02.190 --> 03:45:05.680
Now, people were concerned
about fire, flammable stuff,

03:45:05.680 --> 03:45:10.000
but not anything like to
the degree after the fire.

03:45:10.000 --> 03:45:12.700
In the IMU, had a
nice thick layer

03:45:12.700 --> 03:45:14.710
of polyurethane-foam
insulation around it--

03:45:14.710 --> 03:45:19.290
which in 100% oxygen would burn
like that, but not in zero g.

03:45:22.180 --> 03:45:26.470
And it was then after the
fire that people had really

03:45:26.470 --> 03:45:28.780
gone through and looked
at every single thing,

03:45:28.780 --> 03:45:30.820
to see what would burn.

03:45:30.820 --> 03:45:33.370
And even after the
fire, North American

03:45:33.370 --> 03:45:38.290
kept, for quite a while,
their big Plexiglas panel.

03:45:38.290 --> 03:45:39.940
It was now painted
with fire retardant

03:45:39.940 --> 03:45:42.970
paint over the
electroluminescent displays.

03:45:42.970 --> 03:45:48.270
And after test it, I
think it was run at AC.

03:45:48.270 --> 03:45:50.220
And the same kind
of panel was going

03:45:50.220 --> 03:45:52.850
to be put for the
guidance-system controls.

03:45:52.850 --> 03:45:55.900
And the test was
run at AC, where

03:45:55.900 --> 03:45:59.650
they put a hot wire
on that in a chamber,

03:45:59.650 --> 03:46:04.270
with 5 or 16 psi oxygen now.

03:46:04.270 --> 03:46:08.080
But it was quite a
spectacular movie.

03:46:08.080 --> 03:46:11.420
You got this little flame going
initially from this hot wire.

03:46:11.420 --> 03:46:14.230
And then pretty
quick you get poof.

03:46:14.230 --> 03:46:16.720
And here would go this little
explosion of burning plastic

03:46:16.720 --> 03:46:17.219
out.

03:46:17.219 --> 03:46:18.900
And them poof, and
poof, and poof.

03:46:18.900 --> 03:46:21.370
And when people
saw that movie they

03:46:21.370 --> 03:46:22.990
decided that all
these panels had

03:46:22.990 --> 03:46:25.010
to be changed to be aluminum.

03:46:25.010 --> 03:46:26.540
So there was a lot
of focus on that.

03:46:26.540 --> 03:46:28.790
POUNDSTONE: That [INAUDIBLE]
passed down the guidance,

03:46:28.790 --> 03:46:29.600
wasn't it?

03:46:29.600 --> 03:46:32.170
FRASIER: Oh yes, absolutely.

03:46:32.170 --> 03:46:36.730
The panel on the guidance
system was a solid piece

03:46:36.730 --> 03:46:40.090
of aluminum with the
letters engraved out,

03:46:40.090 --> 03:46:42.560
over an electric
luminescent panel.

03:46:42.560 --> 03:46:45.541
There was, on the DSKY,
it had originally--

03:46:45.541 --> 03:46:47.290
I think there was some
plastic back there.

03:46:47.290 --> 03:46:49.800
And it ended up with a
piece of plastic over it.

03:46:49.800 --> 03:46:52.360
And the glass kept cracking
in vacuum testing--

03:46:52.360 --> 03:46:54.010
over and over, and over again.

03:46:54.010 --> 03:46:55.820
It took a long time to get out.

03:46:55.820 --> 03:46:57.800
POUNDSTONE: I remember
cracked glass.

03:46:57.800 --> 03:47:00.334
SPEAKER: [INAUDIBLE]

03:47:00.334 --> 03:47:01.250
FRASIER: Yeah, we did.

03:47:01.250 --> 03:47:04.940
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:47:04.940 --> 03:47:06.190
HALL: That was after the fire.

03:47:06.190 --> 03:47:08.190
POUNDSTONE: I never saw
the spec on flammability

03:47:08.190 --> 03:47:09.070
until after the fire.

03:47:09.070 --> 03:47:09.770
I remember that.

03:47:09.770 --> 03:47:12.420
HALL: I didn't know you had
ever seen a spec on flammability

03:47:12.420 --> 03:47:13.130
at all.

03:47:13.130 --> 03:47:15.047
SPEAKER: I don't remember
a flammability spec.

03:47:15.047 --> 03:47:15.755
But the problem--

03:47:15.755 --> 03:47:16.690
HALL: I don't either.

03:47:16.690 --> 03:47:18.273
SPEAKER: But I was
too into paperwork.

03:47:18.273 --> 03:47:19.960
SPEAKER: You got rid
of the pure oxygen.

03:47:19.960 --> 03:47:23.410
BLONDIN: Kind of like a
spec on cockpit doors.

03:47:23.410 --> 03:47:24.940
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:47:27.370 --> 03:47:28.870
HALL: No, we never had a spec.

03:47:28.870 --> 03:47:29.911
POUNDSTONE: That's right.

03:47:29.911 --> 03:47:31.030
You had no spec.

03:47:31.030 --> 03:47:33.532
But typically, if you'd have
been a sub to North American,

03:47:33.532 --> 03:47:34.990
they'd have written
you a big spec.

03:47:34.990 --> 03:47:36.064
HALL: That's right.

03:47:36.064 --> 03:47:37.480
POUNDSTONE: It
would probably have

03:47:37.480 --> 03:47:39.730
had a lot of mistakes in it,
but you'd have had a spec.

03:47:39.730 --> 03:47:40.880
HALL: And we'd have been
tied up in knots forever.

03:47:40.880 --> 03:47:41.400
POUNDSTONE: That's right.

03:47:41.400 --> 03:47:41.941
That's right.

03:47:41.941 --> 03:47:44.337
BLONDIN: Did you ever read
the Army spec on cookies?

03:47:44.337 --> 03:47:46.170
So help me God, they
have a spec on cookies.

03:47:46.170 --> 03:47:47.378
POUNDSTONE: I'll bet they do.

03:47:47.378 --> 03:47:49.570
BLONDIN: They did.

03:47:49.570 --> 03:47:52.980
Instead of going out and buying
cookies, they had spec on it.

03:47:52.980 --> 03:47:57.190
FRASIER: You ought to read
the specs around the M-16,

03:47:57.190 --> 03:48:00.050
and then how that led to
all the jamming problems.

03:48:00.050 --> 03:48:00.950
I mean, it's crazy.

03:48:03.730 --> 03:48:06.310
POUNDSTONE: Have we
answered your question?

03:48:06.310 --> 03:48:07.700
BROWN: I think so.

03:48:07.700 --> 03:48:10.030
This Intermetrics story
seems kind of interesting.

03:48:10.030 --> 03:48:12.849
Could someone talk a
little about Intermetrics?

03:48:12.849 --> 03:48:13.640
BATES: John Miller?

03:48:13.640 --> 03:48:15.181
SPEAKER: John Miller
is still around.

03:48:15.181 --> 03:48:17.260
POUNDSTONE: Who knows that best?

03:48:17.260 --> 03:48:19.150
BATES: I don't know,
other than John

03:48:19.150 --> 03:48:21.322
asked me to be his
marketing manager once,

03:48:21.322 --> 03:48:22.280
when I was at Raytheon.

03:48:22.280 --> 03:48:23.580
And I went and looked.

03:48:23.580 --> 03:48:25.330
And I saw all the guys.

03:48:25.330 --> 03:48:27.262
They had-- Copps went there.

03:48:27.507 --> 03:48:28.465
SPEAKER: Flanders went.

03:48:28.465 --> 03:48:30.839
BATES: Flanders went there
And I came to the conclusion--

03:48:30.839 --> 03:48:33.940
I said, hey, you got a bunch of
marketing managers right here.

03:48:33.940 --> 03:48:37.430
I said, all you need
is a marketing plan.

03:48:37.430 --> 03:48:40.820
You got [INAUDIBLE] what you
got, and where you want to go,

03:48:40.820 --> 03:48:42.480
and how you going to get there?

03:48:42.480 --> 03:48:44.770
And put together a marketing
plan that would do that.

03:48:44.770 --> 03:48:47.970
Now, I did that with him, OK?

03:48:47.970 --> 03:48:51.800
And he paid me off by
taking me out for dinner.

03:48:51.800 --> 03:48:54.930
But he wanted to go to Joe
Tecce's, which he couldn't

03:48:54.930 --> 03:48:57.870
get into, so I had to go through
the back door to get him in.

03:48:57.870 --> 03:49:04.030
But anyway, no, he left, and he
started that company as a spin

03:49:04.030 --> 03:49:04.710
off--

03:49:04.710 --> 03:49:06.060
and did very well with it.

03:49:06.060 --> 03:49:07.980
And all the people
did well [INAUDIBLE]

03:49:07.980 --> 03:49:09.480
HALL: Yeah, he
became a millionaire,

03:49:09.480 --> 03:49:11.100
while the rest of were paupers.

03:49:11.100 --> 03:49:13.100
POUNDSTONE: Does Intermetrics
still exist today?

03:49:13.100 --> 03:49:13.950
BATES: No, no.

03:49:13.950 --> 03:49:14.480
He sold it.

03:49:14.480 --> 03:49:15.313
SPEAKER: He sold it.

03:49:15.313 --> 03:49:17.130
POUNDSTONE: To who?

03:49:17.130 --> 03:49:20.010
BATES: I don't know, but
he sold it to somebody.

03:49:20.010 --> 03:49:22.950
And now he just raises orchids.

03:49:22.950 --> 03:49:29.150
HALL: Well, at the last meeting
Jim Miller was in this meeting.

03:49:29.150 --> 03:49:31.800
He was with him at Intermetrics.

03:49:31.800 --> 03:49:33.880
He'd be the one that
got [INAUDIBLE]..

03:49:33.880 --> 03:49:36.400
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:49:36.400 --> 03:49:38.520
BATES: Well, Ed Copps is
still around, isn't he?

03:49:38.520 --> 03:49:39.680
SPEAKER: John [INAUDIBLE]

03:49:39.680 --> 03:49:40.830
SPEAKER: Steve Copps
is around still.

03:49:40.830 --> 03:49:41.855
BATES: Is it Steve Copps?

03:49:41.855 --> 03:49:42.700
SPEAKER: Well, that's
the younger brother.

03:49:42.700 --> 03:49:44.340
I don't know [INAUDIBLE].

03:49:44.340 --> 03:49:46.140
SPEAKER: Did Steve go
to [? Big ?] [? I? ?]

03:49:46.140 --> 03:49:48.036
SPEAKER: Yeah, yes.

03:49:48.036 --> 03:49:50.160
SPEAKER: Ed was at the very
first meeting, I think.

03:49:50.160 --> 03:49:51.140
SPEAKER: Ed Copps was--

03:49:51.140 --> 03:49:54.270
SPEAKER: Yeah, I think he's
been one of the [INAUDIBLE]..

03:49:54.270 --> 03:49:56.970
HALL: Yeah, and Jim Miller
was in the second meeting.

03:49:56.970 --> 03:49:58.970
SPEAKER: That would be a
better contact on that.

03:49:58.970 --> 03:50:00.220
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:50:01.924 --> 03:50:03.840
SPEAKER: And you know
Alonso formed a company,

03:50:03.840 --> 03:50:04.917
and you had him in here.

03:50:04.917 --> 03:50:06.000
He probably talked about--

03:50:07.050 --> 03:50:09.780
BATES: I'd get a hold
of Eisenhower, too.

03:50:09.780 --> 03:50:13.020
Eisenhower has a company
over here, Space something--

03:50:13.020 --> 03:50:14.490
whatever it's called.

03:50:14.490 --> 03:50:17.430
I'll get you his business
card, or whatever it is.

03:50:17.430 --> 03:50:20.460
FRASIER: Was Eisenhower
involved in Apollo?

03:50:20.460 --> 03:50:22.867
BATES: I think he was, but
I can't really remember.

03:50:22.867 --> 03:50:24.200
HALL: I don't remember the name.

03:50:24.200 --> 03:50:26.207
BATES: He was a spin
off out of [INAUDIBLE]..

03:50:26.207 --> 03:50:27.540
FRASIER: But that's in the '80s.

03:50:27.540 --> 03:50:29.165
BATES: And he brought
Stanton with him,

03:50:29.165 --> 03:50:32.980
and he brought a whole
bunch of other people.

03:50:32.980 --> 03:50:35.400
HALL: Yeah, he wasn't
involved in Apollo.

03:50:35.400 --> 03:50:37.167
BATES: I don't think so, no.

03:50:37.167 --> 03:50:38.500
But he didn't ask that question.

03:50:38.500 --> 03:50:40.210
He talked about
spin offs out of--

03:50:40.210 --> 03:50:44.610
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:50:44.610 --> 03:50:47.166
BATES: Well, certainly
Alonso and Miller are.

03:50:47.166 --> 03:50:48.540
SPEAKER: I'm sure
there are more,

03:50:48.540 --> 03:50:49.820
but I can't think of them.

03:50:49.820 --> 03:50:50.030
HALL: Hopkins.

03:50:50.030 --> 03:50:50.870
All of those guys.

03:50:50.870 --> 03:50:53.140
SPEAKER: Oh, Hopkins
formed a company?

03:50:53.140 --> 03:50:55.030
HALL: Well, he joined a company.

03:50:59.701 --> 03:51:01.950
He was the one that got into
trouble with Ed Driscoll,

03:51:01.950 --> 03:51:03.900
because he was leaving
to go to a company,

03:51:03.900 --> 03:51:07.290
and supposedly taking
proprietary information

03:51:07.290 --> 03:51:08.500
with him [INAUDIBLE].

03:51:08.500 --> 03:51:09.750
SPEAKER: That's why you leave.

03:51:09.750 --> 03:51:11.430
[LAUGHTER]

03:51:11.430 --> 03:51:14.030
HALL: Especially that
was Draper's design.

03:51:14.030 --> 03:51:17.050
You learn something, and
you're leaving, and go do it.

03:51:17.050 --> 03:51:19.660
BATES: How about
Mike [? Zupuppo ?]

03:51:19.660 --> 03:51:22.485
DUGGAN: That's another aspect
of the Draper organization

03:51:22.485 --> 03:51:25.970
that you may want
to take note of.

03:51:25.970 --> 03:51:28.230
Doc always had the idea that
if you come up with idea

03:51:28.230 --> 03:51:29.280
and execute it--

03:51:29.280 --> 03:51:31.620
and have the capability
to do it yourself--

03:51:31.620 --> 03:51:35.850
and that went to [INAUDIBLE]
initially, which is his forte.

03:51:35.850 --> 03:51:39.330
But the capability to actually
mechanize what it was you

03:51:39.330 --> 03:51:40.590
thought up--

03:51:40.590 --> 03:51:42.330
which means you made
that transition,

03:51:42.330 --> 03:51:44.540
and tested and used it.

03:51:44.540 --> 03:51:48.152
This is kind of a
unique thing I found.

03:51:48.152 --> 03:51:53.210
It was not just a little
research lab [INAUDIBLE]..

03:51:53.210 --> 03:51:55.490
He actually had
machining capabilites

03:51:55.490 --> 03:51:57.440
to extraordinary
degrees, particularly

03:51:57.440 --> 03:51:59.610
in the [? general ?] technology.

03:51:59.610 --> 03:52:00.580
And he carried it.

03:52:00.580 --> 03:52:02.720
If you brought a program
into [INAUDIBLE]----

03:52:02.720 --> 03:52:05.520
at least in the initial stages--

03:52:05.520 --> 03:52:09.310
and it was significant,
you got to run it.

03:52:09.310 --> 03:52:12.481
BATES: That's how
[INAUDIBLE] got the job.

03:52:12.481 --> 03:52:13.980
HALL: Yeah, [INAUDIBLE]
got the job.

03:52:13.980 --> 03:52:15.022
But that was that policy.

03:52:15.022 --> 03:52:16.396
BATES: Yeah, that
was the policy.

03:52:16.396 --> 03:52:19.070
DUGGAN: And [INAUDIBLE] and
people that were with him

03:52:19.070 --> 03:52:20.350
were key [INAUDIBLE].

03:52:20.350 --> 03:52:22.900
BATES: But you're talking
about the Apollo computer here.

03:52:22.900 --> 03:52:25.810
If you haven't got an
[INAUDIBLE] from Draper Lab,

03:52:25.810 --> 03:52:27.590
or from the
Instrumentation Laboratory,

03:52:27.590 --> 03:52:31.320
it had the eye of the gyro,
the IMU in the middle,

03:52:31.320 --> 03:52:35.310
and the over in the
periphery was the computer.

03:52:35.310 --> 03:52:38.385
Now, if you go in from Raytheon,
the computer was in the middle.

03:52:38.385 --> 03:52:39.802
Everything else
was the periphery.

03:52:39.802 --> 03:52:42.051
DUGGAN: He also have that
tree, and Draper [INAUDIBLE]

03:52:42.051 --> 03:52:43.170
and it was all the rest.

03:52:43.170 --> 03:52:44.170
BATES: I asked him once.

03:52:44.170 --> 03:52:45.607
I said, where's
your organization?

03:52:45.607 --> 03:52:46.440
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:52:46.440 --> 03:52:48.982
BATES: He's picked up the phone
book, and he says, that's it.

03:52:48.982 --> 03:52:50.689
DUGGAN: The other
thing he did, he always

03:52:50.689 --> 03:52:52.460
gave you titles
that were similar

03:52:52.460 --> 03:52:53.601
to the academic structure.

03:52:53.601 --> 03:52:55.850
And if you get promoted
enough, you could have a title

03:52:55.850 --> 03:52:59.960
[INAUDIBLE] deputy assistant--

03:52:59.960 --> 03:53:01.050
whatever it was you're--

03:53:01.050 --> 03:53:02.800
SPEAKER: And you were
still doing the same

03:53:02.800 --> 03:53:05.021
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:53:05.021 --> 03:53:07.270
BATES: He also had a funny
one about a program manager

03:53:07.270 --> 03:53:08.490
as a technical director.

03:53:08.490 --> 03:53:10.170
I remember [INAUDIBLE]
went in there,

03:53:10.170 --> 03:53:12.650
and he had a
problem with Porter.

03:53:12.650 --> 03:53:14.670
And [INAUDIBLE]
says to him, Doc,

03:53:14.670 --> 03:53:16.470
I want to be the
program manager.

03:53:16.470 --> 03:53:18.750
And he says, you don't want
to be a program manager,

03:53:18.750 --> 03:53:19.950
they don't do anything.

03:53:19.950 --> 03:53:21.400
All they do is shuffle papers.

03:53:21.400 --> 03:53:23.040
You want to be the
technical director,

03:53:23.040 --> 03:53:24.630
he's the guy that
runs everything.

03:53:24.630 --> 03:53:28.050
He says, not with Porter, Porter
runs everything [INAUDIBLE]

03:53:28.050 --> 03:53:31.230
So Doc says, no, you've got
to be the technical director.

03:53:31.230 --> 03:53:32.836
So [INAUDIBLE] was that.

03:53:32.836 --> 03:53:34.830
DUGGAN: It was an
interesting organization.

03:53:34.830 --> 03:53:36.274
BATES: Oh yeah.

03:53:36.274 --> 03:53:38.190
POUNDSTONE: Well, what
else can we do for you?

03:53:38.190 --> 03:53:39.780
BROWN: Well, I think I only
have one more question.

03:53:39.780 --> 03:53:41.670
And that really
picks up the question

03:53:41.670 --> 03:53:43.260
we started the session with--

03:53:43.260 --> 03:53:44.800
who else should
we be talking to?

03:53:44.800 --> 03:53:47.342
I mean, there have been a lot
of names that have come up.

03:53:47.342 --> 03:53:49.800
I'm just wondering if you can
think of anybody else that we

03:53:49.800 --> 03:53:51.260
ought to be speaking
to that we haven't--

03:53:51.260 --> 03:53:52.509
that obviously hasn't come up.

03:53:52.509 --> 03:53:56.250
BATES: I think you ought to
come up with a set of topics

03:53:56.250 --> 03:53:58.350
that you're interested in.

03:53:58.350 --> 03:54:00.482
And then send it around
to everybody here.

03:54:00.482 --> 03:54:02.190
HALL: And then we can
fill in the blanks.

03:54:02.190 --> 03:54:05.400
BATES: Fill in the blanks
of who you should talk to.

03:54:05.400 --> 03:54:09.090
HALL: I did mention to
Dave that the key issue

03:54:09.090 --> 03:54:10.640
is the reliability aspect.

03:54:10.640 --> 03:54:11.190
BROWN: Sure.

03:54:11.190 --> 03:54:13.000
HALL: That should
be another meeting--

03:54:13.000 --> 03:54:18.170
pulling in people who
contributed to the [INAUDIBLE]..

03:54:18.170 --> 03:54:21.495
POUNDSTONE: Well, I believe
that Raytheon and Draper

03:54:21.495 --> 03:54:24.680
Lab did a lot of pioneering in
reliability in that program.

03:54:24.680 --> 03:54:26.840
HALL: Yes, we did.

03:54:26.840 --> 03:54:28.850
POUNDSTONE: I know
what I did from my end.

03:54:28.850 --> 03:54:30.970
In a lot of ways, we
competed with each other,

03:54:30.970 --> 03:54:32.780
which was healthy.

03:54:32.780 --> 03:54:35.622
But we started out
with a policy of,

03:54:35.622 --> 03:54:38.210
if you're going to analyze
every stinking failure down

03:54:38.210 --> 03:54:40.960
to the nitty gritty, you're
going to understand it.

03:54:40.960 --> 03:54:43.560
And we started building
up a failure analysis lab.

03:54:43.560 --> 03:54:46.730
I bought the first sim
out there near Sudbury.

03:54:46.730 --> 03:54:50.400
And all sorts of fancy
materials, equipment,

03:54:50.400 --> 03:54:52.820
and developed a very good
materials laboratory,

03:54:52.820 --> 03:54:54.222
that never existed before.

03:54:54.222 --> 03:54:55.680
And Draper started
doing the thing.

03:54:55.680 --> 03:54:57.230
DUGGAN: Yeah, it forced us
to get into self-defense.

03:54:57.230 --> 03:54:58.271
POUNDSTONE: That's right.

03:54:58.271 --> 03:54:59.985
They had to.

03:54:59.985 --> 03:55:02.360
HALL: Plus you had to have a
lot of those for the Apollo,

03:55:02.360 --> 03:55:04.846
because the flight
processing specs required

03:55:04.846 --> 03:55:05.720
a lot of [INAUDIBLE].

03:55:05.720 --> 03:55:07.220
BATES: Well, those
were the guys who

03:55:07.220 --> 03:55:10.250
were hired by North American,
to do an analysis of some

03:55:10.250 --> 03:55:11.605
of the parts.

03:55:11.605 --> 03:55:13.730
We did an awful lot
of outside analysis

03:55:13.730 --> 03:55:16.450
for people, because that
facility was established.

03:55:16.450 --> 03:55:17.960
POUNDSTONE: But that was a--

03:55:17.960 --> 03:55:21.350
I assume the other
people in other programs

03:55:21.350 --> 03:55:23.240
were probably doing
similar things.

03:55:23.240 --> 03:55:25.940
But I think we were probably
a year two ahead of everybody

03:55:25.940 --> 03:55:27.930
there, in terms of the depth.

03:55:28.545 --> 03:55:31.030
HANLEY: Not the same
that I've ever seen.

03:55:31.030 --> 03:55:33.230
Because what we used
to do is we used

03:55:33.230 --> 03:55:37.550
to take all the
failures out of a lot.

03:55:37.550 --> 03:55:39.870
And then we used
to analyze them.

03:55:39.870 --> 03:55:43.890
And then we used to put a
weighting factor on them.

03:55:43.890 --> 03:55:46.590
And with the
weighting factor, you

03:55:46.590 --> 03:55:50.450
could just reject the whole lot.

03:55:50.450 --> 03:55:51.760
I don't think I've ever seen--

03:55:51.760 --> 03:55:53.850
POUNDSTONE: But I think there
was a general understanding--

03:55:53.850 --> 03:55:55.266
whether it was in
the spec or not,

03:55:55.266 --> 03:55:58.260
I'll be damned if I know-- that
anytime there was a failure,

03:55:58.260 --> 03:56:00.580
you just got to find out why.

03:56:00.580 --> 03:56:01.640
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:56:01.640 --> 03:56:03.890
HANLEY: Oh, yeah, the failure
analysis had to be done.

03:56:03.890 --> 03:56:09.870
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:56:09.870 --> 03:56:12.770
DUGGAN: Later on there were
a lot of burn in experience,

03:56:12.770 --> 03:56:15.040
and other burn in components.

03:56:15.040 --> 03:56:16.780
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:56:21.240 --> 03:56:23.520
HANLEY: Yeah, that was
another, where we were planning

03:56:23.520 --> 03:56:24.021
[INAUDIBLE].

03:56:24.021 --> 03:56:25.061
POUNDSTONE: That's funny.

03:56:25.061 --> 03:56:26.730
You know, all that
burn in that we did,

03:56:26.730 --> 03:56:33.450
and so forth, years later I
went up to where he came from.

03:56:33.450 --> 03:56:35.340
It was Delco in those days.

03:56:35.340 --> 03:56:41.880
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:56:41.880 --> 03:56:45.150
POUNDSTONE: And they were
building 18,000 computers a day

03:56:45.150 --> 03:56:46.890
up there.

03:56:46.890 --> 03:56:48.177
And they had--

03:56:48.177 --> 03:56:49.260
HALL: One for automobiles.

03:56:49.260 --> 03:56:50.426
POUNDSTONE: For automobiles.

03:56:50.426 --> 03:56:53.200
And they had the damnedest
burn in rack you ever saw.

03:56:53.200 --> 03:56:54.450
And a burn in--

03:56:54.450 --> 03:56:58.935
it was just Apollo multiplied
by about a million.

03:56:58.935 --> 03:57:00.540
But I think a lot
of the principles

03:57:00.540 --> 03:57:02.514
came out of what we were doing.

03:57:02.514 --> 03:57:04.680
FRASIER: Initially, they
did I think 18 days burn in

03:57:04.680 --> 03:57:06.920
on those computers.

03:57:06.920 --> 03:57:09.280
HANLEY: Remember when
we had leaky packages?

03:57:09.280 --> 03:57:10.470
SPEAKER: Yeah.

03:57:10.470 --> 03:57:11.550
[INAUDIBLE]

03:57:11.550 --> 03:57:13.840
HANLEY: I think that's
what Ed is referring to.

03:57:13.840 --> 03:57:16.670
And what [INAUDIBLE]
and I did is

03:57:16.670 --> 03:57:21.090
we got some devices that
we knew were real bad.

03:57:21.090 --> 03:57:24.420
And we shoved them through the
line, to see who would come,

03:57:24.420 --> 03:57:26.190
and who would be picked up.

03:57:26.190 --> 03:57:28.770
And they didn't get picked up.

03:57:28.770 --> 03:57:32.640
Then after that, we tried all
kinds of different systems.

03:57:32.640 --> 03:57:35.790
And finally, there was
the weighing method.

03:57:35.790 --> 03:57:36.550
Remember that?

03:57:36.550 --> 03:57:38.050
POUNDSTONE: Yeah,
the weight, right.

03:57:38.050 --> 03:57:40.380
HANLEY: You had all
these marble platformed

03:57:40.380 --> 03:57:43.006
with these balances on them.

03:57:43.870 --> 03:57:46.490
POUNDSTONE: I think Aaron came
up with that one, I bet you.

03:57:46.490 --> 03:57:47.912
SPEAKER: [INAUDIBLE]

03:57:47.912 --> 03:57:49.620
POUNDSTONE: Yeah, that
was the technique.

03:57:49.620 --> 03:57:53.734
We pressurized them and weighed
them to find leaky packages.

03:57:53.734 --> 03:57:54.650
Yeah, I remember that.

03:57:54.650 --> 03:57:56.880
HANLEY: When I first
went into the room

03:57:56.880 --> 03:57:58.920
and I saw all those
scales, I said,

03:57:58.920 --> 03:58:01.590
my God, what have we done?

03:58:01.590 --> 03:58:03.940
[LAUGHS]

03:58:03.940 --> 03:58:06.210
POUNDSTONE: But
as I said, I think

03:58:06.210 --> 03:58:09.690
a lot of the reliability
techniques and practices that

03:58:09.690 --> 03:58:12.249
were developed on that program
spun off to other industries

03:58:12.249 --> 03:58:13.040
around the country.

03:58:13.540 --> 03:58:16.660
HALL: [INAUDIBLE] spun
off to the military spec--

03:58:16.660 --> 03:58:20.130
1833, or whenever it was.

03:58:20.130 --> 03:58:22.050
A lot of the pictures
that was developed

03:58:22.050 --> 03:58:24.220
and the [? container ?]
analysis was done on Apollo

03:58:24.220 --> 03:58:26.195
is directly into
that military spec.

03:58:26.195 --> 03:58:27.244
SPEAKER: That's true.

03:58:27.244 --> 03:58:28.910
HANLEY: Yeah, I know
what you're saying.

03:58:28.910 --> 03:58:31.320
They copied them
verbatim [INAUDIBLE]..

03:58:31.320 --> 03:58:33.030
FRASIER: The thing I
wish I had still is

03:58:33.030 --> 03:58:36.390
the color slide of
the one flat pack

03:58:36.390 --> 03:58:41.950
that had purple plague, that
got found in a computer.

03:58:41.950 --> 03:58:44.400
That I had to then stand up
before the flight readiness

03:58:44.400 --> 03:58:47.160
review board in Huntsville,
or at the Cape--

03:58:47.160 --> 03:58:49.210
I've forgotten where.

03:58:49.210 --> 03:58:50.760
Yeah, it must have
been at the Cape.

03:58:50.760 --> 03:58:52.150
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:58:52.150 --> 03:58:56.010
FRASIER: And explain that this
was a failure, that we had not

03:58:56.010 --> 03:58:57.180
been able to correct.

03:58:57.180 --> 03:58:59.790
And it was the only one
we'd ever seen like this.

03:58:59.790 --> 03:59:02.850
HANLEY: Well, that was because
of the leakage, I'm sure.

03:59:02.850 --> 03:59:05.200
POUNDSTONE: There probably
aren't any more in there.

03:59:05.200 --> 03:59:06.486
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:59:07.300 --> 03:59:09.800
HANLEY: Don't you remember when
we shut down the whole line?

03:59:12.410 --> 03:59:15.740
Hugh Brady was yelling
and screaming at us,

03:59:15.740 --> 03:59:20.550
and getting us fired,
and everything else?

03:59:20.550 --> 03:59:21.250
I can remember.

03:59:21.250 --> 03:59:21.780
[LAUGHS]

03:59:21.780 --> 03:59:24.337
FRASIER: That's the same
flight readiness review that--

03:59:24.337 --> 03:59:26.670
remember the [? wiggly ?]
[? leaded ?] transistor memory

03:59:26.670 --> 03:59:26.820
drivers?

03:59:26.820 --> 03:59:27.620
HANLEY: Oh yeah.

03:59:27.620 --> 03:59:29.996
FRASIER: I [INAUDIBLE]
we had those two,

03:59:29.996 --> 03:59:31.120
and those are in the thing.

03:59:31.120 --> 03:59:32.800
And I thought it was OK to go.

03:59:32.800 --> 03:59:34.520
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

03:59:40.420 --> 03:59:42.660
BLAIR-SMITH: On the
architecture side,

03:59:42.660 --> 03:59:47.280
I wonder whether it's still kind
of extraordinary that we had

03:59:47.280 --> 03:59:49.830
what you might
block 0-- the AGC3--

03:59:49.830 --> 03:59:51.330
block 1, and block 2.

03:59:51.330 --> 03:59:54.570
And nobody worried about
backward compatibility.

03:59:54.570 --> 04:00:00.390
That was extremely beneficial,
because everybody was learning

04:00:00.390 --> 04:00:02.229
everything about the project.

04:00:02.229 --> 04:00:04.770
And it was absolutely correct
to throw away all that old shit

04:00:04.770 --> 04:00:07.140
and start over.

04:00:07.140 --> 04:00:08.970
POUNDSTONE: You only
got away with that one

04:00:08.970 --> 04:00:10.006
because you didn't have a spec.

